// The revl ADMISSION GATE, in revl — stage four of the self-hosting effort
// (roadmap item 145, Path C). Where lexer.rvl / parser.rvl / checker.rvl port
// the compiler *frontend*, this file ports the *lowering-time admission gate*:
// it lowers a checked program to enough of the cordis IR that the admission
// guarantees can be decided, then enforces them. It emits NO code — code
// generation is deferred to the py service (Path A). This is exactly the gate
// `evolve` needs to admit a candidate natively on any tier: lexer -> parser ->
// checker -> lower, with lower answering one question — admit, or refuse and
// name the guarantee.
//
// It is the second half of a differential oracle: this file and
// src/revl/lower.py's `check_and_lower` / `_link` are two independent
// implementations of the same admission algebra, forced to agree on every
// input (tests/test_selfhost_lower.py). Neither is the spec; a disagreement is
// a real defect in one of them.
//
// ============================================================ WHAT IT COVERS
// The first coherent slice mirrors three guarantees, in the reference's own
// checking order (per-component G4/A1 during lowering, then G2 at link):
//
//   * G4 (capability containment) — a service declaration is the upper bound
//     on its providers' effects. A plain-declared provision method whose body
//     reaches an emission (a required-service `emission` op, or an `emission`
//     extern, directly or through a named fn-call chain) is refused; an
//     `emission[caps]`-scoped method that emits through a boundary outside its
//     declared set is refused; an unmarked call to a required-service emission
//     op is refused. Ported from lower.py's `_method_emissions` + provider
//     checks and the `_emitting_capabilities` fixed point. (This is the same
//     algebra checker.rvl's slice two already cross-checks; it is re-affirmed
//     here at the IR level because that is where lower.py enforces it.)
//
//   * A1 (sync-reaching-async refusal, item 117) — an `async` extern is an
//     emission whose host op may suspend the caller. A sync-declared provide
//     method that reaches an async callable (an async extern, or a fn colored
//     async by the transitive `_async_callables` fixed point) — or an async
//     *service operation* through a required key — has no in-flight window to
//     await it, so it is refused. A method whose service op is declared
//     `async fn` admits the same body.
//
//   * G2 (provision disjointness) — across the composed components, a key has
//     at most one provider per realm. Two components providing the same key in
//     the shared realm is a conflict. Ported from lower.py's `_link`.
//
// Slice two (this file) adds three more, in the reference's order (G1 during
// lowering, then G2/G3 at link):
//
//   * G1 (declared access) — a component body reads only names it can resolve:
//     a required key, a local/parameter, a callable (module fn/extern or a
//     host/stdlib constructor), or `config`. A `root.<m>(...)` or `f(...)` head
//     that resolves to none of these is an undeclared access, refused with the
//     reference's `` `<x>` is not a declared requirement of <Comp> `` (from
//     `_lower_postfix`'s head resolution).
//
//   * G3 (acyclic dependencies) — a dependency cycle can never activate. Over
//     provider -> consumer edges (a consumer's required key resolved in the
//     realm its provider provides it), lower.py's colored DFS reports the first
//     back edge as `dependency cycle: A -> B -> A (G3)`; a component that
//     requires a key it provides itself is the degenerate one-node case.
//
//   * per-realm G2 — provision disjointness scoped to (key, realm): the same
//     key in two DIFFERENT realms composes (multi-tenancy), while two providers
//     in the SAME realm conflict, the realm named only when it isn't shared.
//     `isolate <k> in realm("<r>")` supplies the realm dimension.
//
// Slice three (this file) completes the check-parity for the non-spawn program
// surface, in the reference's order:
//
//   * G1 bare-value name resolution — a bare `Var` used as a VALUE (not only a
//     call/access head) inside a provide-method body must resolve to a scope
//     name (local/param, including an arrow parameter or match-arm binding) or
//     a callable; anything else is the same undeclared-access refusal. In a
//     setup/activation body the reference reports a different, out-of-slice
//     diagnostic, so the bare-value refusal is gated to provide bodies.
//     Ported from `_lower_component_pure_expr`'s `ExprVar` `_plain_body` branch.
//
//   * A1 first-class async value (`passed_async`) — an async extern or colored
//     fn referenced as a VALUE in a provide method has a colorless arrow type,
//     so a suspension cannot be awaited through it; refused in ANY method, sync
//     or async. A first-class EMISSION value is G4 evidence the same way
//     (labelled "<name> (passed as a function value)", reaching the unnameable
//     `*` boundary plus its own capability), so a plain provider that hands an
//     emitting callable off is refused at G4 exactly as the reference is.
//
//   * A1 setup/activation async-reach — a setup body that reaches an async
//     callable (by call or as a value) cannot suspend a fiber; ported from
//     `_async_reached_outside_provide`. (An async service OP in setup is
//     deliberately NOT refused by the reference, so it stays admitted here.)
//
//   * the realm PRELUDE rule — realm/metadata declarations (`isolate` /
//     `intercept`) derive the resolution context and must precede every effect,
//     emit, await, and provide statement; one placed after an action is refused
//     with the reference's exact wording (`_lower_component`'s `action_seen`).
//     `config` is a component-level declaration, not an action, so it never
//     shifts that boundary.
//
// Slice four (this file) hardens the statement reader (item 168) and mirrors the
// remaining tractable non-spawn admission surface:
//
//   * item 168 — the statement reader now ends a run at a STRUCTURAL boundary,
//     not only a newline (`p_stmt_run` reports where it ended; callers advance
//     by THAT). A single-line component body — `{ let s = effect … undo …
//     isolate d … provide s {…} }` — now parses into the same statement
//     sequence the reference sees, closing an oracle gap the multi-line-only
//     fuzzers never covered. The cross-check fuzzers now each run a single-line
//     variant too.
//
//   * `intercept` metadata VALIDATION (lower.py's `InterceptStmt` branch): the
//     target must name a REQUIRED key (an undeclared key is the G1
//     head-resolution refusal — this is why fixture `v2_intercept_undeclared`
//     classifies G1; a PROVIDED key has its own provision-specific wording), and
//     a key may be intercepted at most once. Surfaced (with the prelude ORDER
//     rule) through `CompD.refuse`, already tagged.
//
//   * A1 `await` outside a component body (`_lower_provide`'s AwaitStmt check):
//     an `await` in a SYNC provide method has no in-flight window; an `async`
//     method (or a component activation body) may await.
//
//   * services-2.0 signature parity (lower.py's `method.async_ != decl.async_`):
//     an `async fn` service op must be implemented by an `async fn` method, and
//     vice-versa — the `async` marker is part of the interface.
//
// Slice 5a (this file) closed the A1 arrow-color LEAK trio and the module-fn
// first-class async-value refusal, on the `Async[T]` param-slot coercion model
// (`_coerce_async_args`): a sync-typed arrow reaching a suspension leaks, while
// the same arrow landing in an `Async[T]` slot is coerced and admits.
//
// Slice 5b (this file) mirrors the spawn/instance dimension core
// (lower.py's `_check_spawn_*`, docs/design-v2-instances.md). A `spawn <Comp>`
// is read at the token level (parser.rvl has no spawn production) and the
// component it instantiates becomes a runtime TEMPLATE:
//
//   * templates are EXCLUDED from the static G2/G3 composition (decision 5/6),
//     so two components providing one key both compose when both are spawned.
//
//   * G4/G6 spawn-emission bounds (`_check_spawn_emission_bounds`, decision 8):
//     a spawn inside a PROVIDE method whose op is plain — or `emission[caps]` —
//     may not spawn a target whose (closed) emission surface exceeds that bound.
//
//   * capability attenuation (`_check_spawn_attenuation`, item 66): a spawn in an
//     ACTIVATION body may only grant a child capabilities its spawner HOLDS (its
//     `requires` keys plus its own emit surface) — narrowing is sound, widening
//     is not.
//
//   * an unmarked emission reached through a spawn HANDLE (`h.<key>.<op>(…)`,
//     `_instance_get_call`) is the same G4 refusal as an unmarked required-
//     service emission — enforced inline during the body walk.
// All four carry `code="G4"` (the emission-capability machinery), so they
// cross-check on tag AND message exactly like the other G4 surfaces.
//
// Slice 6 (this file, the FINAL Path C slice) closes the async-coloring rule-2
// gap and mirrors the tractable code-less refusals — the ones the reference
// raises with NO `e.code`, so the cross-check matches them on message text:
//
//   * async coloring rule 2 (item 92 §3 / `_async_callables`'s `async_params`):
//     a fn that CALLS one of its own parameters whose declared type is an async
//     function type is itself colored. `async_colored` now folds
//     `callees ∩ asyncParams` into the fixed point, so a sync method reaching
//     such a fn is A1-refused, naming both the colored fn and the callables it
//     reaches — closing approximation (a).
//
//   * the bind-to-a-handle rule (decision 2) and the unknown-spawn-target check
//     (`_lower_spawn`): an unbound `effect spawn`, or a `spawn` naming a
//     component not in the composition. Code-less; tagged "SPAWN".
//
//   * `handoff` admission (item 53): the prelude ordering rule (reuses the
//     PRELUDE wording), a target that is not a PROVIDED key, and the one-per-
//     component uniqueness rule. Target/uniqueness are code-less; tagged
//     "HANDOFF".
//
//   * `isolate` target validation (`IsolateStmt` branch): the single-realm pin
//     form must name a key from the header (a requirement or a provision), and
//     a key is pinned at most once. Code-less; tagged "G1" (the declared-wiring
//     family, as `intercept` is).
//
// Item 186 (bounded pieces) then closed the last two SINGLE-SOURCE residues:
//
//   * multi-realm routing VALIDATION (item 162, lower.py's `RouteStmt` branch +
//     its `_link` route loop): `isolate <key> in realms(...) [strategy(...)]` was
//     parsed-but-unvalidated. Its prelude placement (PRELUDE), its target rules
//     (a provision routed / an undeclared key), one-binding-per-key (pinned
//     already / routed twice), the closed strategy set, and the link-time
//     per-realm provider check are all mirrored. Code-less; the four
//     routing-specific ones are tagged "ROUTE", the undeclared target IS the
//     shared G1 diagnostic, and a routed key's G3 edges are drawn per realm.
//
//   * the async-coloring APPROXIMATION: callee collection and leak-reach now
//     STOP at a nested COERCED arrow (`stop_async_arrows`) instead of descending
//     into it. `abody_callees` feeds the coloring fixed point while `FnD.callees`
//     (unpruned) still feeds the emission one, exactly as the reference runs the
//     same walk with and without the flag. The inputs that reach it — an
//     async-typed but UNCALLED parameter, so rule 2 does not mask it — are now
//     in the corpus and in a five-switch fuzzer.
//
// ============================================================ WHAT REMAINS
// Path C is COMPLETE for the SINGLE-SOURCE admission surface — every guarantee
// `admit_src(src)` can decide from one program text is mirrored and cross-checked
// (G1-G4, A1, PRELUDE, the spawn dimension, handoff, isolate, routing). The
// residue below is either NOT a single-source verdict (so it has no `admit_src`
// cross-check) or a shape the gate's verdict vocabulary cannot express:
//   * The type/arg-checking spine (checker.rvl already cross-checks typing).
//   * Refusal ORDER against the reference's collect-all sink (item 386): the
//     reference orders its collected diagnostics by LINE, so an earlier-line
//     link (G2) refusal outranks a later-line component refusal, while this gate
//     reports the first refusal it reaches. The two agree on every program with
//     ONE refusal; only a multi-defect program can separate them, and closing it
//     needs line numbers on every gate refusal plus a collecting sink.
//   * The routing form's PARSE-level rules (an empty `realms()`, a dynamic label,
//     a repeated label) stay unvalidated: the reference raises those from its
//     parser, a family outside this gate's verdict vocabulary. The route clause
//     is left unvalidated rather than guessed at when it is not the well-formed
//     static-literal shape.
//   * Spawn `with { … }` config-field validation and nested (non-statement)
//     spawn discovery — the gate skips the config block structurally.
//   * AMBIENT-input admission (no single-source cross-check via `admit_src`):
//     service replacement, `handoff` state replacement against a running
//     manifest, ambient (running-manifest) composition — `check_and_lower`'s
//     `ambient` parameter has no `admit_src` analogue.
//   * `ir_version` computation, fault/lifecycle/prop test declarations, and every
//     emitter-oriented lowering detail — the gate produces a verdict, not an IR
//     document. The native `revl_compile` reachable on top of this gate pairs it
//     with the py service's code generation (Path A) for exactly those.
//
// Verdict shape: `admit_src` returns "" to admit, else a two-part string
// "<G>|<message>" where <G> is the guarantee tag ("G1" | "G2" | "G3" | "G4" |
// "A1" | "PRELUDE" | "SPAWN" | "HANDOFF" | "ROUTE") and <message> is the refusal
// spelled exactly as the reference spells it. The cross-check compares both the
// tag and the message; for the code-less refusals ("SPAWN"/"HANDOFF"/"ROUTE" and
// the code-less "G1"/"PRELUDE" families) the message text is the ground truth.

use "./lexer.rvl" { Token, lex_src }
use "./parser.rvl" { Expr, ParamN, InitN, ArmN, PartN, CallN, FieldN,
                    is_bad, expr_at, params_at, type_at, nesting_limit }

// ================================================================ IR model
//
// The slice of the cordis IR the admission checks read. A program lowers to:
// externs and fns (the call graph the two fixed points run over), services
// (the upper-bound declarations G4/A1 check against), and components (the
// providers whose bodies are gated, plus the provision keys G2 links).

// An extern OR a plain fn. An extern carries its flags and no body-calls; a
// plain fn carries the callee NAMES its body reaches (all the two fixed points
// need) and no flags. Unifying them lets one fixed point walk both.
//
// `asyncSlots` are the parameter INDICES whose declared type is an async
// function type `(…) -> Async[T]` (item 92 / lower.py's `_is_async_fn_type`).
// An arrow ARGUMENT landing in such a slot is the sync->async coercion
// (lower.py's `_coerce_async_args`): it is stamped async and is legitimately
// awaited, so the arrow-color LEAK check must NOT refuse it. `body` is a plain
// fn's statement body — the module-fn twin refusals
// (`_refuse_leaky_pure_arrow` and the first-class async-value refusal) walk it;
// an extern has none, so its body is [].
//
// `asyncParams` are the parameter NAMES whose declared type is an async function
// type — rule 2 of the async coloring (item 92 §3 / `_async_callables`'s
// `async_params`): a fn that CALLS such a parameter is itself colored (a
// declaration-shaped seed), so `async_colored` unions `callees ∩ asyncParams`
// into the fixed point exactly as the reference does.
type FnD = { name: Str, isEmExtern: Bool, isAsyncExtern: Bool,
             callees: List[Str], asyncSlots: List[Int], asyncParams: List[Str],
             body: List[Stmt], cachePure: Bool }

// A service method signature: enough to be an upper bound. `caps` is the
// `emission[a, b]` scope list ([] when the method is plain or uncapped-
// emission); `isEm`/`isAsync` are the declared emission/async properties.
type MSig = { name: Str, isEm: Bool, caps: List[Str], isAsync: Bool }
type SvcD = { name: Str, methods: List[MSig] }

// A body statement. kind: emit | effect | undo | return | expr | skip. The
// `emit` kind (statement-level `emit ...`, or `let x = emit ...`) marks the
// expression's calls as crossing the boundary intentionally. `bind` is the
// name a `let x = ...` introduces ("" when the statement binds nothing) — the
// G1 name-resolution scope reads it (a component local is visible to every
// provide method, exactly as the reference's `env.locals` is).
// `spawnTarget` (non-"") marks a statement as a `spawn <Comp> with { … }`
// acquisition (`let h = effect spawn Comp …`): the component it instantiates.
// parser.rvl's expression grammar has no spawn form, so the statement reader
// recognises `spawn` at the token level (below) and records the target here,
// while `bind` carries the handle name. Every non-spawn statement leaves it "".
type Stmt = { kind: Str, e: Expr, bind: Str, spawnTarget: Str }

// A provide method: its parameters feed the G1 scope (a param is a declared
// name in the method body), and its body is walked for G4/A1/G1. `isAsync` is
// the method's own `async fn` modifier — checked for signature parity against
// the service op it implements (lower.py's `method.async_ != decl.async_`).
type ProvM   = { name: Str, params: List[Str], body: List[Stmt], isAsync: Bool }
type Provide = { key: Str, svcName: Str, methods: List[ProvM] }
// A declared provision key and the realm it lives in ("" = the shared realm).
type ProvKey = { key: Str, rlm: Str }
// `iso` carries the component's `isolate <key> in realm("<label>")` bindings:
// key -> realm. It scopes both a provided key (its provision's realm, for G2)
// and a required key (the realm it resolves in, for G3 edges), exactly as
// lower.py's `_realm(entry, key)` reads one isolate map for both roles.
// A multi-realm bind (item 162, lower.py's `RouteStmt` branch):
// `isolate <key> in realms("a","b") [strategy(<name>)]` distributes a REQUIRED
// key across N backend realms. `rlms` is the realm list in declaration order (a
// router's rotation is defined over it) and `strat` the strategy name ("" when
// omitted — the router's default).
type Route   = { key: Str, rlms: List[Str], strat: Str }
type CompD   = { name: Str, provKeys: List[ProvKey], provs: List[Provide],
                 reqMap: List[Bind], setup: List[Stmt], iso: List[ProvKey],
                 routes: List[Route], refuse: Str }

type Bind = { name: Str, ty: Str }

type Prog = { fns: List[FnD], svcs: List[SvcD], comps: List[CompD], bad: Str }

// ================================================================ tokens
//
// A small structural reader over the frontend's token stream, in the shape
// checker.rvl established: declarations are brace-structural, statements are
// line-based (the lexer's tokens carry line numbers).

// The token at `i`, or a synthetic eof when there is none — the total read
// every scan in this file leans on so it can look ahead without first proving
// the stream is long enough.
//
// BOTH bounds, not just the upper one. This guard used to test `i <
// ts.length()` alone, which reads as total but is not: a scan that backs up
// past the start (a `hi - 1` on an empty span, an `i - 1` at the head of a
// declaration) arrives with a negative `i`, and `i < length` is happily true
// for it. What happened next was tier-dependent, which is the worst shape a
// defect can have in a language whose whole claim is that one program means
// one thing everywhere:
//
//   python   ts[-1]  -> the LAST token, silently, and the scan carries on
//                       reading a stream that has nothing to do with where it
//                       is. A wrong answer, no diagnostic.
//   rust     ts[(-1) as usize] -> index out of bounds: len is 11 but the index
//                       is 18446744073709551615. `crates/revl-gate` is emitted
//                       rust, so this is the shipped gate panicking.
//   go/java  index out of range / IndexOutOfBoundsException.
//
// Testing `i >= 0` costs one comparison and makes the total read total. Found
// by tools/fuzz_frontend.py; the reproducer is `fn t])->t[`.
fn tkc(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Token {
  if (i >= 0 && i < ts.length()) { return ts[i] }
  return { kind: "eof", text: "", line: 0 }
}

fn atk(ts: List[Token], i: Int, k: Str) -> Bool { return tkc(ts, i).kind == k }

fn atw(ts: List[Token], i: Int, w: Str) -> Bool {
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  return t.kind == "kw" && t.text == w
}

// item 350: `boot` is a CONTEXTUAL keyword, recognised only immediately before
// `component`. It marks the composition's ENVIRONMENT CONTRACT arrival point —
// an admission-time config concern, not a composition-guarantee one — so this
// gate carries no verdict for it and every top-level walk simply steps over the
// token and proceeds at the `component` it heads. Without this the gate would
// refuse a valid program with BAD|unexpected token at top level, a divergence
// from the reference frontend.
fn at_boot(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  return t.kind == "ident" && t.text == "boot" && atw(ts, i + 1, "component")
}

// The names of every `boot component` declared at top level, in declaration
// order — the reference's `_link` boot check reads the same order off its
// composition entries. Brace-structural and ITERATIVE (a per-token recursion
// over a whole file would be a stack risk); an extern's verbatim `@py { ... }`
// body is one `hostbody` token, so its braces cannot perturb the depth.
fn boot_names(ts: List[Token]) -> List[Str] {
  var i = 0
  var depth = 0
  var out: List[Str] = []
  while (i < ts.length() && !atk(ts, i, "eof")) {
    if (atk(ts, i, "{")) { depth += 1 }
    if (atk(ts, i, "}")) { depth -= 1 }
    if (depth == 0 && at_boot(ts, i)) { out = out.push(tkc(ts, i + 2).text) }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// Whether a token starts a TOP-LEVEL declaration — where a `type` declaration's
// token span ends. Kept next to `type_ctors`, its only reader.
fn at_top_decl(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  return atw(ts, i, "type") || atw(ts, i, "fn") || atw(ts, i, "extern")
      || atw(ts, i, "service") || atw(ts, i, "component") || atw(ts, i, "use")
      || atw(ts, i, "test") || at_boot(ts, i)
}

// Every Upper-cased name a `type` declaration mentions — the ADT CONSTRUCTORS a
// bare call head may legitimately be (`type Step = Final(Str) | NeedTool(T)`
// makes `Final(...)` and `NeedTool(...)` callable heads in a component body).
//
// `p_top` steps over `type` declarations structurally: they are a checker-stage
// concern and admission reads nothing else out of them. This scan is the one
// thing admission does need from them, so it runs beside the parse rather than
// inside it, the way `boot_names` does.
//
// A case sits in CONSTRUCTOR POSITION: an Upper-cased ident right after the
// declaration's `=` or one of its `|` separators, at nesting depth 0. That
// keeps a record's field types out (`type Row = { id: Int }` declares no `Int`
// constructor) while covering every arm of `type Step = Final(Str) |
// NeedTool(ToolCall)`, multi-line arms included — the span runs to the next
// top-level declaration, not to the end of the line.
//
// It still OVER-approximates in one direction: a union of TYPES rather than
// cases (`type Payload = List[Row] | Str`) contributes its arms too. That is
// the safe side — over-collecting only ever keeps admitting a program the
// reference admits, while under-collecting would refuse one, and refusing what
// the reference admits is the direction this gate may not invent. Iterative for
// the same reason `boot_names` is.
fn type_ctors(ts: List[Token]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < ts.length() && !atk(ts, i, "eof")) {
    if (atw(ts, i, "type")) {
      var j = i + 1
      var depth = 0
      var ctor = false
      while (j < ts.length() && !atk(ts, j, "eof")
             && (depth > 0 || !at_top_decl(ts, j))) {
        let t = tkc(ts, j)
        if (t.kind == "{" || t.kind == "[" || t.kind == "(") { depth += 1 }
        if (t.kind == "}" || t.kind == "]" || t.kind == ")") { depth -= 1 }
        if (depth == 0 && (t.kind == "=" || t.kind == "|")) {
          ctor = true
        } else {
          if (ctor && t.kind == "ident" && is_upper_name(t.text)) {
            out = union_into(out, [t.text])
          }
          ctor = false
        }
        j += 1
      }
      i = j
    } else {
      i += 1
    }
  }
  return out
}

fn join_comma(xs: List[Str], i: Int, acc: Str) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return join_comma(xs, i + 1, acc == "" ? xs[i] : acc.concat(", ").concat(xs[i]))
}

fn skip_line(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Int {
  if (i >= ts.length()) { return i }
  let ln = tkc(ts, i).line
  var j = i
  while (j < ts.length() && tkc(ts, j).line == ln) { j += 1 }
  return j
}

fn line_end(ts: List[Token], i: Int, hi: Int) -> Int {
  let ln = tkc(ts, i).line
  var j = i
  while (j < hi && tkc(ts, j).line == ln) { j += 1 }
  return j
}

// Index just past the `}` matching the `{` at i; -1 when unbalanced.
fn close_brace(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Int {
  var depth = 0
  var j = i
  while (j < ts.length()) {
    if (atk(ts, j, "{")) { depth += 1 }
    else {
      if (atk(ts, j, "}")) {
        depth -= 1
        if (depth == 0) { return j + 1 }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  return 0 - 1
}

// ================================================================ list utils

fn contains(xs: List[Str], s: Str) -> Bool { return xs.indexOf(s) != 0 - 1 }

fn contains_int(xs: List[Int], v: Int) -> Bool {
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (xs[i] == v) { return true }
    i += 1
  }
  return false
}

// Prefix test over the string primitives the frontend exposes (no `startsWith`).
fn starts_with(s: Str, pre: Str) -> Bool {
  if (pre.length() > s.length()) { return false }
  return s.slice(0, pre.length()) == pre
}

// Read the return after the outer parameter list, not an arrow nested inside
// a callback parameter. Shared by async coloring and typed call lowering.
fn function_return_ty(ty: Str) -> Str {
  if (ty.length() == 0) { return "" }
  if (ty.charAt(0) != "(") { return "" }
  var depth = 0
  var i = 0
  var close = 0 - 1
  while (i < ty.length() && close == 0 - 1) {
    let c = ty.charAt(i)
    if (c == "(") { depth += 1 }
    if (c == ")") {
      depth -= 1
      if (depth == 0) { close = i }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  if (close == 0 - 1) { return "" }
  let rest = ty.slice(close + 1, ty.length())
  if (!starts_with(rest, " -> ")) { return "" }
  return rest.slice(4, rest.length())
}

fn is_async_fn_ty(ty: Str) -> Bool {
  return starts_with(function_return_ty(ty), "Async[")
}

// The parameter INDICES whose declared type is an async function type — the
// `Async[T]` slots a call-site coercion (lower.py's `_coerce_async_args`) stamps
// its arrow argument async into.
fn async_slots_of(ps: List[ParamN]) -> List[Int] {
  var out: List[Int] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < ps.length()) {
    if (is_async_fn_ty(ps[i].ty)) { out = out.push(i) }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// The parameter NAMES whose declared type is an async function type — rule 2's
// seed (`_async_callables`'s `async_params`): a fn calling one of these is
// colored.
fn async_params_of(ps: List[ParamN]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < ps.length()) {
    if (is_async_fn_ty(ps[i].ty)) { out = out.push(ps[i].name) }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn union_into(xs: List[Str], ys: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out = xs
  var i = 0
  while (i < ys.length()) {
    if (!contains(out, ys[i])) { out = out.push(ys[i]) }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn subset_of(a: List[Str], b: List[Str]) -> Bool {
  var i = 0
  while (i < a.length()) {
    if (!contains(b, a[i])) { return false }
    i += 1
  }
  return true
}

fn minus_list(xs: List[Str], ys: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (!contains(ys, xs[i])) { out = out.push(xs[i]) }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// Insertion sort ascending; diagnostics sort their extras alphabetically,
// exactly as lower.py's `sorted(...)` does.
fn sort_strs(xs: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    let v = xs[i]
    var k = 0
    var placed = false
    var res: List[Str] = []
    while (k < out.length()) {
      if (!placed && out[k] > v) {
        res = res.push(v)
        placed = true
      }
      res = res.push(out[k])
      k += 1
    }
    if (!placed) { res = res.push(v) }
    out = res
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn quote_join(xs: List[Str]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (i > 0) { out = out.concat(", ") }
    out = out.concat("`").concat(xs[i]).concat("`")
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn comma_join(xs: List[Str]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (i > 0) { out = out.concat(", ") }
    out = out.concat(xs[i])
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// ================================================================ prog build

fn bad_prog(pg: Prog, msg: Str) -> Prog {
  if (pg.bad != "") { return pg }
  return { fns: pg.fns, svcs: pg.svcs, comps: pg.comps, bad: msg }
}

fn push_fn(pg: Prog, f: FnD) -> Prog {
  return { fns: pg.fns.push(f), svcs: pg.svcs, comps: pg.comps, bad: pg.bad }
}

fn push_svc(pg: Prog, s: SvcD) -> Prog {
  return { fns: pg.fns, svcs: pg.svcs.push(s), comps: pg.comps, bad: pg.bad }
}

fn push_comp(pg: Prog, c: CompD) -> Prog {
  return { fns: pg.fns, svcs: pg.svcs, comps: pg.comps.push(c), bad: pg.bad }
}

type PStep = { pg: Prog, i: Int }

fn mk_step(p: Prog, i: Int) -> PStep { return { pg: p, i: i } }

// Value-taking record constructors: a `var`'s fields cannot appear directly in
// a record literal (a `var` never escapes its function, syntax-2.0 §3.5), so
// every construction from mutable state goes through one of these, exactly as
// checker.rvl does.
fn mk_fnd(nm: Str, em: Bool, asy: Bool, cs: List[Str], slots: List[Int],
          aps: List[Str], bd: List[Stmt]) -> FnD {
  return { name: nm, isEmExtern: em, isAsyncExtern: asy, callees: cs,
           asyncSlots: slots, asyncParams: aps, body: bd, cachePure: false }
}

fn mk_msig(nm: Str, em: Bool, cs: List[Str], asy: Bool) -> MSig {
  return { name: nm, isEm: em, caps: cs, isAsync: asy }
}

fn mk_compd(nm: Str, pks: List[ProvKey], pvs: List[Provide],
            rq: List[Bind], su: List[Stmt], iso: List[ProvKey],
            rts: List[Route], refuse: Str) -> CompD {
  return { name: nm, provKeys: pks, provs: pvs, reqMap: rq, setup: su, iso: iso,
           routes: rts, refuse: refuse }
}

fn mk_provkey(k: Str, r: Str) -> ProvKey { return { key: k, rlm: r } }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- statements
//
// One line's worth of tokens as statement(s), the reach-only subset of
// checker.rvl's p_stmt_run: kinds are collapsed to whether they mark an
// emission (`emit`) and the expression carried. `effect X undo Y` on one line
// yields two statements.

fn mkstmt(kind: Str, e: Expr) -> Stmt {
  return { kind: kind, e: e, bind: "", spawnTarget: "" }
}

fn mkstmtb(kind: Str, e: Expr, bind: Str) -> Stmt {
  return { kind: kind, e: e, bind: bind, spawnTarget: "" }
}

// A `spawn <target>` acquisition bound to handle `bn`. It is an `effect`
// statement (an acquisition, an action for the prelude rule); its expression is
// an inert placeholder — the spawn's admission is decided from `spawnTarget`,
// not by walking an expression parser.rvl cannot produce.
fn mkstmt_spawn(bn: Str, target: Str) -> Stmt {
  return { kind: "effect", e: int_lit0(), bind: bn, spawnTarget: target }
}

// An UNBOUND `effect spawn <target>` (no `let` handle). A spawn's inverse is the
// instance's own teardown, which needs a handle to name, so a bare spawn is
// refused (lower.py decision 2 — the bind-to-a-handle rule). kind
// "spawn_unbound" keeps it out of the template/handle tables (it never
// composes); the post-parse `spawn_form_verdict` reports it.
fn mkstmt_spawn_unbound(target: Str) -> Stmt {
  return { kind: "spawn_unbound", e: int_lit0(), bind: "", spawnTarget: target }
}

fn int_lit0() -> Expr { return IntLit("0") }

// `[await] <expr>` in an operand position. `await` is a keyword, so the
// expression grammar stops dead on it: `let h = effect await fetch()` parsed as
// a bad expression, the statement became a `skip`, and the reader jumped to the
// end of the line — taking the operand, and every head in it, with it. Since a
// suspension is exactly where a component reaches outside itself, that is the
// last operand a gate should decline to read.
//
// `aw` is reported rather than folded away because `has_await` decides the A1
// await-placement surface off a statement of kind "await"; `await_marker`
// appends that statement so the placement rule sees the suspension it always
// saw, while the operand itself is now walked like any other.
type AwOperand = { e: Expr, i: Int, aw: Bool }

fn operand_at(ts: List[Token], k: Int) -> AwOperand {
  if (atw(ts, k, "await")) {
    let r = expr_at(ts, k + 1)
    return { e: r.e, i: r.i, aw: true }
  }
  let r2 = expr_at(ts, k)
  return { e: r2.e, i: r2.i, aw: false }
}

fn await_marker(aw: Bool, ss: List[Stmt]) -> List[Stmt] {
  if (aw) { return ss.push(mkstmt("await", int_lit0())) }
  return ss
}

// One statement run and the index just PAST the tokens it consumed. Item 168:
// the reader must end a run at a STRUCTURAL boundary, not only a newline, so a
// single-line body (`let x = a() let y = b() isolate d …`) parses into the same
// statement sequence the reference sees. The expression grammar already stops
// at a keyword head (`let`/`return`/`emit`/`effect`/`isolate`/`provide`/…), so
// reporting where the run actually ended — and having callers advance by THAT,
// not by `line_end` — is the whole fix: a caller re-dispatches at the next
// statement/declaration even when it shares the source line. `i` falls back to
// `hi` (skip to the next line) only on a bad/degenerate parse, to guarantee
// forward progress.
type SRun = { ss: List[Stmt], i: Int }

fn mk_srun(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int) -> SRun { return { ss: ss, i: i } }

fn p_stmt_run(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int) -> SRun {
  let t = tkc(ts, lo)
  if (t.kind == "kw" && (t.text == "let" || t.text == "var")) {
    var eq = lo + 2
    if (atk(ts, eq, ":")) { eq = type_at(ts, eq + 1).i }
    if (!atk(ts, eq, "=")) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", int_lit0())], hi) }
    let bn = tkc(ts, lo + 1).text
    var j = eq + 1
    var kd = "expr"
    if (atw(ts, j, "emit")) {
      kd = "emit"
      j += 1
    } else {
      if (atw(ts, j, "effect")) {
        kd = "effect"
        j += 1
      }
    }
    // `let h = effect spawn <Comp> with { … } [undo …]` — an instance
    // acquisition (docs/design-v2-instances.md). parser.rvl has no spawn
    // expression, so the target is read at the token level here and carried on
    // the statement; the optional `with { … }` config block is skipped
    // structurally (config-field validation is an out-of-slice, code-less
    // refusal). The handle `bn` binds a local exactly as any `let` does.
    if (kd == "effect" && atw(ts, j, "spawn")) {
      let tgt = tkc(ts, j + 1).text
      var sk = j + 2
      if (atw(ts, sk, "with") && atk(ts, sk + 1, "{")) {
        let ce = close_brace(ts, sk + 1)
        if (ce != 0 - 1) { sk = ce }
      }
      let sp = mkstmt_spawn(bn, tgt)
      if (atw(ts, sk, "undo")) {
        let su = expr_at(ts, sk + 1)
        if (!is_bad(su.e)) { return mk_srun([sp, mkstmt("undo", su.e)], su.i) }
        return mk_srun([sp], hi)
      }
      return mk_srun([sp], sk)
    }
    let r = operand_at(ts, j)
    if (is_bad(r.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", r.e)], hi) }
    let out: List[Stmt] = await_marker(r.aw, [mkstmtb(kd, r.e, bn)])
    if (atw(ts, r.i, "undo")) {
      let u = operand_at(ts, r.i + 1)
      if (!is_bad(u.e)) {
        return mk_srun(await_marker(u.aw, out.push(mkstmt("undo", u.e))), u.i)
      }
      return mk_srun(out, hi)
    }
    return mk_srun(out, r.i)
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "return") {
    let rr = expr_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(rr.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", rr.e)], hi) }
    return mk_srun([mkstmt("return", rr.e)], rr.i)
  }
  // `emit <expr> [compensate <expr>]`. The compensation is the emit's inverse
  // and is read as its own statement, exactly as `undo` is read after
  // `effect` — it is an ordinary expression in an ordinary position and its
  // head resolves like any other. It carries the emit's MARKING (see
  // `walk_stmts`): reversing an emission is itself an emission.
  //
  // Before this the `emit` branch stopped at its own expression and left the
  // reader pointing at `compensate`, which matched no branch, parsed as a bad
  // expression, and skipped to the end of the line.
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "emit") {
    let re1 = operand_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(re1.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", re1.e)], hi) }
    let oute: List[Stmt] = await_marker(re1.aw, [mkstmt("emit", re1.e)])
    if (atw(ts, re1.i, "compensate")) {
      let uc = operand_at(ts, re1.i + 1)
      if (!is_bad(uc.e)) {
        return mk_srun(await_marker(uc.aw, oute.push(mkstmt("compensate", uc.e))), uc.i)
      }
      return mk_srun(oute, hi)
    }
    return mk_srun(oute, re1.i)
  }
  // `await <expr>` is a statement of its own (the A1 await-outside-body surface
  // reads its kind). The expression is still walked for G4/A1/G1.
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "await") {
    let ra = expr_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(ra.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", ra.e)], hi) }
    return mk_srun([mkstmt("await", ra.e)], ra.i)
  }
  // a bare `effect spawn <Comp> …` with no `let` handle — parser.rvl has no
  // spawn production, so `spawn` is read at the token level; unbound, it is the
  // bind-to-a-handle refusal (decision 2). Skip the target/`with`/`undo` tail
  // structurally so the reader makes forward progress.
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "effect" && atw(ts, lo + 1, "spawn")) {
    let tgt = tkc(ts, lo + 2).text
    var sk = lo + 3
    if (atw(ts, sk, "with") && atk(ts, sk + 1, "{")) {
      let ce = close_brace(ts, sk + 1)
      if (ce != 0 - 1) { sk = ce }
    }
    if (atw(ts, sk, "undo")) {
      let su = expr_at(ts, sk + 1)
      if (!is_bad(su.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt_spawn_unbound(tgt)], su.i) }
      return mk_srun([mkstmt_spawn_unbound(tgt)], hi)
    }
    return mk_srun([mkstmt_spawn_unbound(tgt)], sk)
  }
  // `every <n><unit> { … }` / `after <n><unit> { … }` — a timer block. Its body
  // is an ordinary statement sequence and the reference resolves the heads in
  // it exactly as it resolves the statements around it, so the block's
  // statements are READ OUT and returned inline: to this walk a timer body is
  // simply more setup.
  //
  // Without this the reader had no case for the keyword at all, fell through
  // to `expr_at`, got a Bad parse, and skipped to the end of the line — taking
  // the whole block with it. A statement form the reader cannot read is a
  // statement form the gate cannot check, and unreadable is not the same
  // answer as clean. `close_brace` bounds the body; a malformed header or an
  // unclosed brace still falls through to the old skip, since there is nothing
  // to read out.
  if (t.kind == "kw" && (t.text == "every" || t.text == "after")) {
    var b = lo + 1
    while (b < hi && !atk(ts, b, "{")) { b += 1 }
    if (b < hi) {
      let bend = close_brace(ts, b)
      if (bend != 0 - 1) {
        return mk_srun(p_stmts(ts, b + 1, bend - 1, []), bend)
      }
    }
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && (t.text == "effect" || t.text == "undo"
                        || t.text == "compensate")) {
    let rf = operand_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(rf.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", rf.e)], hi) }
    let out2: List[Stmt] = await_marker(rf.aw, [mkstmt(t.text, rf.e)])
    if (atw(ts, rf.i, "undo")) {
      let u2 = operand_at(ts, rf.i + 1)
      if (!is_bad(u2.e)) {
        return mk_srun(await_marker(u2.aw, out2.push(mkstmt("undo", u2.e))), u2.i)
      }
      return mk_srun(out2, hi)
    }
    return mk_srun(out2, rf.i)
  }
  let r2 = expr_at(ts, lo)
  if (is_bad(r2.e)) { return mk_srun([mkstmt("skip", r2.e)], hi) }
  return mk_srun([mkstmt("expr", r2.e)], r2.i)
}

fn append_stmts(a: List[Stmt], bs: List[Stmt]) -> List[Stmt] {
  var out = a
  var i = 0
  while (i < bs.length()) {
    out = out.push(bs[i])
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn p_stmts(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, acc: List[Stmt]) -> List[Stmt] {
  if (lo >= hi || atk(ts, lo, "}")) { return acc }
  let le = line_end(ts, lo, hi)
  let r = p_stmt_run(ts, lo, le)
  let nx = r.i > lo ? r.i : le
  return p_stmts(ts, nx, hi, append_stmts(acc, r.ss))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- fn callees
//
// A plain fn contributes to the two fixed points only through the callee NAMES
// its body reaches (`_calls_in`, name-call subset). We collect them at parse
// time so a FnD needs no stored body.

fn callee_name(tg: Expr) -> Str { return match tg { Var(n) => n, _ => "" } }

fn exprs_calls(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return exprs_calls(xs, i + 1, calls_in(xs[i], acc))
}

fn inits_calls(xs: List[InitN], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return inits_calls(xs, i + 1, calls_in(xs[i].value, acc))
}

fn arms_calls(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return arms_calls(xs, i + 1, calls_in(xs[i].body, acc))
}

fn parts_calls(xs: List[PartN], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return parts_calls(xs, i + 1, calls_in(xs[i].e, acc))
}

fn call_calls(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  let cn = callee_name(tg)
  var a = calls_in(tg, acc)
  if (cn != "") { a = a.push(cn) }
  return exprs_calls(args, 0, a)
}

fn calls_in(e: Expr, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  return match e {
    IntLit(_)   => acc,
    FloatLit(_) => acc,
    StrLit(_)   => acc,
    BoolLit(_) => acc,
    NullLit    => acc,
    Var(_)     => acc,
    Hole(_)    => acc,
    Bad(_)     => acc,
    Bin(b)     => calls_in(b.r, calls_in(b.l, acc)),
    Un(u)      => calls_in(u.e, acc),
    Emit(u)    => calls_in(u.e, acc),
    Call(c)    => call_calls(c.target, c.args, acc),
    Field(f)   => calls_in(f.target, acc),
    OptField(f) => calls_in(f.target, acc),
    OptCall(c) => exprs_calls(c.args, 0, calls_in(c.target, acc)),
    Index(x)   => calls_in(x.idx, calls_in(x.target, acc)),
    If(x)      => calls_in(x.els, calls_in(x.then_, calls_in(x.cond, acc))),
    Rec(r)     => inits_calls(r.fields, 0, acc),
    Lst(l)     => exprs_calls(l.items, 0, acc),
    Arrow(a)   => calls_in(a.body, acc),
    Match(m)   => arms_calls(m.arms, 0, calls_in(m.scrut, acc)),
    Templ(t)   => parts_calls(t.parts, 0, acc),
  }
}

fn body_callees(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return acc }
  return body_callees(ss, i + 1, calls_in(ss[i].e, acc))
}

// ------------------------------------------------ callees, async-arrow-pruned
//
// `_calls_in(..., stop_async_arrows=True)` — the SECOND callee collection the
// reference runs over the same bodies. An arrow that lands in an `Async[T]`
// parameter slot is stamped async (`_coerce_async_args` on the component path,
// `_resolve_arrow` on the pure path), and an async-colored arrow is a VALUE
// whose suspension belongs to whoever awaits it, not to the body that
// constructs it (item 92 §3, "the arrow owns its color"). So the async fixed
// point (`_async_callables`) STOPS at such an arrow, while the emission fixed
// point passes no flag and keeps bubbling.
//
// That is why `FnD.callees` (unpruned) still feeds `emit_caps` and this pruned
// collection feeds `async_colored`: one walk cannot serve both. `slots` is the
// callable -> `Async[T]` parameter-index table (`Ctx.fnAsyncSlots`), so the
// pruning is decided exactly where the reference's stamp is placed — at the
// argument position of a call to a fn with an async-typed parameter.
//
// Closing this was roadmap item 186's residual coloring approximation: without
// it a fn whose ONLY async reach sits inside a coerced arrow is over-colored,
// and every sync method calling it is falsely A1-refused.

fn acalls_slots(slots: Map[Str, List[Int]], n: Str) -> List[Int] {
  return match slots.lookup(n) { Some(v) => v, None => [] }
}

// Is this expression an arrow literal? (The payload is BOUND rather than
// wildcarded: `Arrow(_)` lowers to a rust arm reading the discarded binding,
// which cargo refuses — `is_bad` in parser.rvl binds for the same reason.)
fn is_arrow(e: Expr) -> Bool {
  return match e { Arrow(a) => true, _ => false }
}

fn aexprs_calls(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
                acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return aexprs_calls(xs, i + 1, sl, acalls_in(xs[i], sl, acc))
}

fn ainits_calls(xs: List[InitN], i: Int, sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
                acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return ainits_calls(xs, i + 1, sl, acalls_in(xs[i].value, sl, acc))
}

fn aarms_calls(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int, sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
               acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return aarms_calls(xs, i + 1, sl, acalls_in(xs[i].body, sl, acc))
}

fn aparts_calls(xs: List[PartN], i: Int, sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
                acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return aparts_calls(xs, i + 1, sl, acalls_in(xs[i].e, sl, acc))
}

// The argument list of a call to `n`: an ARROW at one of `n`'s `Async[T]` slots
// is the coerced (async-stamped) value — pruned. Every other argument, and a
// non-arrow in an async slot (the reference refuses it separately), is walked.
fn aargs_calls(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, asl: List[Int], sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
               acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  if (contains_int(asl, i) && is_arrow(xs[i])) {
    return aargs_calls(xs, i + 1, asl, sl, acc)
  }
  return aargs_calls(xs, i + 1, asl, sl, acalls_in(xs[i], sl, acc))
}

fn acall_calls(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
               acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  let cn = callee_name(tg)
  if (cn != "") {
    return aargs_calls(args, 0, acalls_slots(sl, cn), sl, acc.push(cn))
  }
  return aexprs_calls(args, 0, sl, acalls_in(tg, sl, acc))
}

fn acalls_in(e: Expr, sl: Map[Str, List[Int]], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  return match e {
    IntLit(_)   => acc,
    FloatLit(_) => acc,
    StrLit(_)   => acc,
    BoolLit(_)  => acc,
    NullLit     => acc,
    Var(_)      => acc,
    Hole(_)     => acc,
    Bad(_)      => acc,
    Bin(b)      => acalls_in(b.r, sl, acalls_in(b.l, sl, acc)),
    Un(u)       => acalls_in(u.e, sl, acc),
    Emit(u)     => acalls_in(u.e, sl, acc),
    Call(c)     => acall_calls(c.target, c.args, sl, acc),
    Field(f)    => acalls_in(f.target, sl, acc),
    OptField(f) => acalls_in(f.target, sl, acc),
    OptCall(c)  => aexprs_calls(c.args, 0, sl, acalls_in(c.target, sl, acc)),
    Index(x)    => acalls_in(x.idx, sl, acalls_in(x.target, sl, acc)),
    If(x)       => acalls_in(x.els, sl, acalls_in(x.then_, sl, acalls_in(x.cond, sl, acc))),
    Rec(r)      => ainits_calls(r.fields, 0, sl, acc),
    Lst(l)      => aexprs_calls(l.items, 0, sl, acc),
    Arrow(a)    => acalls_in(a.body, sl, acc),
    Match(m)    => aarms_calls(m.arms, 0, sl, acalls_in(m.scrut, sl, acc)),
    Templ(t)    => aparts_calls(t.parts, 0, sl, acc),
  }
}

fn abody_callees(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, sl: Map[Str, List[Int]],
                 acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return acc }
  return abody_callees(ss, i + 1, sl, acalls_in(ss[i].e, sl, acc))
}

// ================================================================ parsing
//
// The declaration layer. Modelled on checker.rvl's parser, extended to capture
// the `async` modifier (externs and service methods) that admission needs and
// checker's slice does not, and the component's provision keys/realm for G2.

fn ret_at(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Int {
  if (atk(ts, i, "arrow")) { return type_at(ts, i + 1).i }
  return i
}

// `extern [emission|acquire|pure] [async] fn name(params) -> T [= @host {..}]*`
// Flags are consumed in any order until `fn`, so `extern emission async fn` and
// `extern async emission fn` both parse (docs spell the former).
fn p_extern(ts: List[Token], i: Int, pg: Prog) -> PStep {
  var j = i + 1
  var isEm = false
  var isAsync = false
  while (atw(ts, j, "emission") || atw(ts, j, "acquire") || atw(ts, j, "pure")
         || atw(ts, j, "async")) {
    if (atw(ts, j, "emission")) { isEm = true }
    if (atw(ts, j, "async")) { isAsync = true }
    j += 1
  }
  if (!atw(ts, j, "fn")) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "expected fn after extern"), i: skip_line(ts, j) }
  }
  let nm = tkc(ts, j + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, j + 3)
  var k = ret_at(ts, ps.i)
  // skip any `= @host { ... }` bodies without letting a brace end the decl early
  while (k < ts.length() && !atw(ts, k, "service") && !atw(ts, k, "component")
         && !atw(ts, k, "extern") && !atw(ts, k, "fn") && !atk(ts, k, "eof")) {
    if (atk(ts, k, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, k)
      if (e == 0 - 1) { k = ts.length() } else { k = e }
    } else { k += 1 }
  }
  return mk_step(push_fn(pg, mk_fnd(nm, isEm, isAsync, [],
                                    async_slots_of(ps.ps),
                                    async_params_of(ps.ps), [])), k)
}

fn p_fn(ts: List[Token], i: Int, pg: Prog) -> PStep {
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, i + 3)
  var rti = ret_at(ts, ps.i)
  let cachePure = ati(ts, rti, "cache") && atw(ts, rti + 1, "pure")
  if (cachePure) { rti += 2 }
  if (!atk(ts, rti, "{")) {
    return mk_step(bad_prog(pg, "expected { after fn signature"), rti)
  }
  let bend = close_brace(ts, rti)
  if (bend == 0 - 1) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "unbalanced braces in fn body"), i: ts.length() }
  }
  let body = p_stmts(ts, rti + 1, bend - 1, [])
  return mk_step(push_fn(pg, { name: nm, isEmExtern: false,
                              isAsyncExtern: false,
                              callees: body_callees(body, 0, []),
                              asyncSlots: async_slots_of(ps.ps),
                              asyncParams: async_params_of(ps.ps),
                              body: body, cachePure: cachePure }), bend)
}

type MethsR = { xs: List[MSig], i: Int, ok: Bool }

fn mk_meths(xs: List[MSig], i: Int, ok: Bool) -> MethsR {
  return { xs: xs, i: i, ok: ok }
}

fn p_methods(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int, acc: List[MSig]) -> MethsR {
  if (i >= end || atk(ts, i, "}")) { return { xs: acc, i: i, ok: true } }
  var j = i
  var em = false
  var isAsync = false
  var isIdempotent = false
  var caps: List[Str] = []
  // Service flags may appear in any order before `fn`.
  while (atw(ts, j, "emission") || atw(ts, j, "async") || atw(ts, j, "idempotent")) {
    if (atw(ts, j, "emission")) {
      em = true
      j += 1
      if (atk(ts, j, "[")) {
        var k = j + 1
        while (k < end && !atk(ts, k, "]")) {
          if (atk(ts, k, "ident")) { caps = caps.push(tkc(ts, k).text) }
          k += 1
        }
        j = k + 1
      }
    } else if (atw(ts, j, "idempotent")) {
      isIdempotent = true
      j += 1
    } else {
      isAsync = true
      j += 1
    }
  }
  if (isIdempotent && !em) { return mk_meths(acc, j, false) }
  if (!atw(ts, j, "fn")) { return mk_meths(acc, j, false) }
  let nm = tkc(ts, j + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, j + 3)
  let rti = ret_at(ts, ps.i)
  return p_methods(ts, rti, end, acc.push(mk_msig(nm, em, caps, isAsync)))
}

fn p_service(ts: List[Token], i: Int, pg: Prog) -> PStep {
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  if (!atk(ts, i + 2, "{")) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "expected { after service name"), i: i + 2 }
  }
  let end = close_brace(ts, i + 2)
  if (end == 0 - 1) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "unbalanced braces in service"), i: ts.length() }
  }
  let ms = p_methods(ts, i + 3, end - 1, [])
  if (!ms.ok) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "bad method signature in service ".concat(nm)),
             i: end }
  }
  return { pg: push_svc(pg, { name: nm, methods: ms.xs }), i: end }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- provide

type ProvMR = { xs: List[ProvM], i: Int, ok: Bool }

// The parameter NAMES of a signature — the declared names a method body may
// read (they seed the G1 scope, just as `env.params` does in the reference).
fn param_names(ps: List[ParamN]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < ps.length()) {
    out = out.push(ps[i].name)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// A provide method body is either a brace block `fn m(p) { ... }` or the arrow
// shorthand `fn m(p) = expr` (one expression, unmarked). The shorthand is what
// the A1 fixtures use, so it must parse.
fn p_prov_methods(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int,
                  acc: List[ProvM]) -> ProvMR {
  if (i >= end || atk(ts, i, "}")) { return { xs: acc, i: i, ok: true } }
  // a provide method may be declared `async fn` (matching an async service op).
  // The modifier is captured for the signature-parity check (lower.py's
  // `method.async_ != decl.async_`); the body walk uses the service op's color.
  let masync = atw(ts, i, "async")
  let h = masync ? i + 1 : i
  if (!atw(ts, h, "fn")) { return { xs: acc, i: h, ok: false } }
  let nm = tkc(ts, h + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, h + 3)
  let pnames = param_names(ps.ps)
  if (atk(ts, ps.i, "=")) {
    let le = line_end(ts, ps.i + 1, end)
    let r = p_stmt_run(ts, ps.i + 1, le)
    let nx = r.i > ps.i + 1 ? r.i : le
    return p_prov_methods(ts, nx, end,
                          acc.push({ name: nm, params: pnames, body: r.ss,
                                     isAsync: masync }))
  }
  if (!atk(ts, ps.i, "{")) { return { xs: acc, i: ps.i, ok: false } }
  let bend = close_brace(ts, ps.i)
  if (bend == 0 - 1) { return { xs: acc, i: ts.length(), ok: false } }
  let bbody = p_stmts(ts, ps.i + 1, bend - 1, [])
  return p_prov_methods(ts, bend, end,
                        acc.push({ name: nm, params: pnames, body: bbody,
                                   isAsync: masync }))
}

// The names bound by a list of `key: Service` pairs (a header row).
fn bind_names(env: List[Bind], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= env.length()) { return acc }
  return bind_names(env, i + 1, acc.push(env[i].name))
}

fn bind_lookup(env: List[Bind], n: Str, i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= env.length()) { return "" }
  if (env[i].name == n) { return env[i].ty }
  return bind_lookup(env, n, i + 1)
}

// Whether a header row declares `n` (a requirement type is never "", so the
// lookup's empty result is an unambiguous "absent").
fn bind_has(env: List[Bind], n: Str) -> Bool { return bind_lookup(env, n, 0) != "" }

// `refuse` (non-"") is a semantic admission refusal spelled fully as the
// reference spells it, ALREADY TAGGED ("<TAG>|<message>") and surfaced verbatim
// by `admit_src`. Two families flow through it, both decided as the component
// body is scanned (before its provide methods are gated): the realm PRELUDE
// rule (an `isolate`/`intercept` placed after an action) and `intercept`
// metadata validation (a G1 target/uniqueness refusal). It is distinct from
// `ok: false`, which is a structural parse failure ("BAD").
type ProvR = { provs: List[Provide], setup: List[Stmt], iso: List[ProvKey],
               routes: List[Route], refuse: Str, i: Int, ok: Bool }

fn mk_provr(pv: List[Provide], su: List[Stmt], iso: List[ProvKey],
            rts: List[Route], refuse: Str, i: Int, ok: Bool) -> ProvR {
  return { provs: pv, setup: su, iso: iso, routes: rts, refuse: refuse,
           i: i, ok: ok }
}

// The prelude rule (lower.py `_lower_component`): realm/metadata declarations
// (`isolate`/`intercept`) derive the resolution context and must precede every
// effect, emit, await, and provide statement. `action` becomes true at the
// first non-prelude statement (a setup statement OR a `provide` block); an
// `isolate`/`intercept` seen thereafter is refused with the reference's exact
// wording. The refusal is carried in `refuse` up to `admit_src`.
fn prelude_msg(kw: Str) -> Str {
  return "`".concat(kw)
    .concat("` must precede every effect, emit, await, and provide statement")
}

// `intercept` metadata validation (lower.py's `InterceptStmt` branch): the
// target must name a REQUIRED key. Interception is the component-declared
// metadata d(k) whose domain is the dependency set — a provision has nothing
// to intercept, and an undeclared key resolves to nothing. Both surface the
// reference's exact wording; both are the declared-wiring (G1) family, so the
// undeclared case is the very `` `<x>` is not a declared requirement of <C> ``
// G1 diagnostic (this is why fixture `v2_intercept_undeclared` classifies G1).
fn intercept_target_msg(key: Str, cname: Str, isProv: Bool) -> Str {
  if (isProv) {
    return "`intercept` applies to required keys only — `".concat(key)
      .concat("` is a provision")
  }
  return "`".concat(key).concat("` is not a declared requirement of ").concat(cname)
}

fn intercept_twice_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "key `".concat(key).concat("` is intercepted twice in ").concat(cname)
}

// `isolate` target validation (lower.py's `IsolateStmt` branch): the key must be
// declared by the component header (a requirement OR a provision), and a key is
// isolated at most once. Both are code-less refusals in the reference; the gate
// tags them G1 (the declared-wiring family, as `intercept` does) so the oracle
// classifies them alongside the other G1 head-resolution refusals.
fn isolate_target_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "`".concat(key)
    .concat("` is not a declared requirement or provision of ").concat(cname)
}

fn isolate_twice_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "key `".concat(key).concat("` is isolated twice in ").concat(cname)
}

fn iso_has(iso: List[ProvKey], key: Str, i: Int) -> Bool {
  if (i >= iso.length()) { return false }
  if (iso[i].key == key) { return true }
  return iso_has(iso, key, i + 1)
}

// ---------------------------------------------- multi-realm routing (item 162)
//
// `isolate <key> in realms("a","b") [strategy(<name>)]` — lower.py's `RouteStmt`
// branch. The routing FORM parses in the reference's frontend; these are its
// component-level admission rules, each spelled exactly as the reference spells
// it. All are code-less refusals: the prelude one reuses the PRELUDE wording
// (with the routing form's own spelling of the keyword), the undeclared-key one
// IS the shared G1 `is not a declared requirement of` diagnostic, and the four
// routing-specific ones are tagged "ROUTE" so the oracle compares tag AND text.
//
// Routing distributes a CONSUMER's dependency, so it targets a required key: a
// provision has one installed instance in one realm (G2), which is why a routed
// provision, and a key both pinned and routed, are refusals rather than widenings.

fn route_prelude_msg() -> Str {
  return "`isolate ... in realms(...)` must precede every effect, emit, await, and provide statement"
}

fn route_provision_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "`isolate ... in realms(...)` routes a *required* key — `".concat(key)
    .concat("` is a provision of ").concat(cname)
}

fn route_undeclared_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "`".concat(key).concat("` is not a declared requirement of ").concat(cname)
}

fn route_pinned_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "key `".concat(key)
    .concat("` is already isolated to a single realm in ").concat(cname)
    .concat(" — it cannot also be routed across `realms(...)`")
}

fn route_twice_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "key `".concat(key).concat("` is routed twice in ").concat(cname)
}

// The closed strategy set (lower.py's `KNOWN_STRATEGIES`): validated at compile
// time so a typo is a refusal, not a silent runtime fallback. The diagnostic
// lists the known names SORTED, exactly as `", ".join(sorted(...))` renders them.
fn known_strategies() -> List[Str] {
  return ["least_loaded", "random", "round_robin", "sticky"]
}

fn route_strategy_msg(strat: Str, key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "unknown routing strategy `".concat(strat).concat("` for `")
    .concat(key).concat("` in ").concat(cname)
}

fn routes_has(rs: List[Route], key: Str, i: Int) -> Bool {
  if (i >= rs.length()) { return false }
  if (rs[i].key == key) { return true }
  return routes_has(rs, key, i + 1)
}

fn route_of(rs: List[Route], key: Str, i: Int) -> Route {
  if (i >= rs.length()) { return { key: "", rlms: [], strat: "" } }
  if (rs[i].key == key) { return rs[i] }
  return route_of(rs, key, i + 1)
}

// The token-level reader for the routing clause, entered at the `realms` ident.
// `ok` is false when the clause is not the well-formed static-literal shape the
// reference's `realms_route` accepts — every such input is a reference PARSE
// error (an empty list, a dynamic label, a repeated label), a family outside
// this gate's verdict vocabulary, so the clause is left unvalidated exactly as
// it was before this slice rather than guessed at.
type RouteP = { rlms: List[Str], strat: Str, i: Int, ok: Bool }

fn ati(ts: List[Token], i: Int, w: Str) -> Bool {
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  return t.kind == "ident" && t.text == w
}

fn p_realm_labels(ts: List[Token], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> RouteP {
  if (!atk(ts, i, "string")) { return { rlms: acc, strat: "", i: i, ok: false } }
  let label = tkc(ts, i).text
  if (label == "" || contains(acc, label)) {
    return { rlms: acc, strat: "", i: i, ok: false }
  }
  let xs = acc.push(label)
  if (atk(ts, i + 1, ",")) {
    if (atk(ts, i + 2, ")")) { return { rlms: xs, strat: "", i: i + 3, ok: true } }
    return p_realm_labels(ts, i + 2, xs)
  }
  if (atk(ts, i + 1, ")")) { return { rlms: xs, strat: "", i: i + 2, ok: true } }
  return { rlms: xs, strat: "", i: i + 1, ok: false }
}

fn p_route_clause(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> RouteP {
  if (!ati(ts, i, "realms") || !atk(ts, i + 1, "(")) {
    return { rlms: [], strat: "", i: i, ok: false }
  }
  let r = p_realm_labels(ts, i + 2, [])
  if (!r.ok) { return r }
  // `strategy(<ident>)` is optional; `strategy` is an ordinary identifier that
  // heads a clause only in this position.
  if (ati(ts, r.i, "strategy") && atk(ts, r.i + 1, "(")
      && atk(ts, r.i + 2, "ident") && atk(ts, r.i + 3, ")")) {
    return { rlms: r.rlms, strat: tkc(ts, r.i + 2).text, i: r.i + 4, ok: true }
  }
  return r
}

// `handoff <key>: <Type>` (item 53, lower.py's `HandoffStmt` branch): the
// verified state hand-off of a stateful provider. A prelude declaration (it
// names the provider's live state before any effect creates it) that targets a
// key this component PROVIDES, at most once. The prelude case reuses the
// PRELUDE wording; the target and uniqueness cases are code-less refusals the
// gate tags "HANDOFF" and the oracle classifies by message.
fn handoff_target_msg(key: Str, cname: Str) -> Str {
  return "`".concat(key).concat("` is not a declared provision of ").concat(cname)
}

fn handoff_twice_msg(cname: Str) -> Str {
  return cname.concat(" declares more than one `handoff` — a component has one ")
    .concat("activation frame, so it hands off one state shape")
}

fn p_comp_body(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int, provh: List[Bind],
               reqh: List[Bind], cname: Str, provs: List[Provide],
               setup: List[Stmt], iso: List[ProvKey], rts: List[Route],
               icept: List[Str], hoff: Bool, action: Bool) -> ProvR {
  if (i >= end || atk(ts, i, "}")) {
    return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts, "", i, true)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "provide")) {
    let key = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    if (!atk(ts, i + 2, "{")) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts, "", i + 2, false)
    }
    let pend = close_brace(ts, i + 2)
    if (pend == 0 - 1) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts, "", ts.length(), false)
    }
    let ms = p_prov_methods(ts, i + 3, pend - 1, [])
    if (!ms.ok) { return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts, "", pend, false) }
    // a `provide` block is an action: it counts against later prelude decls.
    return p_comp_body(ts, pend, end, provh, reqh, cname,
                       provs.push({ key: key,
                                    svcName: bind_lookup(provh, key, 0),
                                    methods: ms.xs }),
                       setup, iso, rts, icept, hoff, true)
  }
  // `isolate <key> in realm("<label>")` — the realm dimension G2/G3 read, the
  // prelude ordering rule (after an action it is refused), and the isolate
  // TARGET validation: the key must be declared by the header, isolated at most
  // once. `isolate <key> in realms(...)` is the multi-realm ROUTING form (item
  // 162), validated by `p_route_stmt` — it carries its own prelude wording, so
  // the form is recognised BEFORE the ordering rule fires.
  if (atw(ts, i, "isolate")) {
    if (atw(ts, i + 2, "in") && ati(ts, i + 3, "realms")) {
      return p_route_stmt(ts, i, end, provh, reqh, cname, provs, setup, iso,
                          rts, icept, hoff, action)
    }
    if (action) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
                      tagged("PRELUDE", prelude_msg("isolate")),
                      skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    let key = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    // kw `in`, kw `realm`, `(`, string label, `)` — the single-realm pin form.
    if (atw(ts, i + 2, "in") && atw(ts, i + 3, "realm")
        && atk(ts, i + 4, "(") && atk(ts, i + 5, "string")) {
      if (!bind_has(reqh, key) && !bind_has(provh, key)) {
        return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
          tagged("G1", isolate_target_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
      }
      // only the pin table is consulted, exactly as the reference's IsolateStmt
      // branch does: the pin-after-route direction is NOT a refusal there (the
      // routed key resolves through `routes`, which shadows the single-realm
      // table at link), so mirroring it would over-refuse.
      if (iso_has(iso, key, 0)) {
        return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
          tagged("G1", isolate_twice_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
      }
      let rlm = tkc(ts, i + 5).text
      // advance past the closing `)` STRUCTURALLY (item 168): a line-based skip
      // swallows the rest of a single-line component body, losing every
      // declaration and `provide` block written after the pin.
      let nx = atk(ts, i + 6, ")") ? i + 7 : skip_line(ts, i)
      return p_comp_body(ts, nx, end, provh, reqh, cname, provs,
                         setup, iso.push(mk_provkey(key, rlm)), rts, icept,
                         hoff, action)
    }
    return p_comp_body(ts, skip_line(ts, i), end, provh, reqh, cname, provs,
                       setup, iso, rts, icept, hoff, action)
  }
  // `handoff <key>: <Type>` (item 53): a prelude declaration naming a PROVIDED
  // key's live state, at most once per component. Prelude order first, then the
  // target/uniqueness validation. It is not an action (the reference `continue`s).
  if (atw(ts, i, "handoff")) {
    if (action) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
                      tagged("PRELUDE", prelude_msg("handoff")),
                      skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    let key = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    if (!bind_has(provh, key)) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
        tagged("HANDOFF", handoff_target_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    if (hoff) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
        tagged("HANDOFF", handoff_twice_msg(cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    return p_comp_body(ts, skip_line(ts, i), end, provh, reqh, cname, provs,
                       setup, iso, rts, icept, true, action)
  }
  // `intercept <key> with { ... }` supplies metadata, not a realm; the prelude
  // rule applies first, then the target/uniqueness validation. Its block is
  // skipped structurally. A valid `intercept` is NOT an action (it `continue`s
  // in the reference), so `action` is threaded unchanged.
  if (atw(ts, i, "intercept")) {
    if (action) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
                      tagged("PRELUDE", prelude_msg("intercept")),
                      skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    let key = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    if (!bind_has(reqh, key)) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
        tagged("G1", intercept_target_msg(key, cname, bind_has(provh, key))),
        skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    if (contains(icept, key)) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
        tagged("G1", intercept_twice_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    var k = i + 1
    while (k < end && !atk(ts, k, "{")) { k += 1 }
    if (atk(ts, k, "{")) {
      let ce = close_brace(ts, k)
      if (ce != 0 - 1) {
        return p_comp_body(ts, ce, end, provh, reqh, cname, provs, setup, iso,
                           rts, icept.push(key), hoff, action)
      }
    }
    return p_comp_body(ts, skip_line(ts, i), end, provh, reqh, cname, provs,
                       setup, iso, rts, icept.push(key), hoff, action)
  }
  // `config { <field>: <Type> ... }` is a component-level declaration, not a
  // body action (the reference reads it off the component header, never the
  // statement stream) — so it neither gates as setup nor trips the prelude
  // rule. Skip its block structurally, leaving `action` unchanged.
  if (atw(ts, i, "config") && atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
    let ce = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
    if (ce != 0 - 1) {
      return p_comp_body(ts, ce, end, provh, reqh, cname, provs, setup, iso,
                         rts, icept, hoff, action)
    }
  }
  // component setup: a statement run (item 168: ended at a structural boundary,
  // not only a newline) — G4/A1/G1 gate setup too, and the statement makes this
  // an action (later prelude decls are refused).
  let le = line_end(ts, i, end)
  let r = p_stmt_run(ts, i, le)
  let nx = r.i > i ? r.i : le
  return p_comp_body(ts, nx, end, provh, reqh, cname, provs,
                     append_stmts(setup, r.ss), iso, rts, icept, hoff, true)
}

// `isolate <key> in realms("a","b") [strategy(<s>)]` — the multi-realm bind
// (item 162), lower.py's `RouteStmt` branch, in the reference's own order:
// prelude placement, then the target (a REQUIRED key — routing distributes a
// consumer's dependency; a provision has one installed instance in one realm),
// then one-binding-per-key (pinned already, or routed already), then the
// strategy name against the closed set. The clause is advanced STRUCTURALLY
// (past its `)`), not by line, so a one-line component body keeps its
// following statements (item 168).
fn p_route_stmt(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int, provh: List[Bind],
                reqh: List[Bind], cname: Str, provs: List[Provide],
                setup: List[Stmt], iso: List[ProvKey], rts: List[Route],
                icept: List[Str], hoff: Bool, action: Bool) -> ProvR {
  if (action) {
    return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
                    tagged("PRELUDE", route_prelude_msg()), skip_line(ts, i), true)
  }
  let key = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  if (!bind_has(reqh, key)) {
    if (bind_has(provh, key)) {
      return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
        tagged("ROUTE", route_provision_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
    }
    return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
      tagged("G1", route_undeclared_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
  }
  if (iso_has(iso, key, 0)) {
    return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
      tagged("ROUTE", route_pinned_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
  }
  if (routes_has(rts, key, 0)) {
    return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
      tagged("ROUTE", route_twice_msg(key, cname)), skip_line(ts, i), true)
  }
  let rc = p_route_clause(ts, i + 3)
  if (!rc.ok) {
    return p_comp_body(ts, skip_line(ts, i), end, provh, reqh, cname, provs,
                       setup, iso, rts, icept, hoff, action)
  }
  if (rc.strat != "" && !contains(known_strategies(), rc.strat)) {
    return mk_provr(provs, setup, iso, rts,
      tagged("ROUTE", route_strategy_msg(rc.strat, key, cname)),
      skip_line(ts, i), true)
  }
  return p_comp_body(ts, rc.i, end, provh, reqh, cname, provs, setup, iso,
                     rts.push({ key: key, rlms: rc.rlms, strat: rc.strat }),
                     icept, hoff, action)
}

// The realm a component resolves `key` in: its isolate binding, else shared.
fn realm_of(iso: List[ProvKey], key: Str, i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= iso.length()) { return "" }
  if (iso[i].key == key) { return iso[i].rlm }
  return realm_of(iso, key, i + 1)
}

fn p_component(ts: List[Token], i: Int, pg: Prog) -> PStep {
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  var j = i + 2
  var reqs: List[Bind] = []
  var provh: List[Bind] = []
  while (atw(ts, j, "requires") || atw(ts, j, "provides")) {
    let isReq = atw(ts, j, "requires")
    var k = j + 1
    while (k < ts.length() && !atk(ts, k, "{") && !atk(ts, k, "eof")
           && !atw(ts, k, "requires") && !atw(ts, k, "provides")) {
      if (atk(ts, k, "ident") && atk(ts, k + 1, ":") && atk(ts, k + 2, "ident")) {
        let pair = { name: tkc(ts, k).text, ty: tkc(ts, k + 2).text }
        if (isReq) { reqs = reqs.push(pair) } else { provh = provh.push(pair) }
        k += 3
      } else { k += 1 }
    }
    j = k
  }
  if (!atk(ts, j, "{")) {
    return mk_step(bad_prog(pg, "expected { after component header"), j)
  }
  let end = close_brace(ts, j)
  if (end == 0 - 1) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "unbalanced braces in component"),
             i: ts.length() }
  }
  let body = p_comp_body(ts, j + 1, end - 1, provh, reqs, nm, [], [], [], [],
                         [], false, false)
  if (!body.ok) {
    return { pg: bad_prog(pg, "bad provide block in component ".concat(nm)),
             i: end }
  }
  // each declared provision key carries the realm its `isolate` names (shared
  // when unisolated); the linker's per-(key, realm) G2 reads these. Keys are
  // sorted, mirroring lower.py's `sorted(comp["provides"])`.
  let pkeys = sort_strs(bind_names(provh, 0, []))
  var pks: List[ProvKey] = []
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < pkeys.length()) {
    pks = pks.push(mk_provkey(pkeys[pi], realm_of(body.iso, pkeys[pi], 0)))
    pi += 1
  }
  return mk_step(push_comp(pg,
                 mk_compd(nm, pks, body.provs, reqs, body.setup, body.iso,
                          body.routes, body.refuse)),
                 end)
}

fn p_top(ts: List[Token], i: Int, pg: Prog) -> Prog {
  if (i >= ts.length() || atk(ts, i, "eof")) { return pg }
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (at_boot(ts, i)) { return p_top(ts, i + 1, pg) }
  if (t.kind != "kw") {
    return p_top(ts, skip_line(ts, i),
                 bad_prog(pg, "unexpected token at top level"))
  }
  if (t.text == "use" || t.text == "test" || t.text == "type") {
    // frontend `use`, `test` blocks and type decls are checker-stage concerns;
    // admission reads none of them. Skip structurally (brace blocks included).
    if (t.text == "test" && atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { return p_top(ts, e, pg) }
    }
    return p_top(ts, skip_line(ts, i), pg)
  }
  if (t.text == "extern") {
    let se = p_extern(ts, i, pg)
    return p_top(ts, se.i, se.pg)
  }
  let public_fn = t.text == "pub" && atw(ts, i + 1, "fn")
  if (t.text == "fn" || public_fn) {
    let sf = p_fn(ts, public_fn ? i + 1 : i, pg)
    return p_top(ts, sf.i, sf.pg)
  }
  if (t.text == "service") {
    let sv = p_service(ts, i, pg)
    return p_top(ts, sv.i, sv.pg)
  }
  if (t.text == "component") {
    let sc = p_component(ts, i, pg)
    return p_top(ts, sc.i, sc.pg)
  }
  return p_top(ts, skip_line(ts, i), bad_prog(pg, "unexpected declaration"))
}

pub fn parse_prog(src: Str) -> Prog {
  return p_top(lex_src(src), 0, { fns: [], svcs: [], comps: [], bad: "" })
}

// ================================================================ fixed points
//
// The two least-fixed-points admission runs over the fn call graph, both the
// name-call subset (first-class dispatch is out of this slice). `emit_caps`
// seeds the `emission` externs and yields, per name, the capability set its
// call reaches (docs/capabilities.md). `async_colored` seeds the `async`
// externs and yields the set of names an `await` must be inserted for
// (docs/design/async-extern.md §3) — deliberately no `*`/first-class widening.

type CapsT = Map[Str, List[Str]]

fn caps_of(caps: CapsT, n: Str) -> List[Str] {
  return match caps.lookup(n) { Some(v) => v, None => [] }
}

fn reached_caps(called: List[Str], caps: CapsT, i: Int,
                acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= called.length()) { return acc }
  return reached_caps(called, caps, i + 1,
                      union_into(acc, caps_of(caps, called[i])))
}

fn emit_caps(fs: List[FnD]) -> CapsT {
  return emit_caps_reach(fs, false)
}

fn emit_caps_reach(fs: List[FnD], include_values: Bool) -> CapsT {
  var caps: CapsT = Map.empty()
  let names = fn_names_of(fs)
  var i = 0
  while (i < fs.length()) {
    if (fs[i].isEmExtern) { caps = caps.set(fs[i].name, [fs[i].name]) }
    i += 1
  }
  var changed = true
  while (changed) {
    changed = false
    i = 0
    while (i < fs.length()) {
      let f = fs[i]
      let refs = include_values ? col_values_body(f.body, 0, names, f.callees) : f.callees
      let reached = reached_caps(refs, caps, 0, [])
      let cur = caps_of(caps, f.name)
      if (reached.length() > 0 && !subset_of(reached, cur)) {
        caps = caps.set(f.name, union_into(cur, reached))
        changed = true
      }
      i += 1
    }
  }
  return caps
}

// The `Async[T]` parameter-index table: callable name -> the slots an arrow
// argument is coerced into (lower.py's `_is_async_fn_type` over each signature).
// Built once and read by BOTH the async coloring (which prunes a coerced arrow)
// and the arrow-color leak check (which admits one).
fn async_slots_map(fs: List[FnD]) -> Map[Str, List[Int]] {
  var m: Map[Str, List[Int]] = Map.empty()
  var i = 0
  while (i < fs.length()) {
    if (fs[i].asyncSlots.length() > 0) { m = m.set(fs[i].name, fs[i].asyncSlots) }
    i += 1
  }
  return m
}

// The async twin: a name is colored iff it is an `async` extern, its body calls
// a colored callee, or (rule 2, item 92 §3) its body CALLS one of its own
// parameters whose declared type is an async function type (`asyncParams`) — a
// declaration-shaped seed the fixed point folds in exactly as
// `_async_callables`'s `calls_async_param` does. Monotone closure over the same
// finite name set.
//
// The callee set is the ARROW-PRUNED one (`abody_callees`), not `FnD.callees`:
// `_async_callables` passes `stop_async_arrows=True`, so a call that happens
// only inside a coerced (async-stamped) arrow does not color the body that
// constructs it. `FnD.callees` stays unpruned for the emission fixed point,
// which deliberately keeps bubbling through those arrows.
fn async_colored(fs: List[FnD], slots: Map[Str, List[Int]]) -> List[Str] {
  var colored: List[Str] = []
  var reach: List[List[Str]] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < fs.length()) {
    if (fs[i].isAsyncExtern) { colored = colored.push(fs[i].name) }
    reach = reach.push(abody_callees(fs[i].body, 0, slots, []))
    i += 1
  }
  var changed = true
  while (changed) {
    changed = false
    i = 0
    while (i < fs.length()) {
      let f = fs[i]
      if (!contains(colored, f.name)) {
        if (any_in(reach[i], colored) || any_in(reach[i], f.asyncParams)) {
          colored = colored.push(f.name)
          changed = true
        }
      }
      i += 1
    }
  }
  return colored
}

fn any_in(xs: List[Str], set: List[Str]) -> Bool {
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (contains(set, xs[i])) { return true }
    i += 1
  }
  return false
}

// ================================================================ body walk
//
// One walk over a provide-method (or setup) body collects, in the reference's
// order: an inline G4 emit-marker refusal, the G4 emission evidence (labels +
// capability set), and the A1 async reach (async callables called by name, and
// async service ops reached through a required key). Modelled on
// `_method_emissions` + `_component_req_call`.

// svcs: name -> SvcD ; reqMap: local key -> service name ; caps: the emit
// fixed point ; colored: async-colored names. `scopeNames` are the names
// declared inside the component (its setup/method locals and every provide
// method's parameters) and `fnNames` the callable names (module fns, externs,
// and host/stdlib constructors) — together they are the G1 resolution
// environment: a `root.<m>(...)` or `f(...)` head that is none of a
// requirement, a scope name, a callable, `config`, or an Upper-cased host
// constructor is an undeclared access.
// `fnAsyncSlots` maps a callable name to its `Async[T]` parameter indices — the
// call-site coercion table (lower.py's `_coerce_async_args`): an arrow argument
// in one of these slots is stamped async and is exempt from the arrow-color
// leak refusal. `underArrow` is true while the body walk is inside an arrow
// value: it gates ONLY the async-service-op (`aops`) accumulation, mirroring
// `_reached_async_req_ops`, which prunes arrows (a suspending op reached only
// through an arrow is the leak surface's concern, not the method's own reach).
// The emission (`ecaps`/`labels`), name-reach (`areach`) and value
// (`avals`) accumulations still descend into arrows, exactly as the
// reference's `_method_emissions` / `_calls_in` do.
// `handles` maps a spawn-handle local (bound by `let h = effect spawn C …`) to
// the target component C, so a provision-method call off a handle
// (`h.<key>.<op>(…)`) can be resolved and its emission-marker discipline
// enforced (lower.py's `_instance_get_call` unmarked-handle-emission G4).
// `provKeySvc` is the global (component `#` key -> service) table those handle
// reads resolve the op's service through (a target's provided key -> its SvcD).
// `provAlias` is PROVISION PROVENANCE: a local bound to a spawn-handle
// provision read (`let t = w.task`) mapped to the `<handle>#<key>` it names.
// The binding is not the crossing — `t.run(p)` is the same boundary crossing
// `w.task.run(p)` is — so the alias resolves back to the handle read and the
// marker rule judges the direct form, exactly as `Env.provision_locals` makes
// the reference's IR do. Scoped per body (setup, then each provide method on
// top of setup's), matching the reference's save/restore around a method.
// `acqWhere` is the position phrase the host-acquire refusal reports for the
// statement currently being walked (`stmt_acq_where`), the gate's stand-in for
// `env._expr_mode`.
type Ctx = { svcs: Map[Str, SvcD], reqMap: Map[Str, Str], caps: CapsT,
             colored: List[Str], emittingNames: List[Str], asyncExterns: List[Str],
             scopeNames: List[Str], fnNames: List[Str], compName: Str,
             fnAsyncSlots: Map[Str, List[Int]],
             underArrow: Bool, handles: Map[Str, Str],
             provKeySvc: Map[Str, Str], provAlias: Map[Str, Str],
             acqWhere: Str }

// The accumulator threaded through the walk. `msg` (non-"") is a definite
// inline refusal (the G4 emit-marker rule or a G1 undeclared access), tagged
// by `tag`. `labels`/`ecaps` are the G4 evidence; `areach` the async callables
// reached by name; `aops` the async service ops "req.op" reached; `avals` the
// async callables referenced as a first-class VALUE (a bare `Var` naming an
// async extern/colored fn in value position — the `passed_async` surface).
type Ac = { msg: Str, tag: Str, labels: List[Str], ecaps: List[Str],
            areach: List[Str], aops: List[Str], avals: List[Str] }

fn empty_ac() -> Ac {
  return { msg: "", tag: "", labels: [], ecaps: [], areach: [], aops: [],
           avals: [] }
}

fn ac_refuse(a: Ac, tag: Str, m: Str) -> Ac {
  return { msg: m, tag: tag, labels: a.labels, ecaps: a.ecaps,
           areach: a.areach, aops: a.aops, avals: a.avals }
}

fn find_msig(svc: SvcD, nm: Str, i: Int) -> MSig {
  if (i >= svc.methods.length()) {
    return { name: "", isEm: false, caps: [], isAsync: false }
  }
  if (svc.methods[i].name == nm) { return svc.methods[i] }
  return find_msig(svc, nm, i + 1)
}

fn svc_of(cx: Ctx, svcName: Str) -> SvcD {
  return match cx.svcs.lookup(svcName) {
    Some(s) => s,
    None    => { name: svcName, methods: [] },
  }
}

// `marked` is the G4 emit-marking of the expression being walked. Both `emit`
// and its `compensate` carry it: a compensation is the emission's INVERSE and
// runs under the same marking, so `emit outbox.add(r) compensate db.execute(q)`
// is a marked emission in both halves. Walking a compensation unmarked refuses
// eighty-five programs in this repo's own corpus that the reference admits —
// a compensation that reverses a write is the ordinary shape, not the exotic
// one.
fn walk_stmts(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (i >= ss.length() || a.msg != "") { return a }
  let s = ss[i]
  let marked = s.kind == "emit" || s.kind == "compensate"
  // the host-acquire position this statement puts its expression in, and the
  // one exemption: an `effect` bracket's own root acquisition. Walking that
  // root's ARGUMENTS rather than the root call itself is what makes the
  // exemption identity-shaped (see `acq_root_of`).
  let scx = ctx_acq(cx, stmt_acq_where(s.kind))
  let root = acq_root_of(s)
  let na = root.hit ? walk_exprs(root.args, 0, marked, scx, a)
                    : walk_expr(s.e, marked, scx, a)
  return walk_stmts(ss, i + 1, cx, na)
}

fn walk_exprs(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (i >= xs.length() || a.msg != "") { return a }
  return walk_exprs(xs, i + 1, marked, cx, walk_expr(xs[i], marked, cx, a))
}

fn walk_inits(xs: List[InitN], i: Int, marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (i >= xs.length() || a.msg != "") { return a }
  return walk_inits(xs, i + 1, marked, cx, walk_expr(xs[i].value, marked, cx, a))
}

fn walk_arms(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int, marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (i >= xs.length() || a.msg != "") { return a }
  let arm = xs[i]
  // a match arm's payload binding is a declared name inside the arm body.
  let acx = arm.bind != "" ? ctx_bind(cx, [arm.bind]) : cx
  return walk_arms(xs, i + 1, marked, cx, walk_expr(arm.body, marked, acx, a))
}

fn walk_parts(xs: List[PartN], i: Int, marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (i >= xs.length() || a.msg != "") { return a }
  return walk_parts(xs, i + 1, marked, cx, walk_expr(xs[i].e, marked, cx, a))
}

// FIRST REFUSAL WINS (issue #285). Every list walker above already stops once
// `a.msg` is set, because the reference raises on the first thing it finds and
// this gate promises its refusals are the reference's VERBATIM. `walk_expr`
// was missing the same guard, and the composite arms below thread the
// accumulator through more than one sub-walk — `Bin` walks `l` then `r`,
// `Index` walks the target then the index, `If` walks cond/then/else. A
// refusal raised by the earlier sub-walk was then overwritten by a refusal
// from a later one, because `ac_refuse` replaces `msg`/`tag` unconditionally.
// LAST refusal won instead of first.
//
// The visible symptom was a diagnostic naming a subject that looks truncated:
// a source containing `st|ore` holds two undeclared names, `st` and `ore`; the
// reference names `st`, this gate named `ore`, and the reader searches for a
// symbol their program does not contain. The same clobber also replaced the
// TAG — `kv.put("a") + beta` is a G4 the reference raises on the receiver, and
// this gate reported the G1 from the right operand, so a consumer switching on
// the refusal code was handed the wrong guarantee. That half is not cosmetic.
fn walk_expr(e: Expr, marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (a.msg != "") { return a }
  return match e {
    IntLit(_)   => a,
    FloatLit(_) => a,
    StrLit(_)   => a,
    BoolLit(_) => a,
    NullLit    => a,
    Var(n)     => var_check(n, cx, a),
    Hole(_)    => a,
    Bad(_)     => a,
    Bin(b)     => walk_expr(b.r, marked, cx, walk_expr(b.l, marked, cx, a)),
    Un(u)      => walk_expr(u.e, marked, cx, a),
    Emit(u)    => walk_expr(u.e, true, cx, a),
    Call(c)    => call_check(c.target, c.args, marked, cx, a),
    Field(f)   => field_check(f.target, marked, cx, a),
    OptField(f) => field_check(f.target, marked, cx, a),
    OptCall(c) => walk_exprs(c.args, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(c.target, marked, cx, a)),
    Index(x)   => walk_expr(x.idx, marked, cx, walk_expr(x.target, marked, cx, a)),
    If(x)      => walk_expr(x.els, marked, cx,
                           walk_expr(x.then_, marked, cx,
                                     walk_expr(x.cond, marked, cx, a))),
    Rec(r)     => walk_inits(r.fields, 0, marked, cx, a),
    Lst(l)     => walk_exprs(l.items, 0, marked, cx, a),
    // an arrow's parameters are declared names inside its body (the reference
    // binds them into scope before lowering it), so the G1 bare-value check
    // must see them — otherwise `xs.map(x => x + 1)` refuses its own `x`.
    Arrow(ar)  => walk_expr(ar.body, marked,
                            ctx_under_arrow(ctx_bind(cx, param_names(ar.params))), a),
    Match(m)   => walk_arms(m.arms, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(m.scrut, marked, cx, a)),
    Templ(t)   => walk_parts(t.parts, 0, marked, cx, a),
  }
}

fn call_check(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  return match tg {
    Field(fl) => match fl.target {
      // a local that ALIASES a spawn-handle provision (`let t = w.task`) is
      // the provision, so `t.<op>(…)` takes the handle path and is judged as
      // `w.task.<op>(…)` is — same rule, same message, same spelling.
      Var(v)      => cx.provAlias.has(v)
        ? alias_call(v, fl.name, args, tg, marked, cx, a)
        : req_call(v, fl.name, args, marked, cx, a),
      // `h.<key>.<op>(…)` — a doubly-nested field head is a candidate provision
      // read off a spawn handle. If `h` is a known handle it is the instance
      // path (checked like a `req` emission); otherwise it is walked as a value.
      Field(inner) => handle_call(inner, fl.name, args, tg, marked, cx, a),
      _           => walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(tg, marked, cx, a)),
    },
    Var(n) => fn_call(n, args, marked, cx, a),
    _      => walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(tg, marked, cx, a)),
  }
}

// The spawn-handle provision-method path (lower.py's `_instance_get_call`): a
// call `h.<key>.<op>(…)` where `h` is a handle bound to `spawn <Comp>`. `<key>`
// must be a provision of `<Comp>`, and if the resolved op is an `emission` it
// crosses the boundary exactly as a required-service emission — an unmarked
// call is the same G4 refusal, spelled `h.<key>.<op>`. If the resolved op is
// `async` it suspends exactly as a required-service async op does, so it
// contributes to `aops` on the same terms (roadmap item 98 — the reference's
// `_async_service_op` reads both receiver shapes). A non-handle head, or an op
// that is neither an emission nor async, is walked as a value.
fn handle_call(inner: FieldN, op: Str, args: List[Expr], tg: Expr,
               marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  return match inner.target {
    Var(h) => cx.handles.has(h)
      ? handle_emit(h, inner.name, op, args, marked, cx, a)
      : walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(tg, marked, cx, a)),
    _ => walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(tg, marked, cx, a)),
  }
}

// A call through a local that ALIASES a spawn-handle provision. `provAlias`
// holds `<handle>#<key>`; splitting it restores the receiver the direct
// spelling has, and `handle_emit` then decides the crossing with no knowledge
// that it went through a binding. This is the reference's provenance fix
// (`Env.provision_locals` + `_resolve_provision_alias`) in the gate's shape:
// no new rule, one resolution, and the marker rule, the async reach and the
// argument walk all follow.
fn alias_call(v: Str, op: Str, args: List[Expr], tg: Expr, marked: Bool,
              cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  let ref = match cx.provAlias.lookup(v) { Some(r) => r, None => "" }
  let cut = ref.indexOf("#")
  if (cut == 0 - 1) {
    return walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, walk_expr(tg, marked, cx, a))
  }
  return handle_emit(ref.slice(0, cut), ref.slice(cut + 1, ref.length()), op,
                     args, marked, cx, a)
}

// The `MSig` a `h.<key>.<op>` head resolves to: the handle's spawn target, that
// component's provision key, the service it yields, and the op on it. Empty
// (`name == ""`) when any hop is unknown.
fn handle_msig(h: Str, key: Str, op: Str, cx: Ctx) -> MSig {
  let comp = match cx.handles.lookup(h) { Some(c) => c, None => "" }
  let svcName = match cx.provKeySvc.lookup(comp.concat("#").concat(key)) {
    Some(s) => s, None => "" }
  return find_msig(svc_of(cx, svcName), op, 0)
}

fn handle_emit(h: Str, key: Str, op: Str, args: List[Expr], marked: Bool,
               cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  let decl = handle_msig(h, key, op, cx)
  if (decl.isEm && !marked) {
    return ac_refuse(a, "G4", "call to emission `".concat(h).concat(".")
      .concat(key).concat(".").concat(op).concat("` must be marked `emit` (G4)"))
  }
  var na = a
  // the same arrow prune the `req` path applies: an op reached only THROUGH an
  // arrow value is the leak surface's concern, not the method's own reach.
  if (decl.isAsync && !cx.underArrow) {
    na = { msg: "", tag: "", labels: na.labels, ecaps: na.ecaps, areach: na.areach,
           aops: union_into(na.aops, [h.concat(".").concat(key).concat(".")
                                       .concat(op)]),
           avals: na.avals }
  }
  return walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, na)
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- host acquire verbs
//
// A host ACQUIRE verb is the constructor of a host family whose release is a
// SEPARATE verb (typecheck.py's `_HOST_ACQUIRE_VERBS`), mapped here to that
// release. Calling one opens a host resource that nothing reclaims until its
// inverse runs, so the call belongs in an acquisition bracket
// (`effect <acquire> undo <release>`) and nowhere else: the bracket is what
// registers the release with the activation's teardown accumulator. Anywhere
// else — a plain `let`, a teardown slot, an `emit` expression, a `fn` body a
// component reaches — the resource is acquired irreversibly and the runtime
// reports residue (`pool#1 (open() with no close())`, R1) on a program the
// gate admitted.
//
// The table is the reference's, verbatim and by hand rather than derived: the
// pairing is a semantic claim, not a naming one. `Job.run` is deliberately
// absent (fire-and-forget, no declared release) and `Map.empty` is a VALUE
// constructor, not a host acquisition. "" = not an acquire verb.
fn host_acquire_release(root: Str, verb: Str) -> Str {
  let dotted = root.concat(".").concat(verb)
  if (dotted == "Map.new") { return "drop" }
  if (dotted == "Pool.open") { return "close" }
  if (dotted == "Stream.source") { return "close" }
  return ""
}

// The refusal, spelled exactly as `_refuse_unbracketed_host_acquire` spells it.
// `where` names the position the call occupies, so the two spellings the
// reference produces — a positional one ("this position" / "a teardown slot" /
// "an `emit` expression") and the reach one ("`fn f`, which a component body
// reaches") — are one message with one hole.
fn host_acquire_msg(root: Str, verb: Str, rel: Str, where: Str) -> Str {
  return "host acquisition `".concat(root).concat(".").concat(verb)
    .concat("` cannot be called in ").concat(where)
    .concat(" — nothing registers its `").concat(rel)
    .concat("()`, so the resource is acquired irreversibly (G4)")
}

// The `where` phrase for a statement position, from the statement's kind. The
// reference reads `env._expr_mode`: an `undo`/`compensate` slot lowers under
// mode "undo", an `emit`/`compensate` expression under "emit", everything else
// under "setup". A compensation is a teardown slot for THIS rule even though
// it is emit-MARKED for the G4 marker rule: it is the emission's inverse, and
// an inverse may not acquire.
fn stmt_acq_where(kind: Str) -> Str {
  if (kind == "undo" || kind == "compensate") { return "a teardown slot" }
  if (kind == "emit") { return "an `emit` expression" }
  return "this position"
}

// Whether a statement is an `effect` bracket whose OWN root call is a host
// acquire verb, and the arguments of that root call.
//
// Identity, not shape — the reference marks the bracket's acquisition
// EXPRESSION (`_acquire_position`) and compares by identity, so
// `effect wrap(Pool.open(u, 1)) undo …`, which acquires a pool the bracket's
// inverse never names, is still refused. Here the same distinction is drawn by
// walking the root call's ARGUMENTS instead of the call: the head is exempt,
// everything under it is not.
type AcqRoot = { hit: Bool, args: List[Expr] }

fn acq_no_root() -> AcqRoot { return { hit: false, args: [] } }

fn acq_root_of(s: Stmt) -> AcqRoot {
  if (s.kind != "effect") { return acq_no_root() }
  return match s.e {
    Call(c) => match c.target {
      Field(fl) => match fl.target {
        Var(v) => host_acquire_release(v, fl.name) != ""
          ? { hit: true, args: c.args }
          : acq_no_root(),
        _ => acq_no_root(),
      },
      _ => acq_no_root(),
    },
    _ => acq_no_root(),
  }
}

// A known host root is a host/stdlib constructor receiver (`Map.new()`,
// `Pool.open(...)`) — the reference lowers it as a host acquisition, never a
// G1 access. Unknown Upper-cased names remain undeclared.
fn is_upper_head(name: Str) -> Bool {
  return name == "Map" || name == "Pool" || name == "Job" || name == "Stream"
}

fn is_upper_name(name: Str) -> Bool {
  if (name.length() == 0) { return false }
  let c = name.charAt(0)
  return c >= "A" && c <= "Z"
}

// The reference's head resolution (lower.py `_lower_postfix` / component-expr
// `ExprVar`): a name resolves if it is a declared scope name (local/param), a
// callable, `config`, or an Upper-cased host constructor. A required key is
// resolved separately (it is the service path). Anything else is a G1
// undeclared access.
fn head_declared(cx: Ctx, name: Str) -> Bool {
  return contains(cx.scopeNames, name) || contains(cx.fnNames, name)
      || name == "config" || is_upper_head(name)
}

// The head of a BARE call `f(...)` — no member access follows it.
//
// `head_declared` above is the RECEIVER rule and keeps `is_upper_head`, because
// the reference's host-acquisition branch is
// `head[:1].isupper() and ops and ops[0].args is not None` (lower.py
// `_lower_postfix`): an Upper-cased head is a host constructor only when a
// `.method(...)` follows it. `Map.new()` takes that branch; `Nope(1)`, `P()`
// and `Int("1")` do not — they fall through to the same
// "`X` is not a declared requirement of C" G1 refusal `nope()` draws.
//
// This gate applied `is_upper_head` in BOTH positions, so every bare
// Upper-cased call head resolved and the whole family was admitted. That is the
// residual PR #228 left behind: the statement-position G1 cases it closed all
// went through lower-cased heads.
//
// The legitimate bare Upper-cased calls are the ADT constructors: the built-in
// `Some`/`None`/`Ok`/`Err` (seeded into `fnNames` by `fn_names_of`) and the
// program's own `type T = A | B(Int)` cases, which `type_ctors` collects. That
// collection is deliberately an OVER-approximation — it takes every Upper-cased
// name appearing in a `type` declaration, field types included — because
// over-collecting only ever keeps admitting a program the reference admits,
// while under-collecting would refuse one. A residual `Int("1")` admitted in a
// program that happens to declare a type with an `Int` field is the price, and
// it is the safe side of the asymmetry this crate is built on.
fn call_head_declared(cx: Ctx, name: Str) -> Bool {
  return contains(cx.scopeNames, name) || contains(cx.fnNames, name)
      || name == "config"
}

fn g1_refuse(cx: Ctx, name: Str, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  return ac_refuse(a, "G1", "`".concat(name)
    .concat("` is not a declared requirement of ").concat(cx.compName))
}

// A call through a receiver `root.meth`. When `root` is a required key this is
// the service path (G4: an emission op must be under `emit`, an inline refusal,
// and a marked emission contributes its capability (the key) and label; A1: an
// async op contributes to `aops`). Otherwise `root` must resolve to a declared
// name (local/param/callable/host); an unresolved head is a G1 undeclared
// access — the reference refuses the receiver before it ever inspects the op.
fn req_call(root: Str, meth: Str, args: List[Expr], marked: Bool,
            cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (!cx.reqMap.has(root)) {
    // G4: a host ACQUIRE verb is legal only as an `effect` bracket's own
    // acquisition. `walk_stmts` has already lifted that one exempt position
    // out of the walk, so anything reaching here is unbracketed.
    let rel = host_acquire_release(root, meth)
    if (rel != "") {
      return ac_refuse(a, "G4",
                       host_acquire_msg(root, meth, rel, cx.acqWhere))
    }
    if (!head_declared(cx, root)) { return g1_refuse(cx, root, a) }
    // a host object or a local receiver, not a requirement — unchecked here
    return walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, a)
  }
  let svcName = match cx.reqMap.lookup(root) { Some(v) => v, None => "" }
  let decl = find_msig(svc_of(cx, svcName), meth, 0)
  if (decl.name == "") {
    // an unknown method: out of this slice (checker.rvl's typing catches it)
    return walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, a)
  }
  if (decl.isEm && !marked) {
    return ac_refuse(a, "G4", "call to emission `".concat(root).concat(".")
      .concat(meth).concat("` must be marked `emit` (G4)"))
  }
  var na = a
  if (decl.isEm && marked) {
    // `union_into`, not `push`: the reference's `_method_emissions` accumulates
    // its evidence through `note`, which is first-seen-order and DEDUPED by its
    // `seen` set. A body that emits through the same op twice
    // (`emit db.execute(a)  emit db.execute(b)`) therefore draws
    // "reaches `db.execute`" from the reference and used to draw
    // "reaches `db.execute`, `db.execute`" from this gate — the same refusal,
    // spelled differently, which breaks the crate's byte-agreement promise.
    na = { msg: "", tag: "",
           labels: union_into(na.labels, [root.concat(".").concat(meth)]),
           ecaps: union_into(na.ecaps, [root]), areach: na.areach, aops: na.aops,
           avals: na.avals }
  }
  // a suspending service op reached only THROUGH an arrow value is the leak
  // surface's concern, not the method's own reach — `_reached_async_req_ops`
  // prunes arrows, so `aops` is suppressed while `underArrow`.
  if (decl.isAsync && !cx.underArrow) {
    na = { msg: "", tag: "", labels: na.labels, ecaps: na.ecaps, areach: na.areach,
           aops: union_into(na.aops, [root.concat(".").concat(meth)]),
           avals: na.avals }
  }
  return walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, na)
}

// A bare named call `name(...)`. The head must resolve to a callable (or a
// declared local, an Upper-cased constructor, or `config`) — an unresolved
// head is a G1 undeclared access, exactly as a `req` receiver is. G4 evidence:
// reaching an emitting name labels `f()` with its caps. A1: reaching an
// async-colored name records it for the reach verdict.
fn fn_call(name: Str, args: List[Expr], marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  if (!call_head_declared(cx, name)) { return g1_refuse(cx, name, a) }
  var na = a
  if (contains(cx.emittingNames, name)) {
    // deduped, as `_method_emissions`'s `note` is — see `req_call`
    na = { msg: "", tag: "", labels: union_into(na.labels, [name.concat("()")]),
           ecaps: union_into(na.ecaps, caps_of(cx.caps, name)),
           areach: na.areach, aops: na.aops, avals: na.avals }
  }
  if (contains(cx.colored, name)) {
    na = { msg: "", tag: "", labels: na.labels, ecaps: na.ecaps,
           areach: union_into(na.areach, [name]), aops: na.aops, avals: na.avals }
  }
  return walk_exprs(args, 0, marked, cx, na)
}

// A bare `Var` in VALUE position (never a call/access head — those are routed
// by `call_check` before `walk_expr` sees the head). Mirrors lower.py's
// `_lower_component_pure_expr`/`ExprVar` resolution and `_calls_in(values=…)`:
//   * a first-class reference to an emitting name is G4 evidence, labelled
//     `<name> (passed as a function value)` and contributing its concrete
//     capability set PLUS the unnameable `*` boundary (a dispatched value's
//     reach cannot be statically named), exactly as `_method_emissions` does;
//   * a first-class reference to an async-colored name is the `passed_async`
//     A1 surface, recorded in `avals` for the value-use verdict;
//   * G1: a name that resolves to no scope name and no callable is an
//     undeclared access. This used to be gated on `cx.plainBody`, on the
//     belief that a setup/activation body drew a different, out-of-slice
//     diagnostic from the reference. It does not: `component C requires s: S
//     { effect nope }` draws the same `` `nope` is not a declared requirement
//     of C `` the provide-method case does. The gate was therefore not a slice
//     boundary but a hole in one — the gate admitted an undeclared access in
//     exactly the position a component reaches for its authority.
// An emitting/colored name is always a callable (it is a declared fn/extern),
// so it is `bare_declared` and never trips the G1 branch.
fn var_check(name: Str, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  var na = a
  if (contains(cx.emittingNames, name)) {
    na = { msg: "", tag: "",
           labels: union_into(na.labels,
                              [name.concat(" (passed as a function value)")]),
           ecaps: union_into(na.ecaps, union_into(caps_of(cx.caps, name), ["*"])),
           areach: na.areach, aops: na.aops, avals: na.avals }
  }
  if (contains(cx.colored, name)) {
    na = { msg: "", tag: "", labels: na.labels, ecaps: na.ecaps,
           areach: na.areach, aops: na.aops,
           avals: union_into(na.avals, [name]) }
  }
  if (!bare_declared(cx, name)) { return g1_refuse(cx, name, na) }
  return na
}

// The value-position resolution environment (lower.py `ExprVar`): a bare value
// resolves iff it is a declared scope name (local/param) or a callable
// (module fn/extern or a host/stdlib constructor). Unlike a call/access HEAD,
// a bare `config` and an arbitrary Upper-cased name do NOT resolve here —
// `config` is only ever read as `config.<field>` (handled in `field_check`),
// and a required key is the service path, never a bare value.
fn bare_declared(cx: Ctx, name: Str) -> Bool {
  return contains(cx.scopeNames, name) || contains(cx.fnNames, name)
}

// The same context with extra local names in scope (an arrow's parameters, or
// a match arm's binding) — they are declared names inside the sub-expression.
fn ctx_bind(cx: Ctx, names: List[Str]) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: cx.svcs, reqMap: cx.reqMap, caps: cx.caps, colored: cx.colored,
           emittingNames: cx.emittingNames, asyncExterns: cx.asyncExterns,
           scopeNames: union_into(cx.scopeNames, names), fnNames: cx.fnNames,
           compName: cx.compName,
           fnAsyncSlots: cx.fnAsyncSlots, underArrow: cx.underArrow,
           handles: cx.handles, provKeySvc: cx.provKeySvc,
           provAlias: cx.provAlias, acqWhere: cx.acqWhere }
}

// The same context carrying the host-acquire position phrase of the statement
// being walked.
fn ctx_acq(cx: Ctx, where: Str) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: cx.svcs, reqMap: cx.reqMap, caps: cx.caps, colored: cx.colored,
           emittingNames: cx.emittingNames, asyncExterns: cx.asyncExterns,
           scopeNames: cx.scopeNames, fnNames: cx.fnNames,
           compName: cx.compName,
           fnAsyncSlots: cx.fnAsyncSlots, underArrow: cx.underArrow,
           handles: cx.handles, provKeySvc: cx.provKeySvc,
           provAlias: cx.provAlias, acqWhere: where }
}

// The same context carrying a body's provision-alias map.
fn ctx_alias(cx: Ctx, al: Map[Str, Str]) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: cx.svcs, reqMap: cx.reqMap, caps: cx.caps, colored: cx.colored,
           emittingNames: cx.emittingNames, asyncExterns: cx.asyncExterns,
           scopeNames: cx.scopeNames, fnNames: cx.fnNames,
           compName: cx.compName,
           fnAsyncSlots: cx.fnAsyncSlots, underArrow: cx.underArrow,
           handles: cx.handles, provKeySvc: cx.provKeySvc,
           provAlias: al, acqWhere: cx.acqWhere }
}

// The same context marked as being inside an arrow value: only the `aops`
// accumulation is suppressed downstream (`_reached_async_req_ops` prunes arrows).
fn ctx_under_arrow(cx: Ctx) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: cx.svcs, reqMap: cx.reqMap, caps: cx.caps, colored: cx.colored,
           emittingNames: cx.emittingNames, asyncExterns: cx.asyncExterns,
           scopeNames: cx.scopeNames, fnNames: cx.fnNames,
           compName: cx.compName,
           fnAsyncSlots: cx.fnAsyncSlots, underArrow: true,
           handles: cx.handles, provKeySvc: cx.provKeySvc,
           provAlias: cx.provAlias, acqWhere: cx.acqWhere }
}

// A field access `t.f` that is not a call. `config.<field>` is a config read,
// resolved before the target is treated as a value (so bare `config` is not a
// G1 access); every other target is walked as a value.
fn field_check(target: Expr, marked: Bool, cx: Ctx, a: Ac) -> Ac {
  return match target {
    Var(v) => v == "config" ? a : walk_expr(target, marked, cx, a),
    _      => walk_expr(target, marked, cx, a),
  }
}

// ================================================================ verdicts

// The G4 upper-bound verdict for one provision method (lower.py's provider
// check). "" = admitted.
fn g4_verdict(svcName: Str, mname: Str, decl: MSig, a: Ac) -> Str {
  if (!decl.isEm && decl.caps.length() == 0 && a.labels.length() > 0) {
    return "`".concat(svcName).concat(".").concat(mname)
      .concat("` is declared plain, but this implementation reaches ")
      .concat(quote_join(a.labels))
  }
  if (decl.isEm && decl.caps.length() > 0) {
    let extra = sort_strs(minus_list(a.ecaps, decl.caps))
    if (extra.length() > 0) {
      var msg = "`".concat(svcName).concat(".").concat(mname)
        .concat("` is declared `emission[").concat(comma_join(decl.caps))
        .concat("]`, but this implementation emits through ")
      var i = 0
      while (i < extra.length()) {
        if (i > 0) { msg = msg.concat(", ") }
        if (extra[i] == "*") { msg = msg.concat("an unnameable host boundary") }
        else { msg = msg.concat("`").concat(extra[i]).concat("`") }
        i += 1
      }
      if (a.labels.length() > 0) {
        msg = msg.concat(" (reaching ").concat(quote_join(a.labels)).concat(")")
      }
      return msg
    }
  }
  return ""
}

// The A1 sync-reaching-async verdict for one provision method (lower.py item
// 117). Only fires when the method's service op is sync (`!decl.isAsync`).
// Reaching an async callable is reported before an async service op, matching
// the reference's two checks in order.
fn a1_verdict(svcName: Str, mname: Str, decl: MSig, a: Ac, cx: Ctx) -> Str {
  if (decl.isAsync) { return "" }
  if (a.areach.length() > 0) {
    let names = sort_strs(a.areach)
    let culprit = names[0]
    let kind = contains(cx.asyncExterns, culprit) ? "extern" : "function"
    return "`".concat(svcName).concat(".").concat(mname)
      .concat("` is declared sync, but this implementation reaches async ")
      .concat(kind).concat(" ").concat(quote_join(names))
      .concat(" — a sync method has no in-flight window (A1)")
  }
  if (a.aops.length() > 0) {
    let op = a.aops[0]
    return "`".concat(svcName).concat(".").concat(mname)
      .concat("` is declared sync, but this implementation reaches async operation `")
      .concat(op).concat("` — a sync method has no in-flight window (A1)")
  }
  return ""
}

// ================================================================ admission
//
// The gate. Returns "" to admit, else "<tag>|<message>". Ordering mirrors
// lower.py exactly: per-component G4 then A1 (during lowering), and only if
// every component admits, G2 across the composition (at link).

// The callable name universe G1 resolves a bare-call head against: every
// module fn/extern, plus the host constructors (`_HOST_CALLABLES`) and the
// Opt/Result constructors (`_BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTORS`) the reference treats as
// callables in a component body.
fn fn_names_of(fns: List[FnD]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = ["Map", "Pool", "Job", "Some", "None", "Ok", "Err"]
  var i = 0
  while (i < fns.length()) {
    out = out.push(fns[i].name)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// The names a component declares locally (setup + method locals) and its
// method parameters — the G1 scope. Over-approximated across the whole
// component (a setup local is visible to every method, as in the reference;
// merging the methods' own locals only widens the resolved set, never a
// refusal into an admit on a reference-clean program).
fn stmt_binds(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return acc }
  let b = ss[i].bind
  return stmt_binds(ss, i + 1, b != "" ? acc.push(b) : acc)
}

fn methods_scope(ms: List[ProvM], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ms.length()) { return acc }
  let m = ms[i]
  return methods_scope(ms, i + 1,
                       stmt_binds(m.body, 0, union_into(acc, m.params)))
}

fn provs_scope(pvs: List[Provide], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= pvs.length()) { return acc }
  return provs_scope(pvs, i + 1, methods_scope(pvs[i].methods, 0, acc))
}

fn scope_names_of(comp: CompD) -> List[Str] {
  return provs_scope(comp.provs, 0, stmt_binds(comp.setup, 0, []))
}

fn build_maps(pg: Prog) -> Ctx {
  var svcs: Map[Str, SvcD] = Map.empty()
  var si = 0
  while (si < pg.svcs.length()) {
    svcs = svcs.set(pg.svcs[si].name, pg.svcs[si])
    si += 1
  }
  let caps = emit_caps(pg.fns)
  // the `Async[T]` slot table is built FIRST: the async coloring reads it to
  // prune a coerced arrow, exactly as `_async_callables` does.
  let slots = async_slots_map(pg.fns)
  let colored = async_colored(pg.fns, slots)
  var emitting: List[Str] = []
  var ai: List[Str] = []
  var fi = 0
  while (fi < pg.fns.length()) {
    let f = pg.fns[fi]
    if (caps_of(caps, f.name).length() > 0) { emitting = emitting.push(f.name) }
    if (f.isAsyncExtern) { ai = ai.push(f.name) }
    fi += 1
  }
  return mk_ctx(svcs, Map.empty(), caps, colored, emitting, ai,
                [], fn_names_of(pg.fns), "", slots, prov_key_svc(pg))
}

// The global (component `#` key -> service) table: every component's provided
// keys mapped to the service each supplies. A spawn-handle read `h.<key>.<op>`
// resolves the op's service through the target component's entry here.
fn prov_key_svc(pg: Prog) -> Map[Str, Str] {
  var m: Map[Str, Str] = Map.empty()
  var ci = 0
  while (ci < pg.comps.length()) {
    let c = pg.comps[ci]
    var pi = 0
    while (pi < c.provs.length()) {
      let pv = c.provs[pi]
      m = m.set(c.name.concat("#").concat(pv.key), pv.svcName)
      pi += 1
    }
    ci += 1
  }
  return m
}

fn mk_ctx(svcs: Map[Str, SvcD], rm: Map[Str, Str], caps: CapsT,
          colored: List[Str], emitting: List[Str], ai: List[Str],
          scope: List[Str], fnn: List[Str], cnm: Str,
          slots: Map[Str, List[Int]], pks: Map[Str, Str]) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: svcs, reqMap: rm, caps: caps, colored: colored,
           emittingNames: emitting, asyncExterns: ai, scopeNames: scope,
           fnNames: fnn, compName: cnm, fnAsyncSlots: slots,
           underArrow: false, handles: Map.empty(), provKeySvc: pks,
           provAlias: Map.empty(), acqWhere: "this position" }
}

// The same context with extra callable names — the program's own ADT
// constructors (`type_ctors`), which resolve a bare Upper-cased call head the
// same way the built-in `Ok`/`Err`/`Some`/`None` seeded by `fn_names_of` do.
fn ctx_with_callables(cx: Ctx, names: List[Str]) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: cx.svcs, reqMap: cx.reqMap, caps: cx.caps, colored: cx.colored,
           emittingNames: cx.emittingNames, asyncExterns: cx.asyncExterns,
           scopeNames: cx.scopeNames, fnNames: union_into(cx.fnNames, names),
           compName: cx.compName, fnAsyncSlots: cx.fnAsyncSlots,
           underArrow: cx.underArrow, handles: cx.handles,
           provKeySvc: cx.provKeySvc, provAlias: cx.provAlias,
           acqWhere: cx.acqWhere }
}

fn req_map_of(reqs: List[Bind]) -> Map[Str, Str] {
  var m: Map[Str, Str] = Map.empty()
  var i = 0
  while (i < reqs.length()) {
    m = m.set(reqs[i].name, reqs[i].ty)
    i += 1
  }
  return m
}

// The per-component context: the shared maps/fixed points from `base`, plus
// this component's requirements, local scope, and name (for the G1 diagnostic).
fn ctx_for(base: Ctx, comp: CompD) -> Ctx {
  return { svcs: base.svcs, reqMap: req_map_of(comp.reqMap), caps: base.caps,
           colored: base.colored, emittingNames: base.emittingNames,
           asyncExterns: base.asyncExterns, scopeNames: scope_names_of(comp),
           fnNames: base.fnNames, compName: comp.name,
           fnAsyncSlots: base.fnAsyncSlots, underArrow: false,
           handles: handles_of(comp), provKeySvc: base.provKeySvc,
           provAlias: Map.empty(), acqWhere: "this position" }
}

// The spawn handles a component binds: every `let h = effect spawn C …` in its
// setup or any provide-method body maps `h` -> C. Over-approximated across the
// whole component (a handle bound in one method is visible only there in the
// reference's `type_env`, but merging all only ever RESOLVES more handle reads,
// never turns a clean program's read into a refusal).
fn handles_of(comp: CompD) -> Map[Str, Str] {
  var m: Map[Str, Str] = handles_in(comp.setup, 0, Map.empty())
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      m = handles_in(pv.methods[mi].body, 0, m)
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return m
}

fn handles_in(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, m: Map[Str, Str]) -> Map[Str, Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return m }
  let s = ss[i]
  let hit = s.spawnTarget != "" && s.kind == "effect"
  let m2 = hit ? m.set(s.bind, s.spawnTarget) : m
  return handles_in(ss, i + 1, m2)
}

// The provision ALIASES one body binds: `let t = w.task` maps `t` ->
// `w#task` when `w` is a known spawn handle, and a second hop (`let u = t`)
// carries the same target forward — the reference's `_note_provision_alias`
// lands on both, because the second hop's value has already been resolved
// through the map. Built in statement ORDER, so a name is an alias only after
// the binding that makes it one.
//
// Unlike `handles_of`, this is NOT merged across a component's methods. The
// reference saves and restores `Env.provision_locals` around every provide
// method precisely because two methods may bind the same name to different
// things, and merging here could route an unrelated `t.run(…)` in a second
// method onto a first method's handle — a refusal on a program the reference
// admits, which is the one direction this gate may not err in.
fn alias_note(bind: Str, e: Expr, handles: Map[Str, Str],
              m: Map[Str, Str]) -> Map[Str, Str] {
  return match e {
    Field(fl) => match fl.target {
      Var(h) => handles.has(h)
        ? m.set(bind, h.concat("#").concat(fl.name))
        : m,
      _ => m,
    },
    Var(n) => match m.lookup(n) { Some(r) => m.set(bind, r), None => m },
    _ => m,
  }
}

fn alias_in(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, handles: Map[Str, Str],
            m: Map[Str, Str]) -> Map[Str, Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return m }
  let s = ss[i]
  // a `let h = effect spawn C …` binds the HANDLE, not an alias of one
  let m2 = (s.bind != "" && s.spawnTarget == "")
    ? alias_note(s.bind, s.e, handles, m) : m
  return alias_in(ss, i + 1, handles, m2)
}

fn tagged(tag: Str, msg: Str) -> Str { return tag.concat("|").concat(msg) }

// The A1 first-class-value verdict (lower.py's `passed_async`): a provide
// method that references an async callable as a VALUE — in ANY method, sync or
// async — cannot await a suspension through the value's colorless arrow type.
fn passed_async_verdict(svcName: Str, mname: Str, a: Ac, cx: Ctx) -> Str {
  if (a.avals.length() == 0) { return "" }
  let culprit = sort_strs(a.avals)[0]
  let kind = contains(cx.asyncExterns, culprit) ? "extern" : "function"
  return "`".concat(svcName).concat(".").concat(mname)
    .concat("` uses async ").concat(kind).concat(" `").concat(culprit)
    .concat("` as a function value, but an async callable has no arrow type")
}

// The A1 setup/activation async-reach verdict (lower.py's
// `_async_reached_outside_provide` at the composition loop): a setup body that
// reaches an async callable — by call (`areach`) or as a value (`avals`) —
// cannot suspend a fiber, so it is refused. (Reaching an async service OP in
// setup is deliberately NOT refused by the reference, so `aops` is excluded.)
fn setup_async_verdict(comp: CompD, sa: Ac, cx: Ctx) -> Str {
  let reached = union_into(sa.areach, sa.avals)
  if (reached.length() == 0) { return "" }
  let culprit = sort_strs(reached)[0]
  let kind = contains(cx.asyncExterns, culprit) ? "extern" : "function"
  return "component `".concat(comp.name).concat("` reaches async ").concat(kind)
    .concat(" `").concat(culprit)
    .concat("` in a setup/activation body, which cannot suspend a fiber (A1)")
}

// The services-2.0 signature-parity verdict (lower.py's `method.async_ !=
// decl.async_`): the `async` marker is part of the service interface, so an
// `async fn` op must be implemented by an `async fn` method, and vice-versa.
fn async_sig_msg(key: Str, mname: Str, svc: Str, mAsync: Bool,
                 dAsync: Bool) -> Str {
  return "method `".concat(mname).concat("` of provision `").concat(key)
    .concat("` is ").concat(mAsync ? "async" : "not async")
    .concat(" but service ").concat(svc).concat(" declares it ")
    .concat(dAsync ? "async" : "not async")
}

// Whether a method body contains an `await` statement (the await-outside-body
// A1 surface — the statement reader tags these `await`).
fn has_await(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int) -> Bool {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return false }
  if (ss[i].kind == "await") { return true }
  return has_await(ss, i + 1)
}

// ============================================================ arrow-color leak
//
// The A1 arrow-color LEAK trio (lower.py item 92 / finding #21): a sync-typed
// arrow that reaches a suspension carries no async color, so its caller would
// receive an unawaited suspension. The enabler is the `Async[T]` coercion model
// (`_coerce_async_args`): an arrow ARGUMENT landing in an `Async[T]` parameter
// slot is legitimately async (it is awaited through that colored type) and must
// be ADMITTED — the same arrow in a sync slot must be REFUSED. `fnAsyncSlots`
// carries which slots those are; `leaky_scan` skips a `coerced` arrow and
// checks every other one.
//
//   * `reach_scan` answers `_arrow_reaches_async` for one arrow body: does it
//     reach an async-colored callable BY NAME (`names`) or — in a component
//     body — a required-key async service OP (`hasReq`, rule 3)? A pure module
//     fn has no required keys, so it passes `checkReq = false` (names only,
//     matching `_refuse_leaky_pure_arrow`'s named-callable reach). The walk
//     STOPS at a nested COERCED arrow (`reach_args`), mirroring the reference's
//     prune at an already-async-flagged one: that arrow's suspension is its own
//     value, awaited through the colored type, not the constructing body's. This
//     was item 186's residual approximation — descending instead over-refused an
//     outer sync arrow whose only async reach sat inside a coerced inner one.
//
//   * `leaky_scan` walks a body for arrows; a non-coerced arrow whose body
//     reaches async is the refusal. In a provide method (`checkReq = true`) the
//     message is the reference's un-named `_refuse_leaky_arrow` wording; in a
//     module fn (`checkReq = false`) it is `_refuse_leaky_pure_arrow`'s wording,
//     naming the first (sorted) async callable reached.

type ReachAcc = { names: List[Str], hasReq: Bool }

fn reach_empty() -> ReachAcc { return { names: [], hasReq: false } }

fn reach_add_name(a: ReachAcc, n: Str) -> ReachAcc {
  return { names: union_into(a.names, [n]), hasReq: a.hasReq }
}

fn reach_set_req(a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  return { names: a.names, hasReq: true }
}

fn reach_exprs(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return a }
  return reach_exprs(xs, i + 1, cx, cr, reach_scan(xs[i], cx, cr, a))
}

fn reach_inits(xs: List[InitN], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return a }
  return reach_inits(xs, i + 1, cx, cr, reach_scan(xs[i].value, cx, cr, a))
}

fn reach_arms(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return a }
  return reach_arms(xs, i + 1, cx, cr, reach_scan(xs[i].body, cx, cr, a))
}

fn reach_parts(xs: List[PartN], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return a }
  return reach_parts(xs, i + 1, cx, cr, reach_scan(xs[i].e, cx, cr, a))
}

fn reach_reqcall(v: Str, meth: Str, args: List[Expr], cx: Ctx, cr: Bool,
                 a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  var a2 = a
  if (cr) {
    if (cx.reqMap.has(v)) {
      let svcName = match cx.reqMap.lookup(v) { Some(x) => x, None => "" }
      let decl = find_msig(svc_of(cx, svcName), meth, 0)
      if (decl.isAsync) { a2 = reach_set_req(a) }
    }
  }
  return reach_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr, a2)
}

// The handle twin of `reach_reqcall` (roadmap item 98): `h.<key>.<op>(…)` on a
// spawn handle whose provision op is `async` is a suspension exactly as a
// required-key async op is, so an arrow reaching one carries a colour its sync
// type does not declare. Without this the arrow was admitted and the py tier
// handed its caller an unawaited coroutine.
fn reach_handlecall(inner: FieldN, op: Str, args: List[Expr], tg: Expr,
                    cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  return match inner.target {
    Var(h) => (cr && cx.handles.has(h))
      ? reach_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr,
                    handle_msig(h, inner.name, op, cx).isAsync
                      ? reach_set_req(a) : a)
      : reach_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr, reach_scan(tg, cx, cr, a)),
    _ => reach_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr, reach_scan(tg, cx, cr, a)),
  }
}

// The argument list of a call to `n` while measuring async REACH: an arrow at
// one of `n`'s `Async[T]` slots is the coerced (async-stamped) value, and the
// reference's `_arrow_reaches_async` walk prunes at an async-flagged arrow —
// its suspension is its own, awaited through the colored type, not the
// constructing body's. Every other argument is walked. (Closing item 186's
// residual coloring approximation: without the prune an outer sync arrow is
// falsely reported as reaching async through a nested coerced one.)
fn reach_args(args: List[Expr], i: Int, asl: List[Int], cx: Ctx, cr: Bool,
              a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  if (i >= args.length()) { return a }
  if (contains_int(asl, i) && is_arrow(args[i])) {
    return reach_args(args, i + 1, asl, cx, cr, a)
  }
  return reach_args(args, i + 1, asl, cx, cr, reach_scan(args[i], cx, cr, a))
}

fn reach_call(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  return match tg {
    Field(fl) => match fl.target {
      Var(v)       => reach_reqcall(v, fl.name, args, cx, cr, a),
      Field(inner) => reach_handlecall(inner, fl.name, args, tg, cx, cr, a),
      _            => reach_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr, reach_scan(tg, cx, cr, a)),
    },
    Var(n)  => reach_args(args, 0,
                          match cx.fnAsyncSlots.lookup(n) { Some(v) => v, None => [] },
                          cx, cr,
                          contains(cx.colored, n) ? reach_add_name(a, n) : a),
    _       => reach_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr, reach_scan(tg, cx, cr, a)),
  }
}

// Does one expression tree reach a suspension (a call of an async-colored name,
// or — when `cr` — a required-key async op)? Bare VALUE references are excluded:
// a first-class async value is the `passed_async` surface, refused at the method
// level, not here (matching `_calls_in`'s call-only reach, no `values`).
fn reach_scan(e: Expr, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool, a: ReachAcc) -> ReachAcc {
  return match e {
    IntLit(_)   => a,
    FloatLit(_) => a,
    StrLit(_)   => a,
    BoolLit(_)  => a,
    NullLit     => a,
    Var(_)      => a,
    Hole(_)     => a,
    Bad(_)      => a,
    Bin(b)      => reach_scan(b.r, cx, cr, reach_scan(b.l, cx, cr, a)),
    Un(u)       => reach_scan(u.e, cx, cr, a),
    Emit(u)     => reach_scan(u.e, cx, cr, a),
    Call(c)     => reach_call(c.target, c.args, cx, cr, a),
    Field(f)    => reach_scan(f.target, cx, cr, a),
    OptField(f) => reach_scan(f.target, cx, cr, a),
    OptCall(c)  => reach_call(c.target, c.args, cx, cr, a),
    Index(x)    => reach_scan(x.idx, cx, cr, reach_scan(x.target, cx, cr, a)),
    If(x)       => reach_scan(x.els, cx, cr,
                             reach_scan(x.then_, cx, cr, reach_scan(x.cond, cx, cr, a))),
    Rec(r)      => reach_inits(r.fields, 0, cx, cr, a),
    Lst(l)      => reach_exprs(l.items, 0, cx, cr, a),
    Arrow(ar)   => reach_scan(ar.body, cx, cr, a),
    Match(m)    => reach_arms(m.arms, 0, cx, cr, reach_scan(m.scrut, cx, cr, a)),
    Templ(t)    => reach_parts(t.parts, 0, cx, cr, a),
  }
}

fn or2(x: Str, y: Str) -> Str { return x != "" ? x : y }

fn leaky_provide_msg() -> Str {
  return "this arrow reaches an async operation, but its type carries no async color — the caller would receive an unawaited suspension (A1)"
}

fn leaky_pure_msg(nm: Str) -> Str {
  return "this arrow reaches async callable `".concat(nm)
    .concat("`, but its type carries no async color — the caller would receive an unawaited suspension (A1)")
}

fn leaky_exprs(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let r = leaky_scan(xs[i], false, cx, cr)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return leaky_exprs(xs, i + 1, cx, cr)
}

fn leaky_inits(xs: List[InitN], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let r = leaky_scan(xs[i].value, false, cx, cr)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return leaky_inits(xs, i + 1, cx, cr)
}

fn leaky_arms(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let r = leaky_scan(xs[i].body, false, cx, cr)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return leaky_arms(xs, i + 1, cx, cr)
}

fn leaky_parts(xs: List[PartN], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let r = leaky_scan(xs[i].e, false, cx, cr)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return leaky_parts(xs, i + 1, cx, cr)
}

// A non-coerced arrow whose body reaches async is the leak; a coerced one (in an
// `Async[T]` slot) is admitted. Either way the body is still scanned for a
// nested sync arrow that leaks (matching `_refuse_leaky_arrow`'s "walk below").
fn leaky_arrow_body(body: Expr, coerced: Bool, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (!coerced) {
    let r = reach_scan(body, cx, cr, reach_empty())
    if (cr) {
      if (r.names.length() > 0 || r.hasReq) { return leaky_provide_msg() }
    } else {
      if (r.names.length() > 0) { return leaky_pure_msg(sort_strs(r.names)[0]) }
    }
  }
  return leaky_scan(body, false, cx, cr)
}

// An arrow argument in an `Async[T]` slot of the callee is coerced (admitted);
// every other argument is scanned uncoerced.
fn leaky_args_coerced(args: List[Expr], i: Int, slots: List[Int], cx: Ctx,
                      cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (i >= args.length()) { return "" }
  let r = leaky_scan(args[i], contains_int(slots, i), cx, cr)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return leaky_args_coerced(args, i + 1, slots, cx, cr)
}

fn leaky_call(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  return match tg {
    Var(n) => leaky_args_coerced(args, 0,
                match cx.fnAsyncSlots.lookup(n) { Some(v) => v, None => [] }, cx, cr),
    _      => or2(leaky_scan(tg, false, cx, cr), leaky_exprs(args, 0, cx, cr)),
  }
}

fn leaky_scan(e: Expr, coerced: Bool, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  return match e {
    IntLit(_)   => "",
    FloatLit(_) => "",
    StrLit(_)   => "",
    BoolLit(_)  => "",
    NullLit     => "",
    Var(_)      => "",
    Hole(_)     => "",
    Bad(_)      => "",
    Bin(b)      => or2(leaky_scan(b.l, false, cx, cr), leaky_scan(b.r, false, cx, cr)),
    Un(u)       => leaky_scan(u.e, false, cx, cr),
    Emit(u)     => leaky_scan(u.e, false, cx, cr),
    Call(c)     => leaky_call(c.target, c.args, cx, cr),
    Field(f)    => leaky_scan(f.target, false, cx, cr),
    OptField(f) => leaky_scan(f.target, false, cx, cr),
    OptCall(c)  => leaky_call(c.target, c.args, cx, cr),
    Index(x)    => or2(leaky_scan(x.target, false, cx, cr), leaky_scan(x.idx, false, cx, cr)),
    If(x)       => or2(leaky_scan(x.cond, false, cx, cr),
                       or2(leaky_scan(x.then_, false, cx, cr), leaky_scan(x.els, false, cx, cr))),
    Rec(r)      => leaky_inits(r.fields, 0, cx, cr),
    Lst(l)      => leaky_exprs(l.items, 0, cx, cr),
    Arrow(ar)   => leaky_arrow_body(ar.body, coerced, cx, cr),
    Match(m)    => or2(leaky_scan(m.scrut, false, cx, cr), leaky_arms(m.arms, 0, cx, cr)),
    Templ(t)    => leaky_parts(t.parts, 0, cx, cr),
  }
}

fn leaky_body(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, cx: Ctx, cr: Bool) -> Str {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return "" }
  let r = leaky_scan(ss[i].e, false, cx, cr)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return leaky_body(ss, i + 1, cx, cr)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- module fns
//
// The module-fn TWIN of the two provide-method A1 value/leak refusals
// (lower.py's fn loop, run over every lowered module fn BEFORE any component):
//   * a first-class reference to an async callable as a VALUE — an arrow type
//     carries no color, so a suspension cannot be awaited through it (the exact
//     wording names the fn and the first sorted culprit);
//   * a sync-typed arrow in the fn body reaching an async callable
//     (`_refuse_leaky_pure_arrow`) — a pure fn has no required keys, so only the
//     named-callable reach applies (`checkReq = false`).
// The value refusal is checked before the arrow leak, per fn, in declaration
// order, exactly as the reference's single loop does.

fn col_exprs(xs: List[Expr], i: Int, names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return col_exprs(xs, i + 1, names, col_values(xs[i], names, acc))
}

fn col_inits(xs: List[InitN], i: Int, names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return col_inits(xs, i + 1, names, col_values(xs[i].value, names, acc))
}

fn col_arms(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int, names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return col_arms(xs, i + 1, names, col_values(xs[i].body, names, acc))
}

fn col_parts(xs: List[PartN], i: Int, names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return col_parts(xs, i + 1, names, col_values(xs[i].e, names, acc))
}

// The call HEAD var is the callee, not a flowing value (mirrors `_calls_in`,
// which records it in `found`, never `values`); a non-var head is walked.
fn col_call(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  let a2 = match tg { Var(_) => acc, _ => col_values(tg, names, acc) }
  return col_exprs(args, 0, names, a2)
}

// Named callables referenced as VALUES (mirrors `_calls_in(values=…)`).
// Async admission supplies the colored set; cache reach supplies all callables.
fn col_values(e: Expr, names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  return match e {
    Var(n)      => contains(names, n) ? union_into(acc, [n]) : acc,
    IntLit(_)   => acc,
    FloatLit(_) => acc,
    StrLit(_)   => acc,
    BoolLit(_)  => acc,
    NullLit     => acc,
    Hole(_)     => acc,
    Bad(_)      => acc,
    Bin(b)      => col_values(b.r, names, col_values(b.l, names, acc)),
    Un(u)       => col_values(u.e, names, acc),
    Emit(u)     => col_values(u.e, names, acc),
    Call(c)     => col_call(c.target, c.args, names, acc),
    Field(f)    => col_values(f.target, names, acc),
    OptField(f) => col_values(f.target, names, acc),
    OptCall(c)  => col_call(c.target, c.args, names, acc),
    Index(x)    => col_values(x.idx, names, col_values(x.target, names, acc)),
    If(x)       => col_values(x.els, names, col_values(x.then_, names, col_values(x.cond, names, acc))),
    Rec(r)      => col_inits(r.fields, 0, names, acc),
    Lst(l)      => col_exprs(l.items, 0, names, acc),
    Arrow(ar)   => col_values(ar.body, names, acc),
    Match(m)    => col_arms(m.arms, 0, names, col_values(m.scrut, names, acc)),
    Templ(t)    => col_parts(t.parts, 0, names, acc),
  }
}

fn col_values_body(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, names: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return acc }
  return col_values_body(ss, i + 1, names, col_values(ss[i].e, names, acc))
}

// The module-fn value + leaky-arrow verdict for one fn (lower.py's fn loop).
// "" admits; else a tagged A1 refusal. An extern has an empty body, so both
// checks are no-ops on it.
// ------------------------------------ host acquisition in a reachable fn body
//
// `_refuse_host_acquire_in_component_reachable_fn`. A `fn` body has no
// acquisition bracket, so a host acquire verb in one registers its release
// nowhere. What makes that a soundness break rather than a style question is
// the CALLER: a component activation promises no residue — the teardown
// accumulator runs every registered inverse on unload and on abort — and a
// helper the activation calls acquires inside that promise while contributing
// nothing to it.
//
// The reach qualifier is the invariant, not a carve-out. A `pub fn` no
// component reaches is a library entry point whose lifecycle belongs to the
// foreign caller that invoked it, and revl promises nothing about it. So the
// seed is the fns a component body NAMES, closed over the fn call graph, and
// the scan runs over that closure in sorted name order (the reference's
// `for name in sorted(reached)`).

fn fnd_body(fns: List[FnD], n: Str, i: Int) -> List[Stmt] {
  if (i >= fns.length()) { return [] }
  if (fns[i].name == n) { return fns[i].body }
  return fnd_body(fns, n, i + 1)
}

fn fnd_callees(fns: List[FnD], n: Str, i: Int) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= fns.length()) { return [] }
  if (fns[i].name == n) { return fns[i].callees }
  return fnd_callees(fns, n, i + 1)
}

fn fnd_has(fns: List[FnD], n: Str, i: Int) -> Bool {
  if (i >= fns.length()) { return false }
  if (fns[i].name == n) { return true }
  return fnd_has(fns, n, i + 1)
}

// The bare-call names a component's setup and provide-method bodies reach.
fn comp_called(comp: CompD, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out = body_callees(comp.setup, 0, acc)
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      out = body_callees(pv.methods[mi].body, 0, out)
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn comps_called(comps: List[CompD], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= comps.length()) { return acc }
  return comps_called(comps, i + 1, comp_called(comps[i], acc))
}

// The fn names a component reaches, transitively. A worklist over `callees`,
// bounded by the fn count: each round can only add a name, and a name is added
// once.
fn reached_fns(fns: List[FnD], seed: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < seed.length()) {
    if (fnd_has(fns, seed[i], 0) && !contains(out, seed[i])) {
      out = out.push(seed[i])
    }
    i += 1
  }
  var changed = true
  while (changed) {
    changed = false
    var j = 0
    while (j < out.length()) {
      let cs = fnd_callees(fns, out[j], 0)
      var k = 0
      while (k < cs.length()) {
        if (fnd_has(fns, cs[k], 0) && !contains(out, cs[k])) {
          out = out.push(cs[k])
          changed = true
        }
        k += 1
      }
      j += 1
    }
  }
  return out
}

// The first host acquire call in an expression, spelled `Root.verb` ("" when
// there is none). Every position counts here: a `fn` body has no bracket at
// all, so there is nothing to exempt.
fn acq_exprs(xs: List[Expr], i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let h = acq_in(xs[i])
  if (h != "") { return h }
  return acq_exprs(xs, i + 1)
}

fn acq_inits(xs: List[InitN], i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let h = acq_in(xs[i].value)
  if (h != "") { return h }
  return acq_inits(xs, i + 1)
}

fn acq_arms(xs: List[ArmN], i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let h = acq_in(xs[i].body)
  if (h != "") { return h }
  return acq_arms(xs, i + 1)
}

fn acq_parts(xs: List[PartN], i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return "" }
  let h = acq_in(xs[i].e)
  if (h != "") { return h }
  return acq_parts(xs, i + 1)
}

fn acq_call(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr]) -> Str {
  let here = match tg {
    Field(fl) => match fl.target {
      Var(v) => host_acquire_release(v, fl.name) != ""
        ? v.concat(".").concat(fl.name) : "",
      _ => "",
    },
    _ => "",
  }
  if (here != "") { return here }
  return or2(acq_in(tg), acq_exprs(args, 0))
}

fn acq_in(e: Expr) -> Str {
  return match e {
    IntLit(_)   => "",
    FloatLit(_) => "",
    StrLit(_)   => "",
    BoolLit(_)  => "",
    NullLit     => "",
    Var(_)      => "",
    Hole(_)     => "",
    Bad(_)      => "",
    Bin(b)      => or2(acq_in(b.l), acq_in(b.r)),
    Un(u)       => acq_in(u.e),
    Emit(u)     => acq_in(u.e),
    Call(c)     => acq_call(c.target, c.args),
    Field(f)    => acq_in(f.target),
    OptField(f) => acq_in(f.target),
    OptCall(c)  => or2(acq_in(c.target), acq_exprs(c.args, 0)),
    Index(x)    => or2(acq_in(x.target), acq_in(x.idx)),
    If(x)       => or2(acq_in(x.cond), or2(acq_in(x.then_), acq_in(x.els))),
    Rec(r)      => acq_inits(r.fields, 0),
    Lst(l)      => acq_exprs(l.items, 0),
    Arrow(ar)   => acq_in(ar.body),
    Match(m)    => or2(acq_in(m.scrut), acq_arms(m.arms, 0)),
    Templ(t)    => acq_parts(t.parts, 0),
  }
}

fn acq_body(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return "" }
  let h = acq_in(ss[i].e)
  if (h != "") { return h }
  return acq_body(ss, i + 1)
}

fn check_fn_acquire(fns: List[FnD], reached: List[Str], i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= reached.length()) { return "" }
  let n = reached[i]
  let hit = acq_body(fnd_body(fns, n, 0), 0)
  if (hit != "") {
    let dot = hit.indexOf(".")
    return tagged("G4", host_acquire_msg(
      hit.slice(0, dot), hit.slice(dot + 1, hit.length()),
      host_acquire_release(hit.slice(0, dot), hit.slice(dot + 1, hit.length())),
      "`fn ".concat(n).concat("`, which a component body reaches")))
  }
  return check_fn_acquire(fns, reached, i + 1)
}

fn check_reachable_fn_acquire(pg: Prog) -> Str {
  if (pg.comps.length() == 0 || pg.fns.length() == 0) { return "" }
  let seed = comps_called(pg.comps, 0, [])
  return check_fn_acquire(pg.fns, sort_strs(reached_fns(pg.fns, seed)), 0)
}

fn fn_a1_verdict(f: FnD, base: Ctx) -> Str {
  let vals = sort_strs(col_values_body(f.body, 0, base.colored, []))
  if (vals.length() > 0) {
    return tagged("A1", "function `".concat(f.name)
      .concat("` uses async callable `").concat(vals[0])
      .concat("` as a function value, but an async callable has no arrow type"))
  }
  let leak = leaky_body(f.body, 0, base, false)
  if (leak != "") { return tagged("A1", leak) }
  return ""
}

fn check_module_fns(fns: List[FnD], i: Int, base: Ctx) -> Str {
  if (i >= fns.length()) { return "" }
  let v = fn_a1_verdict(fns[i], base)
  if (v != "") { return v }
  return check_module_fns(fns, i + 1, base)
}

fn check_cache_fns(fns: List[FnD]) -> Str {
  var has_cache = false
  var n = 0
  while (n < fns.length()) {
    if (fns[n].cachePure) { has_cache = true }
    n += 1
  }
  if (!has_cache) { return "" }
  let caps = emit_caps_reach(fns, true)
  var i = 0
  while (i < fns.length()) {
    let f = fns[i]
    let crossed = sort_strs(caps_of(caps, f.name))
    if (f.cachePure && crossed.length() > 0) {
      return tagged("G4", "the reach of fn `".concat(f.name).concat("` crosses ")
        .concat(crossed[0]).concat(": a crossing result is not pure"))
    }
    i += 1
  }
  return ""
}

// G4 + A1 over one component. "" = admitted, else a tagged refusal.
fn check_component(comp: CompD, cx: Ctx) -> Str {
  // setup gets G4/A1/G1 too: an undeclared access or unmarked emission in
  // setup is refused exactly as in a provide body, bare values included (see
  // `var_check`). There is no longer a per-body-kind context: the two bodies
  // resolve names identically, so a provide method walks under the very same
  // `cx`. The `plainBody` flag that used to distinguish them is gone rather
  // than left set-and-unread, so nothing can quietly gate a check on it again.
  // provision provenance is per BODY: setup's aliases are visible to every
  // provide method (the reference lowers setup first and restores to that map
  // after each method), a method's own are not visible to the next one.
  let setupAlias = alias_in(comp.setup, 0, cx.handles, Map.empty())
  let scx = ctx_alias(cx, setupAlias)
  let sa = walk_stmts(comp.setup, 0, scx, empty_ac())
  if (sa.msg != "") { return tagged(sa.tag, sa.msg) }
  let pcx = cx
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    let dsvc = svc_of(cx, pv.svcName)
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      let pm = pv.methods[mi]
      let decl = find_msig(dsvc, pm.name, 0)
      // signature parity FIRST (lower.py `method.async_ != decl.async_`), before
      // the body is walked: an `async fn` op must be implemented by an `async fn`
      // method, and vice-versa.
      if (decl.name != "" && pm.isAsync != decl.isAsync) {
        return tagged("A1", async_sig_msg(pv.key, pm.name, pv.svcName,
                                          pm.isAsync, decl.isAsync))
      }
      let mcx = ctx_alias(pcx, alias_in(pm.body, 0, pcx.handles, setupAlias))
      let a = walk_stmts(pm.body, 0, mcx, empty_ac())
      if (a.msg != "") { return tagged(a.tag, a.msg) }
      // `await` outside a component body (lower.py's method-lowering AwaitStmt
      // check): an await in a SYNC provide method has no in-flight window; an
      // async method (or an activation body) may await. Fires during body
      // lowering, before the G4/A1 provider verdicts.
      if (decl.name != "" && !decl.isAsync && has_await(pm.body, 0)) {
        return tagged("A1", "`await` is only allowed in a component body")
      }
      if (decl.name != "") {
        let g4 = g4_verdict(pv.svcName, pm.name, decl, a)
        if (g4 != "") { return tagged("G4", g4) }
        let pa = passed_async_verdict(pv.svcName, pm.name, a, cx)
        if (pa != "") { return tagged("A1", pa) }
        let a1 = a1_verdict(pv.svcName, pm.name, decl, a, cx)
        if (a1 != "") { return tagged("A1", a1) }
      }
      // the arrow-color LEAK (lower.py's `_refuse_leaky_arrow`) runs last and
      // for EVERY method, sync or async: a sync-typed (non-coerced) arrow whose
      // body reaches async has no color to carry the suspension. A coerced arrow
      // (in an `Async[T]` slot) is admitted.
      let leak = leaky_body(pm.body, 0, pcx, true)
      if (leak != "") { return tagged("A1", leak) }
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  // the setup/activation async-reach refusal runs after every provide method
  // (the reference checks it once the whole component is lowered).
  let su = setup_async_verdict(comp, sa, cx)
  if (su != "") { return tagged("A1", su) }
  return ""
}

// ============================================================ spawn boundary
//
// The instance dimension (docs/design-v2-instances.md, lower.py's `_check_spawn_*`).
// A `spawn <Comp> with { … }` acquisition instantiates a component as a runtime
// TEMPLATE — excluded from the static G2/G3 composition (decision 5/6) — while
// the spawner incurs two boundary obligations, decided after every component is
// lowered:
//
//   * G4/G6 spawn-emission bounds (`_check_spawn_emission_bounds`, decision 8):
//     a spawn inside a PROVIDE method whose service op is plain, or `emission[caps]`,
//     may not spawn a target whose emission surface exceeds that bound — an
//     emission cannot escape its clause by moving into a child.
//
//   * capability attenuation (`_check_spawn_attenuation`, item 66): a spawn in an
//     ACTIVATION (setup) body may only grant a child capabilities its spawner
//     HOLDS (its `requires` keys plus what its own body emits) — a spawn narrows
//     lineage, never widens it, so a supervisor cannot amplify authority.
//
// A third, per-call obligation — an unmarked emission reached through a spawn
// handle (`h.<key>.<op>(…)`) — is enforced inline during the body walk
// (`handle_emit`, mirroring `_instance_get_call`), so it surfaces with the
// per-component checks, before these two composition-time bounds.
//
// All three surfaces reduce to the SAME emission-capability machinery G4 already
// runs, so they carry `code="G4"`; the two below run in the reference's order
// (bounds, then attenuation) and only when the program actually spawns.

type SpawnEdge = { parent: Str, child: Str }

// Every spawn target across the program (setup + provide-method bodies): the
// templates. A template never joins the static G2/G3 table.
fn stmt_spawns(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return acc }
  let s = ss[i]
  let hit = s.spawnTarget != "" && s.kind == "effect"
  return stmt_spawns(ss, i + 1, hit ? acc.push(s.spawnTarget) : acc)
}

fn comp_all_spawns(comp: CompD) -> List[Str] {
  var out = stmt_spawns(comp.setup, 0, [])
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      out = append_stmts_str(out, stmt_spawns(pv.methods[mi].body, 0, []))
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn append_stmts_str(a: List[Str], b: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out = a
  var i = 0
  while (i < b.length()) {
    out = out.push(b[i])
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn spawn_templates(comps: List[CompD]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < comps.length()) {
    out = union_into(out, comp_all_spawns(comps[i]))
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn spawn_edges(comps: List[CompD]) -> List[SpawnEdge] {
  var out: List[SpawnEdge] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < comps.length()) {
    let tgts = comp_all_spawns(comps[i])
    var k = 0
    while (k < tgts.length()) {
      out = out.push({ parent: comps[i].name, child: tgts[k] })
      k += 1
    }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// The capability an `emit` statement crosses (lower.py's `_collect_emit_caps`):
// a required-service emission names its key (`req.op` -> the key); every other
// emission — a host call, a handle emission — is the unnameable `*` boundary.
fn emit_cap(e: Expr, reqSet: List[Str]) -> Str {
  return match e {
    Call(c) => match c.target {
      Field(fl) => match fl.target {
        Var(v) => contains(reqSet, v) ? v : "*",
        _      => "*",
      },
      _ => "*",
    },
    _ => "*",
  }
}

// The caps a component's OWN body emits (its `emit` statements) — the exact
// reached surface attenuation measures (`_spawn_reached_surface`). Scans setup
// and every provide-method body.
fn emit_caps_in(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, reqSet: List[Str], acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return acc }
  let s = ss[i]
  let a2 = s.kind == "emit" ? union_into(acc, [emit_cap(s.e, reqSet)]) : acc
  return emit_caps_in(ss, i + 1, reqSet, a2)
}

fn own_emit_caps(comp: CompD, reqSet: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out = emit_caps_in(comp.setup, 0, reqSet, [])
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      out = emit_caps_in(pv.methods[mi].body, 0, reqSet, out)
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return out
}

// The declaration-based emission surface (`_spawn_emission_surface`, decision 8):
// every emission method of the services the component provides (uncapped -> `*`,
// else its caps), plus its own emit caps. A conservative over-approximation.
fn svc_emission_caps(comp: CompD, cx: Ctx, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  var out = acc
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let svc = svc_of(cx, comp.provs[pi].svcName)
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < svc.methods.length()) {
      let m = svc.methods[mi]
      if (m.isEm) {
        out = m.caps.length() == 0 ? union_into(out, ["*"]) : union_into(out, m.caps)
      }
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn emis_surface_of(comp: CompD, cx: Ctx, reqSet: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  return svc_emission_caps(comp, cx, own_emit_caps(comp, reqSet))
}

// The req keys of a component — the boundaries it holds by wiring.
fn comp_req_keys(comp: CompD) -> List[Str] {
  return bind_names(comp.reqMap, 0, [])
}

type SurfMap = Map[Str, List[Str]]

fn surf_get(m: SurfMap, k: Str) -> List[Str] {
  return match m.lookup(k) { Some(v) => v, None => [] }
}

// Fold the spawn graph into a base surface until fixed: after this a component's
// set includes everything its transitive spawned children can (`_spawn_surface_closure`).
fn surf_closure(base: SurfMap, edges: List[SpawnEdge]) -> SurfMap {
  var surf = base
  var changed = true
  while (changed) {
    changed = false
    var i = 0
    while (i < edges.length()) {
      let e = edges[i]
      let before = surf_get(surf, e.parent)
      let merged = union_into(before, surf_get(surf, e.child))
      if (merged.length() > before.length()) {
        surf = surf.set(e.parent, merged)
        changed = true
      }
      i += 1
    }
  }
  return surf
}

// Render an offending capability list (`*` -> an unnameable host boundary),
// exactly as the capped-emission and attenuation diagnostics spell it.
fn offending_join(xs: List[Str]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (i > 0) { out = out.concat(", ") }
    if (xs[i] == "*") { out = out.concat("an unnameable host boundary") }
    else { out = out.concat("`").concat(xs[i]).concat("`") }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// The reached (own-emit) surface, per component — the base attenuation closes.
fn reach_surface_map(comps: List[CompD]) -> SurfMap {
  var m: SurfMap = Map.empty()
  var i = 0
  while (i < comps.length()) {
    let c = comps[i]
    m = m.set(c.name, own_emit_caps(c, comp_req_keys(c)))
    i += 1
  }
  return m
}

// The declaration-based emission surface, per component — the base bounds closes.
fn emis_surface_map(comps: List[CompD], cx: Ctx) -> SurfMap {
  var m: SurfMap = Map.empty()
  var i = 0
  while (i < comps.length()) {
    let c = comps[i]
    m = m.set(c.name, emis_surface_of(c, cx, comp_req_keys(c)))
    i += 1
  }
  return m
}

// G4/G6 spawn-emission bounds. For every provide-method spawn, the target's
// (closed) emission surface must fit the method's declared bound.
fn bounds_method(pv: Provide, m: ProvM, cx: Ctx, closed: SurfMap) -> Str {
  let decl = find_msig(svc_of(cx, pv.svcName), m.name, 0)
  if (decl.name == "") { return "" }
  let where = pv.svcName.concat(".").concat(m.name)
  let tgts = stmt_spawns(m.body, 0, [])
  var i = 0
  while (i < tgts.length()) {
    let tsurf = surf_get(closed, tgts[i])
    if (tsurf.length() > 0) {
      if (!decl.isEm) {
        return "`".concat(where)
          .concat("` is declared plain, but it spawns `").concat(tgts[i])
          .concat("`, which emits through ").concat(quote_join(sort_strs(tsurf)))
      }
      if (decl.caps.length() > 0) {
        let extra = sort_strs(minus_list(tsurf, decl.caps))
        if (extra.length() > 0) {
          let declared = comma_join(decl.caps)
          let dstr = declared == "" ? "no capabilities" : declared
          return "`".concat(where)
            .concat("` is declared `emission[").concat(dstr)
            .concat("]`, but it spawns `").concat(tgts[i])
            .concat("`, which emits through ").concat(offending_join(extra))
        }
      }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  return ""
}

fn bounds_comp(comp: CompD, cx: Ctx, closed: SurfMap) -> Str {
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      let r = bounds_method(pv, pv.methods[mi], cx, closed)
      if (r != "") { return r }
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return ""
}

fn check_spawn_bounds(comps: List[CompD], ci: Int, cx: Ctx, closed: SurfMap) -> Str {
  if (ci >= comps.length()) { return "" }
  let r = bounds_comp(comps[ci], cx, closed)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return check_spawn_bounds(comps, ci + 1, cx, closed)
}

// Capability attenuation. For every ACTIVATION (setup) spawn, the child's
// (closed) reachable set must be a subset of what the spawner HOLDS.
fn atten_comp(comp: CompD, base: SurfMap, closed: SurfMap) -> Str {
  let held = union_into(comp_req_keys(comp), surf_get(base, comp.name))
  let tgts = stmt_spawns(comp.setup, 0, [])
  var i = 0
  while (i < tgts.length()) {
    let child = tgts[i]
    let extra = sort_strs(minus_list(surf_get(closed, child), held))
    if (extra.length() > 0) {
      let hs = sort_strs(held)
      let heldStr = hs.length() == 0 ? "no capabilities" : quote_join(hs)
      return "`".concat(comp.name).concat("` spawns `").concat(child)
        .concat("`, granting it ").concat(offending_join(extra))
        .concat(", but `").concat(comp.name).concat("` holds only ").concat(heldStr)
        .concat(" — a spawn may narrow a child's capabilities, never widen them")
    }
    i += 1
  }
  return ""
}

fn check_spawn_atten(comps: List[CompD], ci: Int, base: SurfMap, closed: SurfMap) -> Str {
  if (ci >= comps.length()) { return "" }
  let r = atten_comp(comps[ci], base, closed)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return check_spawn_atten(comps, ci + 1, base, closed)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- spawn forms
//
// Two code-less spawn refusals decided as a spawn statement lowers (before the
// composition-time bounds/attenuation): the bind-to-a-handle rule (decision 2 —
// an unbound `effect spawn` has no handle to name its teardown) and the
// unknown-spawn-target check (`_lower_spawn` — a spawn names a component of this
// composition). Both carry no `code` in the reference, so the gate tags them
// "SPAWN" and the oracle matches them by message text. bind-to-a-handle is
// checked first (it fires at the statement, before `_lower_spawn` runs), so a
// bare spawn of an unknown component is still the handle refusal.

fn comp_names(comps: List[CompD], i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= comps.length()) { return acc }
  return comp_names(comps, i + 1, acc.push(comps[i].name))
}

fn spawn_form_stmt(s: Stmt, names: List[Str]) -> Str {
  if (s.kind == "spawn_unbound") {
    return tagged("SPAWN",
      "`spawn` must be bound to a handle: `let s = effect spawn "
        .concat(s.spawnTarget).concat(" … undo s.dispose()`"))
  }
  if (s.spawnTarget != "" && s.kind == "effect" && !contains(names, s.spawnTarget)) {
    return tagged("SPAWN",
      "`spawn ".concat(s.spawnTarget).concat("` names an unknown component"))
  }
  return ""
}

fn spawn_form_stmts(ss: List[Stmt], i: Int, names: List[Str]) -> Str {
  if (i >= ss.length()) { return "" }
  let r = spawn_form_stmt(ss[i], names)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return spawn_form_stmts(ss, i + 1, names)
}

fn spawn_form_comp(comp: CompD, names: List[Str]) -> Str {
  let sv = spawn_form_stmts(comp.setup, 0, names)
  if (sv != "") { return sv }
  var pi = 0
  while (pi < comp.provs.length()) {
    let pv = comp.provs[pi]
    var mi = 0
    while (mi < pv.methods.length()) {
      let r = spawn_form_stmts(pv.methods[mi].body, 0, names)
      if (r != "") { return r }
      mi += 1
    }
    pi += 1
  }
  return ""
}

// The spawn-boundary gate: bounds then attenuation, both `code="G4"`, run only
// when the program spawns (empty edge list -> no obligation, as in the reference).
fn check_spawn(pg: Prog, cx: Ctx) -> Str {
  let edges = spawn_edges(pg.comps)
  if (edges.length() == 0) { return "" }
  let emisClosed = surf_closure(emis_surface_map(pg.comps, cx), edges)
  let bv = check_spawn_bounds(pg.comps, 0, cx, emisClosed)
  if (bv != "") { return tagged("G4", bv) }
  let reachBase = reach_surface_map(pg.comps)
  let reachClosed = surf_closure(reachBase, edges)
  let av = check_spawn_atten(pg.comps, 0, reachBase, reachClosed)
  if (av != "") { return tagged("G4", av) }
  return ""
}

// The static composition excludes spawn templates (decision 5/6): a template is
// instantiated into a fresh per-instance realm, so it never enters G2/G3.
fn non_template_comps(comps: List[CompD], templates: List[Str]) -> List[CompD] {
  var out: List[CompD] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < comps.length()) {
    if (!contains(templates, comps[i].name)) { out = out.push(comps[i]) }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// ================================================================ link (G2/G3)
//
// The composition-time gate, mirroring lower.py's `_link`. Provision
// disjointness is per-(key, realm): the SAME key in two DIFFERENT realms is
// the multi-tenancy feature (no conflict), while two providers in the SAME
// realm conflict — and the realm is named in the diagnostic only when it isn't
// the shared one, so the v1 wording is byte-identical. Dependency acyclicity
// (G3) then runs over provider -> consumer edges, where an edge exists only
// when the consumer resolves a required key in the SAME realm its provider
// provides it — realm separation legitimately breaks a would-be cycle.

// A resolved provider entry: which component provides `key` in realm `rlm`.
type Prov3 = { key: Str, rlm: Str, comp: Str }

fn find_provider(ps: List[Prov3], key: Str, rlm: Str, i: Int) -> Str {
  if (i >= ps.length()) { return "" }
  if (ps[i].key == key && ps[i].rlm == rlm) { return ps[i].comp }
  return find_provider(ps, key, rlm, i + 1)
}

type LinkR = { provs: List[Prov3], msg: Str }

fn realm_where(rlm: Str) -> Str {
  if (rlm == "") { return "" }
  return " in realm `".concat(rlm).concat("`")
}

fn scan_prov_keys(comp: CompD, pks: List[ProvKey], ki: Int,
                  acc: List[Prov3]) -> LinkR {
  if (ki >= pks.length()) { return { provs: acc, msg: "" } }
  let pk = pks[ki]
  let first = find_provider(acc, pk.key, pk.rlm, 0)
  if (first != "") {
    return { provs: acc,
             msg: "provision conflict: key `".concat(pk.key).concat("`")
               .concat(realm_where(pk.rlm)).concat(" is provided by both ")
               .concat(first).concat(" and ").concat(comp.name).concat(" (G2)") }
  }
  return scan_prov_keys(comp, pks, ki + 1,
                        acc.push({ key: pk.key, rlm: pk.rlm, comp: comp.name }))
}

fn g2_scan(comps: List[CompD], ci: Int, provs: List[Prov3]) -> LinkR {
  if (ci >= comps.length()) { return { provs: provs, msg: "" } }
  let r = scan_prov_keys(comps[ci], comps[ci].provKeys, 0, provs)
  if (r.msg != "") { return r }
  return g2_scan(comps, ci + 1, r.provs)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- G3 edges

type EdgeR = { succ: Map[Str, List[Str]], msg: Str }

fn succ_of(succ: Map[Str, List[Str]], name: Str) -> List[Str] {
  return match succ.lookup(name) { Some(v) => v, None => [] }
}

fn add_edge(succ: Map[Str, List[Str]], src: Str, dst: Str) -> Map[Str, List[Str]] {
  return succ.set(src, succ_of(succ, src).push(dst))
}

// One edge per ROUTED realm (item 162): every backend provider must be active
// before the consumer that routes to it (loadOrder), and each leg can still
// surface in a cycle trace by its key. A realm with no provider is already the
// `route_check` refusal above, so a missing leg simply adds no edge here.
fn route_edges(rlms: List[Str], ri: Int, key: Str, cname: Str,
               provs: List[Prov3], succ: Map[Str, List[Str]]) -> Map[Str, List[Str]] {
  if (ri >= rlms.length()) { return succ }
  let provider = find_provider(provs, key, rlms[ri], 0)
  if (provider == "" || provider == cname) {
    return route_edges(rlms, ri + 1, key, cname, provs, succ)
  }
  return route_edges(rlms, ri + 1, key, cname, provs,
                     add_edge(succ, provider, cname))
}

fn scan_reqs(comp: CompD, provs: List[Prov3], keys: List[Str], ki: Int,
             succ: Map[Str, List[Str]]) -> EdgeR {
  if (ki >= keys.length()) { return { succ: succ, msg: "" } }
  let key = keys[ki]
  // a ROUTED key resolves per-realm, not through the single-realm table — a
  // stray shared-realm provider must not shadow the route's own targets, and
  // the G3 self-provision check does not apply (a route targets a *required*
  // key, never this component's own provision).
  if (routes_has(comp.routes, key, 0)) {
    return scan_reqs(comp, provs, keys, ki + 1,
                     route_edges(route_of(comp.routes, key, 0).rlms, 0, key,
                                 comp.name, provs, succ))
  }
  let provider = find_provider(provs, key, realm_of(comp.iso, key, 0), 0)
  if (provider == comp.name) {
    return { succ: succ,
             msg: "component ".concat(comp.name)
               .concat(" requires a key it provides itself (`").concat(key)
               .concat("`) (G3)") }
  }
  if (provider != "") {
    return scan_reqs(comp, provs, keys, ki + 1, add_edge(succ, provider, comp.name))
  }
  return scan_reqs(comp, provs, keys, ki + 1, succ)
}

fn build_edges(comps: List[CompD], ci: Int, provs: List[Prov3],
               succ: Map[Str, List[Str]]) -> EdgeR {
  if (ci >= comps.length()) { return { succ: succ, msg: "" } }
  let comp = comps[ci]
  let keys = sort_strs(bind_names(comp.reqMap, 0, []))
  let r = scan_reqs(comp, provs, keys, 0, succ)
  if (r.msg != "") { return r }
  return build_edges(comps, ci + 1, provs, r.succ)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- G3 DFS
//
// The reference's colored DFS: state 1 = on the stack (gray), 2 = done. A back
// edge to a gray node closes a cycle; the reported path is the stack slice from
// that node onward, with the node repeated to show the edge that shuts the loop
// (`stack[stack.index(succ):] + [succ]`). Entries are visited in composition
// order; each node's successors in the order the edges were added — the two
// orderings that fix which components the diagnostic names.

type DfsRes = { state: Map[Str, Int], cycle: List[Str] }

fn mget_int(m: Map[Str, Int], k: Str) -> Int {
  return match m.lookup(k) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 }
}

fn sub_from(xs: List[Str], idx: Int, i: Int, acc: List[Str]) -> List[Str] {
  if (i >= xs.length()) { return acc }
  return sub_from(xs, idx, i + 1, i >= idx ? acc.push(xs[i]) : acc)
}

fn dfs_visit(succ: Map[Str, List[Str]], name: Str, state: Map[Str, Int],
             stack: List[Str]) -> DfsRes {
  return dfs_iter(succ, name, stack.push(name), succ_of(succ, name), 0,
                  state.set(name, 1))
}

fn dfs_iter(succ: Map[Str, List[Str]], name: Str, stack2: List[Str],
            list: List[Str], j: Int, state: Map[Str, Int]) -> DfsRes {
  if (j >= list.length()) { return { state: state.set(name, 2), cycle: [] } }
  let s = list[j]
  let st = mget_int(state, s)
  if (st == 1) {
    let idx = stack2.indexOf(s)
    return { state: state, cycle: sub_from(stack2, idx, 0, []).push(s) }
  }
  if (st == 0) {
    let r = dfs_visit(succ, s, state, stack2)
    if (r.cycle.length() > 0) { return r }
    return dfs_iter(succ, name, stack2, list, j + 1, r.state)
  }
  return dfs_iter(succ, name, stack2, list, j + 1, state)
}

fn find_cycle(comps: List[CompD], ci: Int, succ: Map[Str, List[Str]],
              state: Map[Str, Int]) -> List[Str] {
  if (ci >= comps.length()) { return [] }
  let name = comps[ci].name
  if (mget_int(state, name) == 0) {
    let r = dfs_visit(succ, name, state, [])
    if (r.cycle.length() > 0) { return r.cycle }
    return find_cycle(comps, ci + 1, succ, r.state)
  }
  return find_cycle(comps, ci + 1, succ, state)
}

fn arrow_join(xs: List[Str]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    if (i > 0) { out = out.concat(" -> ") }
    out = out.concat(xs[i])
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------- multi-realm bind (item 162)
//
// The link-time half of multi-realm routing, mirroring lower.py's `_link` loop
// over `entry["routes"]`: a `realms(...)` route binds the key across N named
// realms and the consumer routes across them at runtime, so EVERY named realm
// must have a provider of the key — a route naming a realm with no provider
// would dangle at that leg. This does not relax G2 (one provider per
// (key, realm) is still enforced above); it is the dual existence check on the
// consumption side, read off the SAME per-(key, realm) table.

fn route_missing_msg(key: Str, cname: Str, rlm: Str) -> Str {
  return "multi-realm bind of `".concat(key).concat("` in ").concat(cname)
    .concat(" names realm `").concat(rlm).concat("`, but no component provides `")
    .concat(key).concat("` in realm `").concat(rlm)
    .concat("` (item 162: every routed realm needs a provider)")
}

fn route_realm_scan(rlms: List[Str], ri: Int, key: Str, cname: Str,
                    provs: List[Prov3]) -> Str {
  if (ri >= rlms.length()) { return "" }
  if (find_provider(provs, key, rlms[ri], 0) == "") {
    return route_missing_msg(key, cname, rlms[ri])
  }
  return route_realm_scan(rlms, ri + 1, key, cname, provs)
}

fn route_comp_scan(rts: List[Route], i: Int, cname: Str,
                   provs: List[Prov3]) -> Str {
  if (i >= rts.length()) { return "" }
  let r = route_realm_scan(rts[i].rlms, 0, rts[i].key, cname, provs)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return route_comp_scan(rts, i + 1, cname, provs)
}

fn route_scan(comps: List[CompD], ci: Int, provs: List[Prov3]) -> Str {
  if (ci >= comps.length()) { return "" }
  let r = route_comp_scan(comps[ci].routes, 0, comps[ci].name, provs)
  if (r != "") { return r }
  return route_scan(comps, ci + 1, provs)
}

fn link_g2_g3(pg: Prog) -> Str {
  // spawn templates are runtime instances, not static composition members — they
  // take no place in the G2/G3 table or loadOrder (decision 5/6).
  let live = non_template_comps(pg.comps, spawn_templates(pg.comps))
  let g2 = g2_scan(live, 0, [])
  if (g2.msg != "") { return tagged("G2", g2.msg) }
  // the multi-realm bind verification (item 162) runs between G2 and the edges,
  // exactly where the reference runs it: the dual EXISTENCE check on the
  // consumption side, over the same per-(key, realm) provider table.
  let rv = route_scan(live, 0, g2.provs)
  if (rv != "") { return tagged("ROUTE", rv) }
  let edges = build_edges(live, 0, g2.provs, Map.empty())
  if (edges.msg != "") { return tagged("G3", edges.msg) }
  let cyc = find_cycle(live, 0, edges.succ, Map.empty())
  if (cyc.length() > 0) {
    return tagged("G3", "dependency cycle: ".concat(arrow_join(cyc)).concat(" (G3)"))
  }
  return ""
}

// ==================================================== item 384: foreign-construct redirects
//
// PORT of the reference's foreign-construct redirect table (item 384,
// src/revl/parser.py `_FOREIGN_STMT_KEYWORDS` / `_reject_incr_decr` and the
// per-form redirects in `for_stmt` / `_postfix` / `_primary`, plus
// src/revl/lower.py `_FOREIGN_NAME_REDIRECTS` / `_reject_foreign_name`).
//
// WHY IT IS A SEPARATE PASS HERE. The reference fires each redirect from the
// ERROR PATH it already reaches — the parser's statement/declaration dispatch,
// or the name resolver after `scope`/`callables` lookup missed. The gate has
// no such error path: its statement reader is deliberately lenient (it reads
// only the forms admission cares about and skips the rest), so every one of
// these programs slid through as ADMITTED. So the port is a TOKEN-LEVEL
// SCANNER that recognises each foreign construct POSITIVELY, run before
// `parse_prog` — which is where the reference's parser half fires, and (since
// no admission refusal can precede a parse refusal) an order the reference
// agrees with for the parser half. The name half is ordered slightly earlier
// than the reference's (which resolves names during lowering, after the parse):
// no program that reaches a G1/G2/G3/G4/A1 refusal also contains one of these
// twelve foreign spellings, so the difference is unobservable.
//
// PRECISION IS THE WHOLE GAME. A redirect that fires on a VALID program is a
// false refusal, and the differential oracle would (rightly) fail. Every
// pattern below is therefore either (a) a token shape no valid revl program
// can produce (`i + +`, `if` not followed by `(`, `for (let`, `xs[a:b]`,
// `{"k":`, `f(k=v)`), or (b) an identifier from the twelve-name table used in
// a position that only the foreign idiom puts it in AND not bound anywhere in
// the file (`bound_idents` over-approximates the binders, so a program that
// genuinely names something `len`/`print`/`and` shadows the entry exactly as
// it does in the reference). When in doubt the scanner stays silent: a missed
// redirect is a smaller defect than a false refusal.

// The twelve identifier-shaped foreign idioms (lower.py `_FOREIGN_NAME_REDIRECTS`).
fn foreign_names() -> List[Str] {
  return ["and", "or", "not", "True", "False", "const", "len", "print",
          "throw", "def", "lambda", "elif"]
}

fn foreign_name_msg(n: Str) -> Str {
  if (n == "and") { return "`and` is not a revl operator" }
  if (n == "or") { return "`or` is not a revl operator" }
  if (n == "not") { return "`not` is not a revl operator" }
  if (n == "True") { return "revl booleans are lowercase" }
  if (n == "False") { return "revl booleans are lowercase" }
  if (n == "const") { return "revl has no `const`" }
  if (n == "len") { return "revl has no `len(...)`" }
  if (n == "print") { return "revl has no `print`" }
  if (n == "throw") { return "revl has no `throw`" }
  if (n == "def") { return "revl has no `def`" }
  if (n == "lambda") { return "revl has no `lambda`" }
  return "revl has no `elif`"
}

// Token kinds that can BEGIN an expression.
fn starts_expr(t: Token) -> Bool {
  if (t.kind == "kw") {
    return contains(["true", "false", "null", "await", "spawn", "match",
                     "hole", "async"], t.text)
  }
  return contains(["ident", "int", "float", "string", "template",
                   "(", "[", "{", "!", "-", "~"], t.kind)
}

// Token kinds that can END an expression.
fn ends_expr(t: Token) -> Bool {
  if (t.kind == "kw") { return contains(["true", "false", "null"], t.text) }
  return contains(["ident", "int", "float", "string", "template",
                   ")", "]", "}"], t.kind)
}

// A BOUNDS-SAFE token lookup. `tkc` guards only the upper bound, so a negative
// index reads from the END of the list under the python backend (silently
// wrong) and PANICS under the rust tier the gate crate ships — which is how
// the crate's own cargo test caught `def foo() ...`, whose `def` sits at index
// 0 and looks one token back. Every backward look in this scanner goes through
// this. `tkc` itself is left alone: its other callers never index backwards,
// and widening it would be a behaviour change outside this slice.
fn tkl(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Token {
  if (i < 0) { return { kind: "eof", text: "", line: 0 } }
  return tkc(ts, i)
}

// Positions where a VALUE — or a prefix operator applied to one — is expected.
// A POSITIVE allow-list, not `!ends_expr`: `@@@ not revl @@@` is a parse
// failure (the reference says "expected a top-level declaration, found '@'"),
// NOT a Python `not`, and reading a redirect out of a malformed adjacency
// would replace an honest BAD with a confident wrong answer. `@` is a real
// revl token (item 396's `@py file "..."`, a composition row label `@db`), so
// it cannot simply abort the scan the way an `error` token does — it just
// never precedes a value.
fn value_pos(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  if (i == 0) { return true }
  let p = tkl(ts, i - 1)
  if (p.kind == "kw") {
    return contains(["return", "if", "while", "assert", "fail", "emit",
                     "await", "else", "of", "in", "spawn", "match", "effect",
                     "undo", "every", "after", "subscribe", "handoff"], p.text)
  }
  return contains(["(", "[", "{", ",", "=", ":", "?", "=>", "->", "!", "+",
                   "-", "*", "/", "%", "==", "!=", "===", "!==", "<", ">",
                   "<=", ">=", "&&", "||", "??", "&", "|", "^", "~", "<<",
                   ">>", ";"], p.kind)
}

// Positions where a STATEMENT may start: the open/close of a block, an
// explicit separator, the start of input, or straight after a statement that
// ended in a complete expression.
fn stmt_pos(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  if (i == 0) { return true }
  let p = tkl(ts, i - 1)
  return contains(["{", "}", ";"], p.kind) || ends_expr(p)
}

// Every identifier the file could possibly BIND, over-approximated: a name in
// this set never triggers a name redirect (it is shadowed, as in the
// reference, where the resolver reaches the table only after a scope miss).
// Over-approximating is the safe direction — it can only SUPPRESS a redirect.
fn bound_idents(ts: List[Token]) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var j = 0
  while (j < ts.length()) {
    if (atk(ts, j, "ident")) {
      let prev = tkl(ts, j - 1)
      let nxt = tkc(ts, j + 1)
      let binder = prev.kind == "kw" && contains(
        ["let", "var", "fn", "type", "service", "component", "extern", "use",
         "spawn", "provide", "handoff", "isolate", "intercept"], prev.text)
      // `x:` (param / record field / config field), `x =>` (single arrow
      // param), `(x of` (for-of bind), and a bare arrow param list `(a, b) =>`.
      let shape = nxt.kind == ":" || nxt.kind == "=>"
        || (prev.kind == "(" && nxt.kind == "kw" && nxt.text == "of")
        || ((prev.kind == "(" || prev.kind == ",")
            && (nxt.kind == "," || nxt.kind == ")"))
      if (binder || shape) { out = out.push(tkc(ts, j).text) }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  return out
}

// The index of the bracket matching the opener at `i` (`(`, `[` or `{`), or -1.
fn match_bracket(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Int {
  let open = tkc(ts, i).kind
  var depth = 0
  var j = i
  while (j < ts.length()) {
    let k = tkc(ts, j).kind
    if (k == "(" || k == "[" || k == "{") { depth += 1 }
    if (k == ")" || k == "]" || k == "}") {
      depth -= 1
      if (depth == 0) { return j }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  return 0 - 1
}

// `f(k=v)` — a depth-1 `=` between a call's parens. `=` never appears in a
// revl expression, and a record update's `=` sits inside `{}` (depth 2+).
fn kwarg_at(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  let end = match_bracket(ts, i)
  if (end == 0 - 1) { return false }
  var depth = 0
  var j = i
  while (j < end) {
    let k = tkc(ts, j).kind
    if (k == "(" || k == "[" || k == "{") { depth += 1 }
    if (k == ")" || k == "]" || k == "}") { depth -= 1 }
    if (depth == 1 && k == "=") { return true }
    j += 1
  }
  return false
}

// `xs[a:b]` — a depth-1 `:` between index brackets that is NOT the `:` of a
// ternary (`?` opens one) and not inside a nested record/call.
fn slice_at(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  let end = match_bracket(ts, i)
  if (end == 0 - 1) { return false }
  var depth = 0
  var q = 0
  var j = i
  while (j < end) {
    let k = tkc(ts, j).kind
    if (k == "(" || k == "[" || k == "{") { depth += 1 }
    if (k == ")" || k == "]" || k == "}") { depth -= 1 }
    if (depth == 1 && k == "?") { q += 1 }
    if (depth == 1 && k == ":") {
      if (q == 0) { return true }
      q -= 1
    }
    j += 1
  }
  return false
}

// `(a, b)` in VALUE position — a depth-1 comma inside a GROUPING paren whose
// close is not followed by `=>`/`:` (an arrow parameter list).
fn tuple_at(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  let end = match_bracket(ts, i)
  if (end == 0 - 1) { return false }
  let closer = tkc(ts, end + 1)
  if (closer.kind == "=>" || closer.kind == ":") { return false }
  var depth = 0
  var j = i
  while (j < end) {
    let k = tkc(ts, j).kind
    if (k == "(" || k == "[" || k == "{") { depth += 1 }
    if (k == ")" || k == "]" || k == "}") { depth -= 1 }
    if (depth == 1 && k == ",") { return true }
    j += 1
  }
  return false
}

// A grouping `(` opened where a VALUE is expected: after `let x =` / `var x =`
// (never `type X =`, whose parenthesised form is a TYPE and carries the
// reference's other tuple diagnostic), or after a binary/prefix operator.
// `->` is deliberately absent: `-> (Int, Int)` is a type position.
fn value_paren(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Bool {
  let prev = tkl(ts, i - 1)
  if (prev.kind == "=") {
    let p2 = tkl(ts, i - 2)
    let p3 = tkl(ts, i - 3)
    return p2.kind == "ident"
      && p3.kind == "kw" && (p3.text == "let" || p3.text == "var")
  }
  if (prev.kind == "kw") { return prev.text == "return" }
  return contains(["+", "*", "/", "%", "==", "!=", "<", ">", "<=", ">=",
                   "&&", "||", "??", "!", "&", "^", "|", "<<", ">>"], prev.kind)
}

// The name-table redirect for the identifier at `i`, "" when it does not fire.
fn foreign_name_at(ts: List[Token], i: Int, bound: List[Str]) -> Str {
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (t.kind != "ident") { return "" }
  let n = t.text
  if (!contains(foreign_names(), n)) { return "" }
  if (contains(bound, n)) { return "" }
  if (tkl(ts, i - 1).kind == ".") { return "" }
  let prev = tkl(ts, i - 1)
  let nxt = tkc(ts, i + 1)
  let nxt2 = tkc(ts, i + 2)
  // infix word operators: two juxtaposed primaries around the word.
  if (n == "and" || n == "or") {
    return ends_expr(prev) && starts_expr(nxt) ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "not") {
    return value_pos(ts, i) && starts_expr(nxt) ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "True" || n == "False") {
    return value_pos(ts, i) ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "const") {
    return nxt.kind == "ident" && nxt2.kind == "=" ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "len" || n == "print") {
    return nxt.kind == "(" ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "throw") {
    return stmt_pos(ts, i) && starts_expr(nxt) ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "def") {
    return nxt.kind == "ident" && nxt2.kind == "(" ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  if (n == "lambda") {
    return nxt.kind == "ident" && nxt2.kind == ":" ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
  }
  // `elif` in its Python shape: it closes a `then` block and opens a condition.
  return prev.kind == "}" && nxt.kind == "(" ? foreign_name_msg(n) : ""
}

// The syntactic (parser-half) redirect at `i`, "" when none fires.
fn foreign_form_at(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Str {
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  let nxt = tkc(ts, i + 1)
  // `i++` — `++` strands a `+` in operand position, and revl has no unary `+`,
  // so an adjacent `+ +` is unconditionally an error (parser `_reject_incr_decr`).
  if (t.kind == "+" && nxt.kind == "+") {
    return "revl has no `++` increment operator — expressions are pure (syntax-2.0 §3.3)"
  }
  // `i--` — `- -` IS valid as `a - (-b)`, so this fires only when what follows
  // cannot begin an expression (exactly where the reference's error path lands).
  if (t.kind == "-" && nxt.kind == "-" && !starts_expr(tkc(ts, i + 2))) {
    return "revl has no `--` decrement operator — expressions are pure (syntax-2.0 §3.3)"
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "for" && nxt.kind == "(") {
    if (atw(ts, i + 2, "let") || atw(ts, i + 2, "var")) {
      return "revl has no C-style `for (init; cond; step)` loop"
    }
    if (atk(ts, i + 2, "ident")
        && (atk(ts, i + 3, "=") || atk(ts, i + 3, ";"))) {
      return "revl has no C-style `for (init; cond; step)` loop"
    }
    if (atk(ts, i + 2, "ident") && atw(ts, i + 3, "in")) {
      return "revl iterates elements with `for (x of xs)`, not `for (x in xs)`"
    }
  }
  // a revl `if` STATEMENT always opens `if (`, so an `if` not followed by `(`
  // is the Python postfix conditional (parser `_ternary`).
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "if" && nxt.kind != "(") {
    return "revl has no Python-style `a if c else b` conditional expression"
  }
  let pv = tkl(ts, i - 1)
  if (t.kind == "ident" && nxt.kind == "("
      && !(pv.kind == "kw" && pv.text == "fn")
      && kwarg_at(ts, i + 1)) {
    return "revl has no keyword arguments — `f(k=v)` is not a call"
  }
  if (t.kind == "[" && ends_expr(pv) && slice_at(ts, i)) {
    return "revl has no slice syntax `xs[a:b]`"
  }
  if (t.kind == "{" && nxt.kind == "string" && atk(ts, i + 2, ":")) {
    return "revl records use identifier keys, not string keys like `{\"k\": v}`"
  }
  if (t.kind == "(" && value_paren(ts, i) && tuple_at(ts, i)) {
    return "revl has no tuples — `(a, b)` is not a value"
  }
  return ""
}

// The whole scan, left to right: the FIRST foreign construct in the token
// stream, "" when the file contains none. Left-to-right approximates the
// reference's own report order (its parser walks the source once).
pub fn foreign_scan(src: Str) -> Str {
  let ts = lex_src(src)
  let bound = bound_idents(ts)
  var i = 0
  while (i < ts.length()) {
    // A lex failure makes every position downstream meaningless — an `error`
    // token means the stream is not this program's tokens any more, so no
    // redirect may be read out of it.
    if (atk(ts, i, "error")) { return "" }
    // `emission[fs.write(path="/tmp")]` — a PARAMETERISED CAPABILITY (item
    // 294) is the one revl form that spells `name=value` inside parens, and it
    // is not a call. Skip the whole capability list rather than mistake it for
    // a Python keyword argument.
    if (atw(ts, i, "emission") && atk(ts, i + 1, "[")) {
      let e = match_bracket(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { i = e + 1 } else { i += 1 }
    } else {
      let f = foreign_form_at(ts, i)
      if (f != "") { return f }
      let n = foreign_name_at(ts, i, bound)
      if (n != "") { return n }
      i += 1
    }
  }
  return ""
}

// The admission entry. "" admits; "<tag>|<message>" refuses.
// ----------------------------------------------------------- nesting bound
//
// How deep the front end would have to go to read this token stream, measured
// WITHOUT going there: one pass, four integers and a small integer stack, no
// recursion of its own.
//
// The front end is recursive over the expression tree twice over — once
// descending to parse it (one nested level costs a full pass down the
// precedence ladder) and again in every walk over the result. Neither descent
// had a bound, so a short source could drive both arbitrarily deep. On the
// python emission that surfaces as a `RecursionError`; on the rust and wasm
// emissions behind `crates/revl-gate` it exhausts the stack, and a stack
// exhaustion in rust ABORTS — `catch_unwind` cannot turn it back into a
// verdict, so an embedder loses the process instead of getting an answer. For
// a gate whose whole job is to decide sources it does not trust, an input that
// kills it is the worst answer available.
//
// selfhost/parser.rvl now holds `nesting_limit()` on both descents itself, and
// that is what makes the abort impossible. What it CANNOT do is say so: a
// parse that stops early returns `Bad`, and an expression-level `Bad` is not
// something `parse_prog` surfaces, so the program would come back with no
// objection raised — the wrong thing to say about a source nobody can read. So
// the bound is measured here too, ahead of any descent, where it is a verdict.
//
// Two quantities, each held against the same limit, because the two ways to
// make the front end descend do not look alike in the token stream:
//
//   * BRACKET DEPTH — `(`/`[`/`{` open a nested expression and the ladder
//     follows them one for one. This is the `((((...))))`, `f(f(f(...)))`,
//     `[[[...]]]`, `{a:{a:...}}`, `List[List[...]]` family, plus the two forms
//     that recurse without a bracket and so are counted alongside it: a prefix
//     run (`!!!x`, `- -x`, `emit emit x`) and a right-associative `??`/`?`
//     chain.
//   * SPINE LENGTH — the operators at ONE bracket level. The parser reads
//     `a + a + a + ...` and `x.f().f()...` with a loop, so they cost it no
//     stack, but they build a tree one level taller per operator and every
//     later walk over that tree pays for it. The count is saved and restored
//     across brackets (so a `.concat()` chain still accumulates across the
//     calls in it) and cleared by a statement or declaration keyword, which is
//     where one expression ends and the next begins.
//
// Both are estimates and both can be off by a little in either direction — a
// `${...}` interpolation is one flattened lexer token so a nested template
// reads as flat here, and a `?` closing an optional type reads as a
// conditional. Neither direction can produce an ABORT, which is the property
// this exists for: under-counting leaves the parser's own bound to stop the
// descent, and over-counting costs a refusal at a depth no program a human or
// a generator writes ever reaches.

// A `kw` token that ends whatever expression preceded it: every statement and
// declaration head. The expression keywords (`true`/`false`/`null`, `match`,
// `emit`, `await`, `hole`, `config`, `async`, `as`) are deliberately absent —
// they continue an expression rather than starting a statement.
fn ends_expr_kw(w: Str) -> Bool {
  if (w == "let" || w == "var" || w == "return" || w == "if" || w == "else") { return true }
  if (w == "while" || w == "for" || w == "of" || w == "break" || w == "continue") { return true }
  if (w == "fn" || w == "pub" || w == "type" || w == "use" || w == "test") { return true }
  if (w == "service" || w == "component" || w == "extern" || w == "provide") { return true }
  if (w == "requires" || w == "provides" || w == "config" || w == "effect") { return true }
  if (w == "assert" || w == "fail" || w == "compensate" || w == "undo") { return true }
  return false
}

// A token that makes a NODE out of what precedes it, and so adds one level to
// the tree the walkers will descend.
fn extends_spine(k: Str, w: Str) -> Bool {
  if (k == "kw") { return w == "emit" }
  if (k == "." || k == "?." || k == "?") { return true }
  if (k == "!" || k == "~") { return true }
  if (k == "+" || k == "-" || k == "*" || k == "/" || k == "%") { return true }
  if (k == "&&" || k == "||" || k == "??") { return true }
  if (k == "&" || k == "|" || k == "^" || k == "<<" || k == ">>") { return true }
  if (k == "==" || k == "!=" || k == "===" || k == "!==") { return true }
  if (k == "<" || k == ">" || k == "<=" || k == ">=") { return true }
  return false
}

// The `${` interpolation levels embedded in a template token's serialized
// text. A NESTED template flattens to ONE token (the lexer does not descend
// into `${...}`) whose `v:` body carries the inner template's source verbatim,
// so each nesting level leaves a literal `${` in the text; SIBLING
// interpolations (`` `${a}${b}` ``) are consumed into separate parts and leave
// none. Counting `${` therefore measures the depth the reference parser
// re-enters `pure_expr` for at each interpolation — the one nesting shape the
// token walk below cannot otherwise see, because the whole nest is one flat
// token, and so the one shape that reached `admit_src` with no objection while
// the reference refused it at the bound (issue #310, template half).
fn interp_levels(s: Str) -> Int {
  var c = 0
  var i = 0
  let m = s.length()
  while (i + 1 < m) {
    if (s.codepoint_at(i) == 36 && s.codepoint_at(i + 1) == 123) { c += 1 }
    i += 1
  }
  return c
}

fn nesting_depth(ts: List[Token]) -> Int {
  var depth = 0        // open brackets
  var run = 0          // consecutive prefix unaries at this point
  var chain = 0        // `??` / `?` levels at this bracket level
  var spine = 0        // operators in the expression being read
  var sibs = 0         // the tallest expression finished at this level
  var up_spine: List[Int] = []
  var up_sibs: List[Int] = []
  var worst = 0
  var i = 0
  let n = ts.length()
  while (i < n) {
    let k = ts[i].kind
    let w = ts[i].text
    if (k == "(" || k == "[" || k == "{") {
      depth += 1
      run = 0
      chain = 0
      up_spine = up_spine.push(spine)
      up_sibs = up_sibs.push(sibs)
      spine = 0
      sibs = 0
    } else if (k == ")" || k == "]" || k == "}") {
      if (depth > 0) { depth -= 1 }
      run = 0
      chain = 0
      var inner = sibs
      if (spine > inner) { inner = spine }
      var os = 0
      var ob = 0
      if (up_spine.length() > 0) {
        os = up_spine[up_spine.length() - 1]
        up_spine = up_spine.slice(0, up_spine.length() - 1)
        ob = up_sibs[up_sibs.length() - 1]
        up_sibs = up_sibs.slice(0, up_sibs.length() - 1)
      }
      // the closed group becomes ONE node at the level above — a call, an
      // index, a list, a record, a block — sitting on top of whichever of its
      // two sides is taller
      spine = os
      if (inner > spine) { spine = inner }
      spine += 1
      sibs = ob
    } else if (k == ":") {
      // a field or conditional `:` ends the expression before it, but NOT the
      // `?`/`??` chain — the `:` of a conditional is that chain's own
      // separator and `a ? b : c ? d : ...` keeps descending across it
      if (spine > sibs) { sibs = spine }
      spine = 0
      run = 0
    } else if (k == "," || k == ";" || k == "="
               || (k == "kw" && ends_expr_kw(w))) {
      // a sibling boundary — a separator, an assignment `=`, a statement or
      // declaration keyword. The next expression starts from nothing, and the
      // one that just ended only matters as far as it was the tallest
      if (spine > sibs) { sibs = spine }
      spine = 0
      run = 0
      chain = 0
    } else {
      if (k == "!" || k == "~" || k == "-" || (k == "kw" && w == "emit")) {
        run += 1
      } else {
        run = 0
      }
      if (k == "??" || k == "?") { chain += 1 }
      if (extends_spine(k, w)) { spine += 1 }
    }
    // a template's own `${` nesting sits on top of the bracket depth it is read
    // at — one flat token, so it is measured from its text rather than walked
    var tmpl = 0
    if (k == "template") { tmpl = interp_levels(w) }
    var here = depth + run + chain
    if (spine > here) { here = spine }
    if (sibs > here) { here = sibs }
    if (depth + tmpl > here) { here = depth + tmpl }
    if (here > worst) { worst = here }
    // stop at the answer. Past the bound the verdict cannot change, and
    // walking the rest of a hostile source is work an attacker chose the size
    // of — the saved-spine stack in particular is copied on every close, so a
    // deeply nested input would cost quadratic time to finish measuring.
    if (worst > nesting_limit()) { return worst }
    i += 1
  }
  return worst
}

// The refusal, worded as `revl.parser` words it so the two implementations
// read the same when they refuse the same program.
fn too_deep_msg() -> Str {
  return `expression nesting is deeper than the parser's limit of ${nesting_limit().to_str()} levels`
}

pub fn admit_src(src: Str) -> Str {
  // item 384: the foreign-construct redirects run first — the reference's
  // parser half fires before any admission check, and no program that reaches
  // an admission refusal carries one of these spellings.
  let fgn = foreign_scan(src)
  if (fgn != "") { return tagged("FOREIGN", fgn) }
  // ... and the nesting bound before any descent, so a source built to run the
  // front end out of stack is refused rather than parsed. The token stream is
  // read once here and handed to `type_ctors` below, which used to lex the
  // source a second time of its own.
  let ts0 = lex_src(src)
  if (nesting_depth(ts0) > nesting_limit()) {
    return tagged("BAD", too_deep_msg())
  }
  let pg = parse_prog(src)
  if (pg.bad != "") { return tagged("BAD", pg.bad) }
  // the ADT constructors the program declares join the callable universe a bare
  // call head resolves against (`call_head_declared`). Read off the token
  // stream rather than out of `Prog`, because `p_top` steps over `type`
  // declarations structurally — see `type_ctors`.
  let base = ctx_with_callables(build_maps(pg), type_ctors(ts0))
  let cachev = check_cache_fns(pg.fns)
  if (cachev != "") { return cachev }
  // G4: a host acquire verb in a `fn` body a component reaches. Decided over
  // the whole program before any body is gated, exactly where `check_and_lower`
  // places `_refuse_host_acquire_in_component_reachable_fn` — after the fn
  // declarations are lowered and BEFORE the module-fn A1 twins below.
  let acq = check_reachable_fn_acquire(pg)
  if (acq != "") { return acq }
  // the module-fn A1 twins (first-class async value, leaky pure arrow) are
  // decided over the lowered fns BEFORE any component is lowered, so they are
  // surfaced first, in declaration order (lower.py's fn loop precedes the
  // component loop).
  let fnv = check_module_fns(pg.fns, 0, base)
  if (fnv != "") { return fnv }
  let cnames = comp_names(pg.comps, 0, [])
  var ci = 0
  while (ci < pg.comps.length()) {
    let comp = pg.comps[ci]
    // a body-scan refusal (the PRELUDE rule, `intercept`/`isolate`/`handoff`
    // metadata validation) is decided before the provide methods are gated, so
    // it is surfaced first, in composition order. `comp.refuse` is already the
    // full "<TAG>|<message>".
    if (comp.refuse != "") { return comp.refuse }
    // the code-less spawn-form refusals (bind-to-a-handle, unknown target) fire
    // as a spawn statement lowers — before the component's G4/A1 provider
    // verdicts and before the composition-time spawn bounds.
    let sf = spawn_form_comp(comp, cnames)
    if (sf != "") { return sf }
    let cx = ctx_for(base, comp)
    let v = check_component(comp, cx)
    if (v != "") { return v }
    ci += 1
  }
  // the spawn-boundary bounds (G4/G6) and capability attenuation (item 66) run
  // once every component is lowered, before the G2/G3 link — the reference's
  // order (`_check_spawn_emission_bounds`, `_check_spawn_attenuation`, `_link`).
  let sv = check_spawn(pg, base)
  if (sv != "") { return sv }
  // item 350: AT MOST ONE `boot` component. The reference decides this in
  // `_link`, ahead of the G2 provision-conflict scan, so the gate does too —
  // otherwise a composition with two contracts AND a provision conflict would
  // get different verdicts from the two implementations. Two contracts cannot
  // both be the exhaustive list of what the host must inject, and an admission
  // check against "the" contract would silently check only one of them.
  let boots = boot_names(lex_src(src))
  if (boots.length() > 1) {
    return tagged("BOOT",
      "a composition declares at most one `boot` component, found "
        .concat(join_comma(boots, 0, "")))
  }
  return link_g2_g3(pg)
}

// The bare verdict tag ("" | "G1" | "G2" | "G3" | "G4" | "A1" | "PRELUDE" |
// "SPAWN" | "HANDOFF" | "ROUTE" | "BOOT" | "BAD") — the coarse oracle.
pub fn admit_tag(src: Str) -> Str {
  let v = admit_src(src)
  if (v == "") { return "" }
  let bar = v.indexOf("|")
  if (bar == 0 - 1) { return v }
  return v.slice(0, bar)
}

// ============================================================ interchange IR
//
// SEAM 1 (roadmap item 227): lower.rvl also PRODUCES the emittable interchange
// IR, not only the admission VERDICT. `lower_to_ir(src)` serialises the covered
// surface of the interchange IR (docs/backend-ir.md) — the shape emit_py.rvl /
// emit_rust.rvl navigate via stdlib/value.rvl's `value_*` — as canonical JSON,
// structurally IDENTICAL to what src/revl/lower.py's `check_and_lower` produces
// for the same source. This is what lets item 224's `compile.rvl` drop the
// reference `compile_source` from the middle of the native pipeline (SEAM 1).
//
// COVERED (byte-identical to the reference IR on the emit_py/emit_rust corpora):
//   * `ir_version` — 1 / 2 / 3, from the header-visible feature triggers (a
//     module fn/extern/type/test declaration; a component `if`/`fail`/timer/
//     `spawn` for v3, an `isolate`/`intercept` for v2) AND the one body-level
//     trigger: a stdlib-builtin (or tagged-ADT) node in a lowered component body
//     bumps v3 (`_has_builtin`) — the now-lowered body makes that node visible
//     (`ir_component`), so services_methods matches the reference's v3.
//   * the SERVICES table — every service's methods with `params` / `returns` /
//     `emission` (+ `capabilities` for a scoped `emission[..]`, + `async`).
//   * each COMPONENT's declaration header — `config` / `requires` / `provides`.
//   * the full typed COMPONENT/method expression BODY (item 242): `let-effect` /
//     `effect` / `emit … compensate` (saga) / `if`+`fail` guards / `timer`
//     (`every`/`after` with `interval_ms`) / `provide` with full method bodies
//     (`effect`/`emit`/`return`/`let`), over the component dialect —
//     `{kind:"name",id}` for a scoped name, `{kind:"config",field}` for a config
//     read, a `req`-target call, plain `bin`/`un` (no `operands`), `builtin`.
//   * the `functions` / `types` sections (items 232), and the `externs` section
//     (item 241): each `extern`'s `class` / `params` / `returns` / verbatim
//     `@backend` `bodies`, from the lexer's `hostbody` token (selfhost/lexer.rvl).
//
// The IR's per-component `source` (the input filename) and the top-level
// `manifest` (the linker's composition artifact) are environment/link artifacts,
// not properties of the program text, so they are intentionally not emitted —
// the covered emitter surface (emit_py.rvl / emit_rust.rvl) never reads them.
// With those two projections aside, `lower_to_ir` is now COMPLETE for the whole
// covered surface (function AND component/extern programs), byte-identical to
// the reference IR and emitter-ready end to end.
//
// The whole section is ADDITIVE: `admit_src` is unchanged, and this code reuses
// the in-file token reader (`tkc`/`atw`/`close_brace`/`params_at`/`type_at`/…)
// plus the declaration advancers (`p_service`/`p_component`/`p_fn`/`p_extern`)
// so its structural walk never drifts from the admission parser's.

// A built IR fragment plus whether it stayed inside the covered surface.
type IrRes    = { ok: Bool, js: Str }
type IrSvcR   = { js: Str, v3: Bool }
type IrCompR  = { js: Str, v3: Bool, v2: Bool }
type IrMethR  = { js: Str, i: Int, v3: Bool }
type IrAcc    = { svcs: Str, comps: Str, v3: Bool, v2: Bool }

fn mk_irres(ok: Bool, js: Str) -> IrRes { return { ok: ok, js: js } }
fn mk_irsvcr(js: Str, v3: Bool) -> IrSvcR { return { js: js, v3: v3 } }
fn mk_ircompr(js: Str, v3: Bool, v2: Bool) -> IrCompR {
  return { js: js, v3: v3, v2: v2 }
}
fn mk_irmethr(js: Str, i: Int, v3: Bool) -> IrMethR {
  return { js: js, i: i, v3: v3 }
}
fn mk_iracc(svcs: Str, comps: Str, v3: Bool, v2: Bool) -> IrAcc {
  return { svcs: svcs, comps: comps, v3: v3, v2: v2 }
}

fn empty_prog() -> Prog { return { fns: [], svcs: [], comps: [], bad: "" } }

// A JSON string literal: the covered values are identifiers/type spellings, but
// the escaper is faithful so a literal carrying `"`/`\`/control chars round-trips.
fn json_escape(s: Str) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < s.length()) {
    let c = s.charAt(i)
    // revl's only string escapes are `\"` and `\\` (control chars never reach a
    // source string literal), so newline/tab/CR are matched by CODE POINT — a
    // verbatim `@backend` host body (item 241) is the first covered value that
    // carries real newlines, which JSON requires escaped.
    let cc = c.charCodeAt(0)
    if (c == "\\") { out = out.concat("\\\\") }
    else { if (c == "\"") { out = out.concat("\\\"") }
    else { if (cc == 10) { out = out.concat("\\n") }
    else { if (cc == 9) { out = out.concat("\\t") }
    else { if (cc == 13) { out = out.concat("\\r") }
    else { out = out.concat(c) } } } } }
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn jstr(s: Str) -> Str { return "\"".concat(json_escape(s)).concat("\"") }

// ================================================ the declared `Secret[T]` mark
//
// `Untrusted[T]`, `Trusted[T]` and `Secret[T]` are QUALIFIERS on a declared
// type, orthogonal to the base type (docs/design/249-taint-provenance.md, open
// question 2). The reference strips all three off every declared type before
// anything downstream sees it (`src/revl/taint.py::strip_qualifiers`) and, for
// `Secret`, leaves a FLAG behind in the IR — which is then the ONLY surviving
// record that the position is confidential, because the spelling is gone.
//
// This is the DECLARATION-side marking and nothing more: it reads a qualifier
// off a declared type and stamps the IR positions a backend consumes. The
// reference's FLOW analysis — the origin lattice, the `confidential` /
// `secret` origins, the G9 refusals, `endorse`, the declassifiers — is a much
// larger surface and is NOT ported here; the self-host still has no taint
// analysis, only the marking a taint analysis would also produce.
//
// Without this the self-host frontend rendered the qualifier as a TYPE NAME
// (`"returns": "Secret[Str]"`) and emitted no stamp at all, so every tier that
// goes through `compile_to` lost the marking before its emitter could act on
// it — an emitter that knows how to redact cannot act on a mark the frontend
// never produced.

type TyParts = { head: Str, args: List[Str] }

fn ty_ident_char(c: Str) -> Bool { return c.is_alnum() || c == "_" }

// Split a rendered argument list at its TOP-LEVEL commas: `Str, Map[Int, Row]`
// -> [`Str`, `Map[Int, Row]`]. Bracket AND paren depth are counted, so a
// function-typed argument (`(Int, Str) -> Bool`) stays one argument.
fn ty_split_args(s: Str) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var depth = 0
  var start = 0
  var i = 0
  while (i < s.length()) {
    let c = s.charAt(i)
    if (c == "[" || c == "(") { depth += 1 }
    else { if (c == "]" || c == ")") { depth -= 1 }
    else { if (c == "," && depth == 0) {
      out = out.push(ty_trim(s.slice(start, i)))
      start = i + 1
    } } }
    i += 1
  }
  let last = ty_trim(s.slice(start, s.length()))
  if (last != "") { out = out.push(last) }
  return out
}

// The index of the `)` matching a leading `(`, or -1.
fn ty_close_paren(ty: Str) -> Int {
  var depth = 0
  var i = 0
  while (i < ty.length()) {
    let c = ty.charAt(i)
    if (c == "(") { depth += 1 }
    else { if (c == ")") { depth -= 1  if (depth == 0) { return i } } }
    i += 1
  }
  return 0 - 1
}

// `List[Row]` -> (`List`, [`Row`]); `Str` -> (`Str`, []); `(Int) -> Str` ->
// (`->`, [`Int`, `Str`]). The last shape is the reference's `parse_type`
// normalisation, which makes a function type an ordinary application whose LAST
// argument is the return; `ty_render` puts the surface spelling back.
fn ty_parse(ty: Str) -> TyParts {
  if (ty.startsWith("(")) {
    let close = ty_close_paren(ty)
    if (close != 0 - 1 && ty.slice(close, ty.length()).startsWith(") -> ")) {
      let ps = ty_split_args(ty.slice(1, close))
      return { head: "->", args: ps.push(ty.slice(close + 5, ty.length())) }
    }
    return { head: ty, args: [] }
  }
  let br = ty.indexOf("[")
  if (br == 0 - 1 || !ty.endsWith("]")) { return { head: ty, args: [] } }
  return { head: ty.slice(0, br),
           args: ty_split_args(ty.slice(br + 1, ty.length() - 1)) }
}

// The inverse of `ty_parse` (the reference's `format_type`).
fn ty_render(head: Str, args: List[Str]) -> Str {
  if (args.length() == 0) { return head }
  if (head == "->") {
    var ps: List[Str] = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < args.length() - 1) { ps = ps.push(args[i])  i += 1 }
    return "(".concat(ps.join(", ")).concat(") -> ").concat(args[args.length() - 1])
  }
  return head.concat("[").concat(args.join(", ")).concat("]")
}

fn is_qual_head(h: Str) -> Bool {
  return h == "Secret" || h == "Trusted" || h == "Untrusted"
}

// Whether any qualifier head appears as a STANDALONE head — never the tail of a
// longer identifier such as a user type `MyTrusted[T]`. The byte-identity fast
// path: a type carrying no qualifier is returned VERBATIM by `taint_strip`,
// never round-tripped through parse/render (which would renormalise spacing and
// break byte-identity for every program that uses no annotation).
fn taint_has_qualifier(ty: Str) -> Bool {
  var i = 0
  while (i < ty.length()) {
    if (ty.charAt(i) == "[") {
      var a = i
      while (a > 0 && ty_ident_char(ty.charAt(a - 1))) { a -= 1 }
      if (is_qual_head(ty.slice(a, i))) { return true }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  return false
}

// Remove every `Untrusted[…]`/`Trusted[…]`/`Secret[…]` wrapper, anywhere in the
// type: `Secret[Str]` -> `Str`; `Opt[Secret[Str]]` -> `Opt[Str]`;
// `Result[Secret[Str], Str]` -> `Result[Str, Str]`. Idempotent, and a no-op on
// any type that carries no qualifier.
fn taint_strip(ty: Str) -> Str {
  if (ty == "" || !taint_has_qualifier(ty)) { return ty }
  let p = ty_parse(ty)
  if (is_qual_head(p.head) && p.args.length() == 1) { return taint_strip(p.args[0]) }
  if (p.args.length() == 0) { return p.head }
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < p.args.length()) { out = out.push(taint_strip(p.args[i]))  i += 1 }
  return ty_render(p.head, out)
}

// True if a `Secret[…]` head appears anywhere the VALUE ITSELF reaches. The
// marking has to follow the VALUE rather than the spelling: a fallible lease
// hands the payload back as `Result[Secret[Str], Str]`, and the bytes crossing
// there are exactly as confidential as `Secret[Str]`'s, so the walk recurses
// through type constructors (`Result`, `Opt`, `List`, `Map`, a tuple, a
// declared record — every container whose value graph physically carries the
// bytes).
//
// The ONE argument position that is not a container is a FUNCTION type's: `(
// Secret[Str]) -> Int` declares what a closure will one day be CALLED WITH, not
// what the closure value holds, so descending there would redact a function
// repr while the real disclosure happens at the call.
fn taint_mentions_secret(ty: Str) -> Bool {
  if (ty == "") { return false }
  let p = ty_parse(ty)
  if (p.head == "Secret") { return true }
  if (p.head == "->" || p.head == "Fn") { return false }
  var i = 0
  while (i < p.args.length()) {
    if (taint_mentions_secret(p.args[i])) { return true }
    i += 1
  }
  return false
}

// True if a declared return puts a `Secret[…]` in the position a WITNESS is
// read out of — the `Ok` arm of `Result[W, E]`. The narrower sibling of
// `taint_mentions_secret`: a witnessed crossing's durable discharge descriptor
// records the Ok witness as the inverse's referent, so `Result[Secret[Str],
// Str]` puts a confidential value on disk while `Result[Str, Secret[Str]]` —
// the same declaration with the qualifier on the error arm — does not.
fn taint_secret_witness(ty: Str) -> Bool {
  if (ty == "") { return false }
  let p = ty_parse(ty)
  return p.head == "Result" && p.args.length() == 2
         && taint_mentions_secret(p.args[0])
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- services IR

// A declaration's `params` list. The qualifier is STRIPPED off each type and,
// where the declared type mentions `Secret[…]`, the position carries
// `"secret": true` instead — the reference's `_ir_params`. Additive: absent
// unless the author wrote the qualifier, so every existing IR document is
// byte-identical.
fn ir_params_json(ps: List[ParamN]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < ps.length()) {
    var e = "{\"name\": ".concat(jstr(ps[i].name))
      .concat(", \"type\": ").concat(jstr(taint_strip(ps[i].ty)))
    if (taint_mentions_secret(ps[i].ty)) { e = e.concat(", \"secret\": true") }
    e = e.concat("}")
    out = out == "" ? e : out.concat(", ").concat(e)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn ir_caps_json(cs: List[Str]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < cs.length()) {
    out = out == "" ? jstr(cs[i]) : out.concat(", ").concat(jstr(cs[i]))
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// One service's method table -> `"m": {params, returns, emission[, caps][, async][, idempotent]}`
// pairs. Mirrors the reference's per-method dict (check_and_lower's `services`
// comprehension): `capabilities` present iff a SCOPED `emission[..]`, `async`
// iff the op is `async` (each of which also bumps `ir_version` to 3).
fn ir_methods(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int, acc: Str, v3: Bool) -> IrMethR {
  if (i >= end || atk(ts, i, "}")) { return mk_irmethr(acc, i, v3) }
  var j = i
  var em = false
  var scoped = false
  var isAsync = false
  var isIdempotent = false
  var caps: List[Str] = []
  while (atw(ts, j, "emission") || atw(ts, j, "async") || atw(ts, j, "idempotent")) {
    if (atw(ts, j, "emission")) {
      em = true
      j += 1
      if (atk(ts, j, "[")) {
        scoped = true
        var k = j + 1
        while (k < end && !atk(ts, k, "]")) {
          if (atk(ts, k, "ident")) { caps = caps.push(tkc(ts, k).text) }
          k += 1
        }
        j = k + 1
      }
    } else if (atw(ts, j, "idempotent")) {
      isIdempotent = true
      j += 1
    } else {
      isAsync = true
      j += 1
    }
  }
  if (!atw(ts, j, "fn")) { return mk_irmethr(acc, j, v3) }
  let nm = tkc(ts, j + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, j + 3)
  var retJson = "null"
  var nexti = ps.i
  if (atk(ts, ps.i, "arrow")) {
    let tr = type_at(ts, ps.i + 1)
    retJson = jstr(taint_strip(tr.ty))
    nexti = tr.i
  }
  var mj = jstr(nm).concat(": {\"params\": [").concat(ir_params_json(ps.ps))
    .concat("], \"returns\": ").concat(retJson)
    .concat(", \"emission\": ").concat(em ? "true" : "false")
  if (scoped) {
    mj = mj.concat(", \"capabilities\": [").concat(ir_caps_json(caps)).concat("]")
  }
  if (isAsync) { mj = mj.concat(", \"async\": true") }
  if (isIdempotent) { mj = mj.concat(", \"idempotent\": true") }
  mj = mj.concat("}")
  let nacc = acc == "" ? mj : acc.concat(", ").concat(mj)
  return ir_methods(ts, nexti, end, nacc, v3 || isAsync || isIdempotent)
}

fn ir_service(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> IrSvcR {
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  let end = close_brace(ts, i + 2)
  let hi = end == 0 - 1 ? i + 3 : end - 1
  let ms = ir_methods(ts, i + 3, hi, "", false)
  return mk_irsvcr(jstr(nm).concat(": {\"methods\": {").concat(ms.js).concat("}}"),
                   ms.v3)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- body IR
//
// The FULL typed component/method expression body (item 242), byte-identical to
// the reference producer (`src/revl/lower.py::_lower_component` / `_lower_provide`
// / `_lower_component_pure_expr` / `_lower_expr`). Every helper returns `ok:false`
// the moment it meets a form outside the covered surface, so the caller omits the
// `body` and keeps the byte-identical header.
//
// The component dialect differs from the module-fn spine (`lir_expr`, item 232):
//   * a name in scope lowers to `{"kind": "name", "id": <n>}`, NOT `var`;
//   * `config.<field>` lowers to `{"kind": "config", "field": <field>}`;
//   * a required-service call `req.op(args)` lowers to a `req`-target call;
//   * `bin`/`un` carry NO `operands` tag (the component path never annotates
//     arithmetic — only the pure-fn lowering does).
// Names accumulate a lexical `sc` scope: activation-body `let-effect` binds, and
// provide-method params/`let` binds. `config` is a header declaration, so it is
// skipped structurally rather than emitted as a step.

fn str_has(s: Str, sub: Str) -> Bool { return s.indexOf(sub) != 0 - 1 }

// `["a", "b"]` content for a method's surface param-name list.
fn str_list_json(xs: List[Str]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < xs.length()) {
    out = out == "" ? jstr(xs[i]) : out.concat(", ").concat(jstr(xs[i]))
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// `<int><unit>` -> milliseconds as decimal digits ("" for an unknown unit),
// mirroring parser.py's `_DURATION_UNITS`.
fn duration_ms_str(numText: Str, unit: Str) -> Str {
  var mult = 0
  if (unit == "ms") { mult = 1 }
  if (unit == "s") { mult = 1000 }
  if (unit == "m") { mult = 60000 }
  if (unit == "h") { mult = 3600000 }
  if (unit == "d") { mult = 86400000 }
  if (mult == 0) { return "" }
  let n = match numText.to_int() { Some(v) => v, None => 0 - 1 }
  if (n < 0) { return "" }
  return (n * mult).to_str()
}

// A component-dialect pure value expression -> an IR node. `sc` is the lexical
// scope (names visible as `{"kind": "name", "id": …}`); `reqSet` the required-
// service roots.
fn cir_expr(e: Expr, sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  return match e {
    IntLit(v)   => mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": ".concat(v).concat("}")),
    FloatLit(v) => mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": ".concat(v).concat("}")),
    BoolLit(v)  => mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": ".concat(v).concat("}")),
    StrLit(v)   => mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": ".concat(jstr(v)).concat("}")),
    Var(n)      => contains(sc, n)
      ? mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"name\", \"id\": ".concat(jstr(n)).concat("}"))
      : mk_irres(false, ""),
    Bin(b)      => cir_bin(b, sc, reqSet),
    Un(u)       => cir_un(u, sc, reqSet),
    If(f)       => cir_if_expr(f, sc, reqSet),
    Field(f)    => cir_field(f, sc, reqSet),
    Call(c)     => cir_call(c.target, c.args, sc, reqSet),
    // `emit <call>` in value/return position is a plain observation of the
    // emission: the reference (`_lower_component_pure_expr` EmitExpr) strips the
    // marker and lowers the inner call, so the IR node is just that call.
    Emit(u)     => cir_expr(u.e, sc, reqSet),
    _           => mk_irres(false, ""),
  }
}

fn cir_args(args: List[Expr], i: Int, sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str], acc: Str) -> IrRes {
  if (i >= args.length()) { return mk_irres(true, acc) }
  let e = cir_expr(args[i], sc, reqSet)
  if (!e.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  let nacc = acc == "" ? e.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(e.js)
  return cir_args(args, i + 1, sc, reqSet, nacc)
}

fn cir_reqcall(root: Str, meth: Str, args: List[Expr], sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  let a = cir_args(args, 0, sc, reqSet, "")
  if (!a.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  return mk_irres(true,
    "{\"kind\": \"call\", \"target\": {\"kind\": \"req\", \"name\": "
      .concat(jstr(root)).concat("}, \"method\": ").concat(jstr(meth))
      .concat(", \"args\": [").concat(a.js).concat("]}"))
}

// A stdlib-builtin method on a name in scope (`n.to_str()`). `to_int` carries a
// `recv` tag; the covered component surface never spells one, so the receiver
// type is unknown here and it is emitted as `null` (byte-exact where `recv` is
// unread, honest where it would be — no corpus component reaches it).
fn cir_builtin(root: Str, meth: Str, args: List[Expr], sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  let a = cir_args(args, 0, sc, reqSet, "")
  if (!a.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  var s = "{\"kind\": \"builtin\", \"method\": ".concat(jstr(meth))
    .concat(", \"target\": {\"kind\": \"name\", \"id\": ").concat(jstr(root)).concat("}")
    .concat(", \"args\": [").concat(a.js).concat("]")
  if (meth == "to_int") { s = s.concat(", \"recv\": null") }
  return mk_irres(true, s.concat("}"))
}

fn cir_call(tg: Expr, args: List[Expr], sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  return match tg {
    Field(fl) => match fl.target {
      Var(root) => contains(reqSet, root)
        ? cir_reqcall(root, fl.name, args, sc, reqSet)
        : (contains(sc, root) && is_builtin_method(fl.name)
            ? cir_builtin(root, fl.name, args, sc, reqSet)
            : mk_irres(false, "")),
      _ => mk_irres(false, ""),
    },
    _ => mk_irres(false, ""),
  }
}

fn cir_field(f: FieldN, sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  let isConfig = match f.target { Var(n) => n == "config", _ => false, }
  if (isConfig) {
    return mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"config\", \"field\": ".concat(jstr(f.name)).concat("}"))
  }
  let t = cir_expr(f.target, sc, reqSet)
  if (!t.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  return mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"field\", \"target\": ".concat(t.js)
    .concat(", \"name\": ").concat(jstr(f.name)).concat("}"))
}

fn cir_bin(b: BinN, sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  let l = cir_expr(b.l, sc, reqSet)
  let r = cir_expr(b.r, sc, reqSet)
  if (!l.ok || !r.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  return mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"bin\", \"op\": ".concat(jstr(b.op))
    .concat(", \"left\": ").concat(l.js).concat(", \"right\": ").concat(r.js).concat("}"))
}

fn cir_un(u: UnN, sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  let o = cir_expr(u.e, sc, reqSet)
  if (!o.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  return mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"un\", \"op\": ".concat(jstr(u.op))
    .concat(", \"operand\": ").concat(o.js).concat("}"))
}

fn cir_if_expr(f: IfN, sc: List[Str], reqSet: List[Str]) -> IrRes {
  let c = cir_expr(f.cond, sc, reqSet)
  let t = cir_expr(f.then_, sc, reqSet)
  let e = cir_expr(f.els, sc, reqSet)
  if (!c.ok || !t.ok || !e.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  return mk_irres(true, "{\"kind\": \"if\", \"cond\": ".concat(c.js)
    .concat(", \"then\": ").concat(t.js).concat(", \"else\": ").concat(e.js).concat("}"))
}

// A step whose extent spans a brace block (`if`/timer) — carries the resume index.
type StepR = { ok: Bool, js: Str, i: Int }

// `if (<cond>) { <guard> } [else { <guard> }]` -> the component guard step. The
// then/else bodies hold only `fail` and nested `if` (a component `if` is an
// L-Raise decision, not general control flow). `else` is present only when spelled.
fn cir_if_step(ts: List[Token], i: Int, reqSet: List[Str], sc: List[Str]) -> StepR {
  let c = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
  if (is_bad(c.e)) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: i } }
  let ce = cir_expr(c.e, sc, reqSet)
  if (!ce.ok || !atk(ts, c.i, "{")) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: i } }
  let tend = close_brace(ts, c.i)
  if (tend == 0 - 1) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: i } }
  let thenR = cir_guard_stmts(ts, c.i + 1, tend - 1, reqSet, sc, "")
  if (!thenR.ok) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: i } }
  var js = "{\"step\": \"if\", \"cond\": ".concat(ce.js).concat(", \"then\": [").concat(thenR.js).concat("]")
  var nexti = tend
  if (atw(ts, tend, "else") && atk(ts, tend + 1, "{")) {
    let eend = close_brace(ts, tend + 1)
    if (eend == 0 - 1) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: i } }
    let elseR = cir_guard_stmts(ts, tend + 2, eend - 1, reqSet, sc, "")
    if (!elseR.ok) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: i } }
    js = js.concat(", \"else\": [").concat(elseR.js).concat("]")
    nexti = eend
  }
  return { ok: true, js: js.concat("}"), i: nexti }
}

// The `fail`/nested-`if` guard steps of a component `if`.
fn cir_guard_stmts(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, reqSet: List[Str], sc: List[Str], acc: Str) -> IrRes {
  if (lo >= hi || atk(ts, lo, "}")) { return mk_irres(true, acc) }
  if (atw(ts, lo, "fail")) {
    let r = expr_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let m = cir_expr(r.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!m.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"fail\", \"message\": ".concat(m.js).concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_guard_stmts(ts, r.i, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, lo, "if")) {
    let g = cir_if_step(ts, lo, reqSet, sc)
    if (!g.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let nacc = acc == "" ? g.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(g.js)
    return cir_guard_stmts(ts, g.i, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc)
  }
  return mk_irres(false, "")
}

// A run of `emit <expr>` steps (a timer body). `compensate` is permitted but
// absent from the covered timer corpus.
fn cir_emit_steps(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, reqSet: List[Str], sc: List[Str], acc: Str) -> IrRes {
  if (lo >= hi || atk(ts, lo, "}")) { return mk_irres(true, acc) }
  if (!atw(ts, lo, "emit")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  let em = cir_emit_step(ts, lo, reqSet, sc)
  if (!em.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  let nacc = acc == "" ? em.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(em.js)
  return cir_emit_steps(ts, em.i, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc)
}

// One `emit <expr> [compensate <expr>]` step.
fn cir_emit_step(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, reqSet: List[Str], sc: List[Str]) -> StepR {
  let r = expr_at(ts, lo + 1)
  if (is_bad(r.e)) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: lo } }
  let ee = cir_expr(r.e, sc, reqSet)
  if (!ee.ok) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: lo } }
  var step = "{\"step\": \"emit\", \"expr\": ".concat(ee.js)
  var nx = r.i
  if (atw(ts, r.i, "compensate")) {
    let cmp = expr_at(ts, r.i + 1)
    if (is_bad(cmp.e)) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: lo } }
    let ce = cir_expr(cmp.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ce.ok) { return { ok: false, js: "", i: lo } }
    step = step.concat(", \"compensate\": ").concat(ce.js)
    nx = cmp.i
  }
  return { ok: true, js: step.concat("}"), i: nx }
}

// A provide-method's body steps (`effect`/`emit`/`return`/`let`). Scope `sc`
// starts as the method's params and grows with each `let`.
fn cir_method_stmts(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, reqSet: List[Str], sc: List[Str], acc: Str) -> IrRes {
  if (lo >= hi || atk(ts, lo, "}")) { return mk_irres(true, acc) }
  if (atk(ts, lo, ";")) { return cir_method_stmts(ts, lo + 1, hi, reqSet, sc, acc) }
  if (atw(ts, lo, "effect")) {
    if (atw(ts, lo + 1, "spawn")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let acq = expr_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(acq.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ae = cir_expr(acq.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ae.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    if (!atw(ts, acq.i, "undo")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let und = expr_at(ts, acq.i + 1)
    if (is_bad(und.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ue = cir_expr(und.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ue.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"effect\", \"acquire\": ".concat(ae.js)
      .concat(", \"undo\": ").concat(ue.js).concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_method_stmts(ts, und.i, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, lo, "emit")) {
    let em = cir_emit_step(ts, lo, reqSet, sc)
    if (!em.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let nacc = acc == "" ? em.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(em.js)
    return cir_method_stmts(ts, em.i, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, lo, "return")) {
    let r = expr_at(ts, lo + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= lo + 1) {
      let step0 = "{\"step\": \"return\", \"expr\": null}"
      let nacc0 = acc == "" ? step0 : acc.concat(", ").concat(step0)
      return cir_method_stmts(ts, lo + 1, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc0)
    }
    let ve = cir_expr(r.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ve.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"return\", \"expr\": ".concat(ve.js).concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_method_stmts(ts, r.i, hi, reqSet, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, lo, "let") || atw(ts, lo, "var")) {
    let mutable = atw(ts, lo, "var")
    let name = tkc(ts, lo + 1).text
    var j = lo + 2
    if (atk(ts, j, ":")) { j = type_at(ts, j + 1).i }
    if (!atk(ts, j, "=")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let r = expr_at(ts, j + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= j + 1) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ve = cir_expr(r.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ve.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"let\", \"name\": ".concat(jstr(name)).concat(", \"value\": ")
      .concat(ve.js).concat(", \"mutable\": ").concat(mutable ? "true" : "false").concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_method_stmts(ts, r.i, hi, reqSet, sc.push(name), nacc)
  }
  return mk_irres(false, "")
}

// A `provide <key> { … }` method table -> `[{name, params, body}]`. Method
// params are the surface names (typed by the service, not carried here); the
// shorthand `= expr` becomes a single `return`.
fn cir_prov_methods(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int, reqSet: List[Str], acc: Str) -> IrRes {
  if (i >= end || atk(ts, i, "}")) { return mk_irres(true, acc) }
  if (atw(ts, i, "async")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  if (!atw(ts, i, "fn")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, i + 3)
  let pnames = param_names(ps.ps)
  let pjson = str_list_json(pnames)
  var h = ps.i
  if (atk(ts, h, "arrow")) { h = type_at(ts, h + 1).i }
  if (atk(ts, h, "=")) {
    let r = expr_at(ts, h + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let be = cir_expr(r.e, pnames, reqSet)
    if (!be.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let mj = "{\"name\": ".concat(jstr(nm)).concat(", \"params\": [").concat(pjson)
      .concat("], \"body\": [{\"step\": \"return\", \"expr\": ").concat(be.js).concat("}]}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? mj : acc.concat(", ").concat(mj)
    return cir_prov_methods(ts, r.i, end, reqSet, nacc)
  }
  if (atk(ts, h, "{")) {
    let bend = close_brace(ts, h)
    if (bend == 0 - 1) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let br = cir_method_stmts(ts, h + 1, bend - 1, reqSet, pnames, "")
    if (!br.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let mj = "{\"name\": ".concat(jstr(nm)).concat(", \"params\": [").concat(pjson)
      .concat("], \"body\": [").concat(br.js).concat("]}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? mj : acc.concat(", ").concat(mj)
    return cir_prov_methods(ts, bend, end, reqSet, nacc)
  }
  return mk_irres(false, "")
}

// A component activation body -> its `body` step list. `config` is a header
// declaration, skipped structurally; the accumulated `sc` threads `let-effect`
// binds into later steps.
fn cir_body(ts: List[Token], i: Int, end: Int, reqSet: List[Str],
            provs: List[Bind], sc: List[Str], acc: Str) -> IrRes {
  if (i >= end || atk(ts, i, "}")) { return mk_irres(true, acc) }
  if (atw(ts, i, "config") && atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
    let ce = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
    if (ce == 0 - 1) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    return cir_body(ts, ce, end, reqSet, provs, sc, acc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "provide")) {
    let key = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    if (!atk(ts, i + 2, "{")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let pend = close_brace(ts, i + 2)
    if (pend == 0 - 1) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ms = cir_prov_methods(ts, i + 3, pend - 1, reqSet, "")
    if (!ms.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let svc = bind_lookup(provs, key, 0)
    let step = "{\"step\": \"provide\", \"name\": ".concat(jstr(key))
      .concat(", \"service\": ").concat(jstr(svc))
      .concat(", \"methods\": [").concat(ms.js).concat("]}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_body(ts, pend, end, reqSet, provs, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "let") && atk(ts, i + 2, "=") && atw(ts, i + 3, "effect")) {
    let name = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    if (atw(ts, i + 4, "spawn")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let acq = expr_at(ts, i + 4)
    if (is_bad(acq.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ae = cir_expr(acq.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ae.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    if (!atw(ts, acq.i, "undo")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let sc2 = sc.push(name)
    let und = expr_at(ts, acq.i + 1)
    if (is_bad(und.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ue = cir_expr(und.e, sc2, reqSet)
    if (!ue.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"let-effect\", \"bind\": ".concat(jstr(name))
      .concat(", \"acquire\": ").concat(ae.js).concat(", \"undo\": ").concat(ue.js).concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_body(ts, und.i, end, reqSet, provs, sc2, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "effect")) {
    if (atw(ts, i + 1, "spawn")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let acq = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
    if (is_bad(acq.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ae = cir_expr(acq.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ae.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    if (!atw(ts, acq.i, "undo")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let und = expr_at(ts, acq.i + 1)
    if (is_bad(und.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ue = cir_expr(und.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!ue.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"effect\", \"acquire\": ".concat(ae.js)
      .concat(", \"undo\": ").concat(ue.js).concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_body(ts, und.i, end, reqSet, provs, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "if")) {
    let g = cir_if_step(ts, i, reqSet, sc)
    if (!g.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let nacc = acc == "" ? g.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(g.js)
    return cir_body(ts, g.i, end, reqSet, provs, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "fail")) {
    let r = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e)) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let m = cir_expr(r.e, sc, reqSet)
    if (!m.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"fail\", \"message\": ".concat(m.js).concat("}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_body(ts, r.i, end, reqSet, provs, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "emit")) {
    let em = cir_emit_step(ts, i, reqSet, sc)
    if (!em.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let nacc = acc == "" ? em.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(em.js)
    return cir_body(ts, em.i, end, reqSet, provs, sc, nacc)
  }
  if (atw(ts, i, "every") || atw(ts, i, "after")) {
    let mode = tkc(ts, i).text
    if (!atk(ts, i + 1, "int")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let numText = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    if (!atk(ts, i + 2, "ident")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let ims = duration_ms_str(numText, tkc(ts, i + 2).text)
    if (ims == "") { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    if (!atk(ts, i + 3, "{")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let bend = close_brace(ts, i + 3)
    if (bend == 0 - 1) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let bodyR = cir_emit_steps(ts, i + 4, bend - 1, reqSet, sc, "")
    if (!bodyR.ok) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let step = "{\"step\": \"timer\", \"mode\": ".concat(jstr(mode))
      .concat(", \"interval_ms\": ").concat(ims)
      .concat(", \"body\": [").concat(bodyR.js).concat("]}")
    let nacc = acc == "" ? step : acc.concat(", ").concat(step)
    return cir_body(ts, bend, end, reqSet, provs, sc, nacc)
  }
  return mk_irres(false, "")
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- components IR

fn ir_binds_json(bs: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < bs.length()) {
    let e = jstr(bs[i].name).concat(": ").concat(jstr(bs[i].ty))
    out = out == "" ? e : out.concat(", ").concat(e)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// A config-field default value -> its JSON. Only a LITERAL default is in the
// covered surface (the reference stores the raw value, e.g. `"hi, "` / `1`, not
// an IR node — `_config_default_type`'s literal families); a non-literal default
// stays `null` (deferred). Returns the value JSON and the resume index.
type CfgDef = { js: Str, i: Int }

fn ir_config_default(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> CfgDef {
  if (!atk(ts, i, "=")) { return { js: "null", i: i } }
  let r = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
  let dv = match r.e {
    IntLit(v)   => v,
    FloatLit(v) => v,
    BoolLit(v)  => v,
    StrLit(v)   => jstr(v),
    _           => "null",
  }
  // a non-literal default is deferred: do not consume it, keep `null`.
  if (dv == "null") { return { js: "null", i: i } }
  return { js: dv, i: r.i }
}

// The `config { field: Type [= <literal>] … }` block -> `[{name, type, default}]`.
fn ir_config(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Str {
  var j = lo
  while (j < hi && !(atw(ts, j, "config") && atk(ts, j + 1, "{"))) { j += 1 }
  if (j >= hi) { return "" }
  let ce = close_brace(ts, j + 1)
  let cend = ce == 0 - 1 ? hi : ce - 1
  var k = j + 2
  var out = ""
  while (k < cend) {
    if (atk(ts, k, "ident") && atk(ts, k + 1, ":")) {
      let nm = tkc(ts, k).text
      let tr = type_at(ts, k + 2)
      let dfl = ir_config_default(ts, tr.i)
      // A `Secret[T]` config field is a declared confidential INPUT. The
      // qualifier is stripped exactly as everywhere else — so the field matches
      // a scalar type and can take a default — and the flag left behind is what
      // an emitted `ConfigSchema` reads to keep the value out of the run log.
      var entry = "{\"name\": ".concat(jstr(nm)).concat(", \"type\": ")
        .concat(jstr(taint_strip(tr.ty))).concat(", \"default\": ").concat(dfl.js)
      if (taint_mentions_secret(tr.ty)) { entry = entry.concat(", \"secret\": true") }
      entry = entry.concat("}")
      out = out == "" ? entry : out.concat(", ").concat(entry)
      k = dfl.i
    } else { k += 1 }
  }
  return out
}

fn comp_has_kw(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, w: Str) -> Bool {
  var k = lo
  while (k < hi) {
    if (atw(ts, k, w)) { return true }
    k += 1
  }
  return false
}

fn ir_component(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> IrCompR {
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  var j = i + 2
  var reqs: List[Bind] = []
  var provs: List[Bind] = []
  while (atw(ts, j, "requires") || atw(ts, j, "provides")) {
    let isReq = atw(ts, j, "requires")
    var k = j + 1
    while (k < ts.length() && !atk(ts, k, "{") && !atk(ts, k, "eof")
           && !atw(ts, k, "requires") && !atw(ts, k, "provides")) {
      if (atk(ts, k, "ident") && atk(ts, k + 1, ":") && atk(ts, k + 2, "ident")) {
        let pair = { name: tkc(ts, k).text, ty: tkc(ts, k + 2).text }
        if (isReq) { reqs = reqs.push(pair) } else { provs = provs.push(pair) }
        k += 3
      } else { k += 1 }
    }
    j = k
  }
  let end = close_brace(ts, j)
  let lo = j + 1
  let hi = end == 0 - 1 ? j + 1 : end - 1
  let reqSet = bind_names(reqs, 0, [])
  let bodyRes = cir_body(ts, lo, hi, reqSet, provs, [], "")
  var js = "{\"name\": ".concat(jstr(nm))
    .concat(", \"config\": [").concat(ir_config(ts, lo, hi))
    .concat("], \"requires\": {").concat(ir_binds_json(reqs))
    .concat("}, \"provides\": {").concat(ir_binds_json(provs)).concat("}")
  if (bodyRes.ok) { js = js.concat(", \"body\": [").concat(bodyRes.js).concat("]") }
  js = js.concat("}")
  // a body-level stdlib builtin (or tagged ADT) construction is the reference's
  // body-only v3 trigger (`_has_builtin`); now that the body is lowered, the
  // lowered node's presence is the same signal a token scan cannot see.
  let bodyV3 = bodyRes.ok
    && (str_has(bodyRes.js, "\"kind\": \"builtin\"") || str_has(bodyRes.js, "\"kind\": \"adt\""))
  let v3 = bodyV3 || comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "if") || comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "fail")
    || comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "every") || comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "after")
    || comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "spawn")
  let v2 = comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "isolate") || comp_has_kw(ts, lo, hi, "intercept")
  return mk_ircompr(js, v3, v2)
}

// ================================================================ functions IR
//
// The `functions` section: each module `fn` lowered to its full typed body
// (statements + the typed-expression tree), byte-identical to the reference
// producer (`src/revl/lower.py::_lower_fns` + `_lower_pure_stmt`/
// `_lower_pure_expr`). Unlike the admission walk, this re-reads the body from
// the parser's Expr AST (`expr_at`) and threads a *type environment* so it can
// reproduce the annotations the reference IR carries: the `operands` tag on
// typed arithmetic, the `recv` tag on `to_int`, match-arm `payload_type`, and
// the arrow's resolved `param_types`/`returns`. The inference here is the
// projection of the checker's `infer_ast` onto the covered surface — enough to
// annotate, never to diagnose (a definite mismatch was already refused by
// `admit_src`). A form the shared parser cannot read (record-update `{ r | .. }`)
// or a receiver the projection cannot type stops the body without corrupting
// the JSON: the entry stays well-formed, it just is not byte-exact there.

type LirFn   = { js: Str, i: Int }
type StmtOne = { js: Str, env: List[Bind], muts: List[Str], i: Int }

fn mk_one(js: Str, env: List[Bind], muts: List[Str], i: Int) -> StmtOne {
  return { js: js, env: env, muts: muts, i: i }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- type env

fn tenv_get(env: List[Bind], n: Str) -> Str {
  var i = env.length() - 1
  while (i >= 0) {
    if (env[i].name == n) { return env[i].ty }
    i -= 1
  }
  return ""
}

fn tenv_put(env: List[Bind], n: Str, ty: Str) -> List[Bind] {
  return env.push({ name: n, ty: ty })
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- type spellings

fn ty_trim(s: Str) -> Str {
  var a = 0
  var b = s.length()
  while (a < b && s.charAt(a) == " ") { a += 1 }
  while (b > a && s.charAt(b - 1) == " ") { b -= 1 }
  return s.slice(a, b)
}

// Head of `Base[...]` -> "Base"; a bare or structural spelling is its own head.
fn parse_head(t: Str) -> Str {
  let b = t.indexOf("[")
  if (b == 0 - 1) { return t }
  return t.slice(0, b)
}

// Top-level (bracket-depth-0) comma split of a type argument list.
fn split_top_commas(s: Str) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var depth = 0
  var cur = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < s.length()) {
    let c = s.charAt(i)
    if (c == "[") { depth += 1  cur = cur.concat(c) }
    else { if (c == "]") { depth -= 1  cur = cur.concat(c) }
    else { if (c == "," && depth == 0) { out = out.push(ty_trim(cur))  cur = "" }
    else { cur = cur.concat(c) } } }
    i += 1
  }
  return out.push(ty_trim(cur))
}

fn type_args(t: Str) -> List[Str] {
  let b = t.indexOf("[")
  if (b == 0 - 1) { return [] }
  let inner = t.slice(b + 1, t.length() - 1)
  if (inner == "") { return [] }
  return split_top_commas(inner)
}

// Top-level comma split that tracks BOTH bracket and paren depth, so a
// function-typed parameter (`(A, T) -> A`) in a signature's parameter list is
// not split at its own comma.
fn split_top_type_commas(s: Str) -> List[Str] {
  var out: List[Str] = []
  var depth = 0
  var cur = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < s.length()) {
    let c = s.charAt(i)
    if (c == "[" || c == "(") { depth += 1  cur = cur.concat(c) }
    else { if (c == "]" || c == ")") { depth -= 1  cur = cur.concat(c) }
    else { if (c == "," && depth == 0) { out = out.push(ty_trim(cur))  cur = "" }
    else { cur = cur.concat(c) } } }
    i += 1
  }
  return out.push(ty_trim(cur))
}

// The parameter type list of a function type `(P1, P2, ..) -> R` ([] when `ty`
// is not a function type). Mirrors `function_return_ty`'s outer-paren scan.
fn fn_param_types(ty: Str) -> List[Str] {
  if (ty.length() == 0 || ty.charAt(0) != "(") { return [] }
  var depth = 0
  var i = 0
  var close = 0 - 1
  while (i < ty.length() && close == 0 - 1) {
    let c = ty.charAt(i)
    if (c == "(") { depth += 1 }
    if (c == ")") { depth -= 1  if (depth == 0) { close = i } }
    i += 1
  }
  if (close <= 0) { return [] }
  let inner = ty.slice(1, close)
  if (inner == "") { return [] }
  return split_top_type_commas(inner)
}

fn type_arg1(t: Str) -> Str {
  let a = type_args(t)
  if (a.length() == 0) { return "" }
  return a[0]
}

fn is_numeric(t: Str) -> Bool {
  return t == "Int" || t == "Int32" || t == "Float"
}

// A structural record type is `{k: T, ...}` (fields sorted); look one up.
fn struct_field(t: Str, name: Str) -> Str {
  if (t.length() < 2 || t.charAt(0) != "{") { return "" }
  let parts = split_top_commas(t.slice(1, t.length() - 1))
  var i = 0
  while (i < parts.length()) {
    let p = parts[i]
    let ci = p.indexOf(":")
    if (ci != 0 - 1 && ty_trim(p.slice(0, ci)) == name) {
      return ty_trim(p.slice(ci + 1, p.length()))
    }
    i += 1
  }
  return ""
}

// (`sort_strs` is defined earlier in this file and reused here.)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- builtin surface

// The stdlib method table (lower.py's `_BUILTIN_METHODS`): the set whose call
// lowers to a `builtin` node rather than a plain `call`.
fn is_builtin_method(m: Str) -> Bool {
  return m == "length" || m == "push" || m == "slice" || m == "charAt"
    || m == "charCodeAt" || m == "codepoint_at" || m == "indexOf"
    || m == "concat" || m == "split"
    || m == "join" || m == "repeat" || m == "startsWith" || m == "endsWith"
    || m == "is_alnum" || m == "is_digit" || m == "is_alpha" || m == "is_space"
    || m == "div_trunc" || m == "div_floor" || m == "div_euclid" || m == "mod"
    || m == "to_int" || m == "to_int32" || m == "checked_div_trunc"
    || m == "checked_div_floor" || m == "checked_div_euclid" || m == "checked_mod"
    || m == "set" || m == "lookup" || m == "has" || m == "size" || m == "keys"
    || m == "remove" || m == "to_str"
    // The Value dot-accessors (item 189) share this table in the reference so a
    // wrong-arity call is diagnosed here; the Value-receiver ones are redirected
    // to their `value_*` free function before this row is ever consulted
    // (`value_accessor_free` / `lir_call`), and `keys` doubles as the Map row.
    || m == "field" || m == "str" || m == "list"
}

fn is_host_root(n: Str) -> Bool {
  return n == "Map" || n == "Pool" || n == "Job"
}

// The stdlib method result type (the checker's `_BUILTIN_SIG` return, non-
// raising). `mod` is deliberately absent from the signature table — it lowers
// as a builtin but infers as unknown — mirroring lower.py exactly.
fn builtin_ret(method: Str, recvTy: Str) -> Str {
  if (method == "length") { return "Int" }
  if (method == "push" || method == "slice" || method == "concat"
      || method == "set" || method == "remove") { return recvTy }
  if (method == "charAt") { return "Str" }
  if (method == "charCodeAt") { return "Int" }
  // Codepoint-at-index scan (item 276): Str receiver, Int index, Int result —
  // the family of `charCodeAt`.
  if (method == "codepoint_at") { return "Int" }
  // Single-character ASCII classification (item 233): Str-only, no argument,
  // Bool result.
  if (method == "is_alnum" || method == "is_digit"
      || method == "is_alpha" || method == "is_space") { return "Bool" }
  if (method == "indexOf") { return "Int" }
  if (method == "split") { return "List[Str]" }
  if (method == "join") { return "Str" }
  if (method == "repeat") { return "Str" }
  if (method == "startsWith" || method == "endsWith" || method == "has") { return "Bool" }
  if (method == "div_trunc" || method == "div_floor" || method == "div_euclid") { return "Int" }
  if (method == "to_int") {
    if (recvTy == "Int32") { return "Int" }
    if (parse_head(recvTy) == "Str") { return "Opt[Int]" }
    return ""
  }
  if (method == "to_int32") { return "Int32" }
  if (method == "to_str") { return "Str" }
  if (method == "size") { return "Int" }
  if (method == "keys") { return "List[Str]" }
  // The Value dot-accessors (item 189): the return type of the `value_*` free
  // function each one redirects to. `keys` is shared with the Map row above and
  // answers `List[Str]` for either receiver.
  if (method == "field") { return "Value" }
  if (method == "str") { return "Str" }
  if (method == "list") { return "List[Value]" }
  if (method == "checked_div_trunc" || method == "checked_div_floor"
      || method == "checked_div_euclid" || method == "checked_mod") {
    return "Result[Int, Str]"
  }
  return ""
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- inference

// `_binop_type` (non-raising): the checker's operator result type.
fn binop_ty(op: Str, lt: Str, rt: Str) -> Str {
  if (op == "==" || op == "!=" || op == "===" || op == "!==") { return "Bool" }
  if (op == "<" || op == "<=" || op == ">" || op == ">=") { return "Bool" }
  if (op == "&&" || op == "||") { return "Bool" }
  if (op == "??") {
    if (parse_head(lt) == "Opt") { let inner = type_arg1(lt)  return inner == "" ? rt : inner }
    return lt == "" ? rt : lt
  }
  if (op == "+" && (lt == "Str" || rt == "Str")) {
    return (lt == "Str" && rt == "Str") ? "Str" : ""
  }
  if (op == "+" || op == "-" || op == "*" || op == "/" || op == "%") {
    if (op == "/") {
      if (is_numeric(lt) && is_numeric(rt)) { return "Float" }
      return ""
    }
    if ((lt == "Int32" || rt == "Int32") && lt != rt && lt != "" && rt != "") { return "" }
    if (op == "%" && (lt == "Int32" || rt == "Int32")) { return "" }
    if (lt == "Float" || rt == "Float") { return "Float" }
    if (lt == "Int32" && rt == "Int32") { return "Int32" }
    if (lt == "Int" && rt == "Int") { return "Int" }
    return ""
  }
  return ""
}

fn join_ty(a: Str, b: Str) -> Str {
  if (a == b) { return a }
  if (a == "") { return b }
  if (b == "") { return a }
  return ""
}

fn infer_field(t: Str, name: Str, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  if (name == "length") {
    let h = parse_head(t)
    if (h == "Str" || h == "Bytes" || h == "List") { return "Int" }
  }
  let structural = struct_field(t, name)
  return structural != "" ? structural : tenv_get(env, "field ".concat(t).concat(".").concat(name))
}

fn infer_index(t: Str) -> Str {
  let h = parse_head(t)
  if (h == "List") { return type_arg1(t) }
  if (h == "Str") { return "Str" }
  return ""
}

fn infer_method(recv: Expr, method: Str, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  let recvHostRoot = match recv { Var(n) => is_host_root(n), _ => false, }
  if (recvHostRoot) { return "" }
  if (!is_builtin_method(method)) { return "" }
  return builtin_ret(method, infer(recv, env))
}

fn infer_callee(callee: Expr, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  return match callee {
    Field(f) => is_empty_map_callee(callee) ? "Map[Str, Never]"
      : infer_method(f.target, f.name, env),
    _ => function_return_ty(infer(callee, env)),
  }
}

fn infer_record_ty(fields: List[InitN], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var names: List[Str] = []
  var i = 0
  while (i < fields.length()) { names = names.push(fields[i].name)  i += 1 }
  let sorted = sort_strs(names)
  var out = "{"
  var j = 0
  while (j < sorted.length()) {
    let nm = sorted[j]
    var ty = ""
    var k = 0
    while (k < fields.length()) {
      if (fields[k].name == nm) { ty = infer(fields[k].value, env) }
      k += 1
    }
    let seg = nm.concat(": ").concat(ty == "" ? "Any" : ty)
    out = j == 0 ? out.concat(seg) : out.concat(", ").concat(seg)
    j += 1
  }
  return out.concat("}")
}

fn infer_list_ty(items: List[Expr], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  if (items.length() == 0) { return "List[Never]" }
  var elem = infer(items[0], env)
  var i = 1
  while (i < items.length()) { elem = join_ty(elem, infer(items[i], env))  i += 1 }
  if (elem == "") { return "List[Never]" }
  return "List[".concat(elem).concat("]")
}

// Best-effort static type of a pure expression ("" = unknown). The covered
// projection of `infer_ast`: enough to reproduce the IR's type annotations.
fn infer(e: Expr, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  return match e {
    IntLit(v)   => "Int",
    FloatLit(v) => "Float",
    StrLit(v)   => "Str",
    BoolLit(v)  => "Bool",
    NullLit     => "",
    Var(n)      => tenv_get(env, n),
    Bin(b)      => binop_ty(b.op, infer(b.l, env), infer(b.r, env)),
    Un(u)       => u.op == "!" ? "Bool" : infer(u.e, env),
    Emit(u)     => "",
    Call(c)     => infer_callee(c.target, env),
    Field(f)    => infer_field(infer(f.target, env), f.name, env),
    OptField(f) => "",
    OptCall(c)  => "",
    Index(x)    => infer_index(infer(x.target, env)),
    If(f)       => join_ty(infer(f.then_, env), infer(f.els, env)),
    Rec(r)      => infer_record_ty(r.fields, env),
    Lst(l)      => infer_list_ty(l.items, env),
    Arrow(a)    => "",
    Match(m)    => "",
    Templ(t)    => "Str",
    Hole(h)     => h.ty,
    Bad(m)      => "",
  }
}

// The lowering's operand-type annotation for `+ - * / %` (lower.py's `bin`
// branch): the operand type when both sides agree, Float when either is Float.
fn operands_of(op: Str, lt: Str, rt: Str) -> Str {
  if (op == "+" || op == "-" || op == "*" || op == "/" || op == "%") {
    if (lt == "Int32" && rt == "Int32") { return "Int32" }
    if (lt == "Int" && rt == "Int") { return "Int" }
    if (lt == "Float" || rt == "Float") { return "Float" }
    return ""
  }
  return ""
}

fn arm_payload(scrutTy: Str, pat: Str, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  // item 391 (self-host port): a USER variant's arm payload comes from the case
  // table (`_variant_case_payload`), gated on the case actually belonging to the
  // scrutinee's ADT. The built-in `Opt`/`Result` payloads come from the
  // scrutinee's type ARGUMENTS below, so the built-in table entries
  // (`Result[Any, Any]`) never match a concrete `Result[Int, Str]` here.
  if (scrutTy != "" && tenv_get(env, "case ".concat(pat)) == scrutTy) {
    return tenv_get(env, "payload ".concat(pat))
  }
  let head = parse_head(scrutTy)
  let args = type_args(scrutTy)
  if (head == "Opt" && pat == "Some" && args.length() >= 1) { return args[0] }
  if (head == "Result" && args.length() == 2) {
    if (pat == "Ok") { return args[0] }
    if (pat == "Err") { return args[1] }
  }
  return ""
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- expr lowering

fn lir_args(args: List[Expr], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < args.length()) {
    let e = lir_expr(args[i], env)
    out = i == 0 ? e : out.concat(",").concat(e)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

// Like `lir_args`, but marks each argument's Int/Int32 -> Float / Int32 -> Int
// widen against the callee's declared parameter types (`_mark_widen` at a call
// site). A missing parameter type (defaults, varargs) leaves its argument bare.
fn lir_call_args(args: List[Expr], ptypes: List[Str], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < args.length()) {
    var e = lir_expr(args[i], env)
    if (i < ptypes.length()) { e = apply_arg_markers(e, ptypes[i], infer(args[i], env)) }
    out = i == 0 ? e : out.concat(",").concat(e)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn lir_fields(fields: List[InitN], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < fields.length()) {
    let seg = "[".concat(jstr(fields[i].name)).concat(",")
      .concat(lir_expr(fields[i].value, env)).concat("]")
    out = i == 0 ? seg : out.concat(",").concat(seg)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn lir_parts(parts: List[PartN], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < parts.length()) {
    let p = parts[i]
    let seg = p.kind == "t"
      ? "[\"text\",".concat(jstr(p.text)).concat("]")
      : "[\"expr\",".concat(lir_expr(p.e, env)).concat("]")
    out = i == 0 ? seg : out.concat(",").concat(seg)
    i += 1
  }
  return out
}

fn lir_bin(b: BinN, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  let opnd = operands_of(b.op, infer(b.l, env), infer(b.r, env))
  var s = "{\"kind\":\"bin\",\"op\":".concat(jstr(b.op))
    .concat(",\"left\":").concat(lir_expr(b.l, env))
    .concat(",\"right\":").concat(lir_expr(b.r, env))
  if (opnd != "") { s = s.concat(",\"operands\":").concat(jstr(opnd)) }
  return s.concat("}")
}

fn lir_un(u: UnN, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var s = "{\"kind\":\"un\",\"op\":".concat(jstr(u.op))
    .concat(",\"operand\":").concat(lir_expr(u.e, env))
  if (u.op == "-") {
    let ot = infer(u.e, env)
    if (ot == "Int" || ot == "Int32") { s = s.concat(",\"operands\":").concat(jstr(ot)) }
  }
  return s.concat("}")
}

fn lir_field(f: FieldN, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  if (f.name == "length") {
    let h = parse_head(infer(f.target, env))
    if (h == "Str" || h == "Bytes" || h == "List") {
      return "{\"kind\":\"len\",\"target\":".concat(lir_expr(f.target, env)).concat("}")
    }
  }
  let opt = parse_head(infer_field(infer(f.target, env), f.name, env)) == "Opt"
  return "{\"kind\":\"field\",\"target\":".concat(lir_expr(f.target, env))
    .concat(",\"name\":").concat(jstr(f.name)).concat(opt ? ",\"opt\":true" : "").concat("}")
}

fn lir_builtin(f: FieldN, args: List[Expr], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var s = "{\"kind\":\"builtin\",\"method\":".concat(jstr(f.name))
    .concat(",\"target\":").concat(lir_expr(f.target, env))
    .concat(",\"args\":[").concat(lir_args(args, env)).concat("]")
  if (f.name == "to_int") {
    let rt = infer(f.target, env)
    s = s.concat(",\"recv\":").concat(rt == "" ? "null" : jstr(rt))
  }
  return s.concat("}")
}

// item 383 (self-host port): the receiver-first list transforms `xs.map(f)` /
// `xs.filter(p)` / `xs.reduce(init, f)` are PURE SUGAR for the generic free
// functions `list_map` / `list_filter` / `list_reduce`. The reference desugars
// them BEFORE typing and lowering (parser.py `desugar_list_transform`, wired at
// the method-call site in typecheck.py and lower.py); the receiver becomes the
// leading argument, preserving the written argument order. A syntactic redirect
// to a real generic `fn`, NOT a builtin-method row — no per-backend branch — so
// the existing generic-call + arrow-argument path does all the work. Skipped for
// a host-family receiver (that stays on the host stub surface), exactly as the
// reference guards on `not _host_receiver`.
fn list_transform_free(name: Str) -> Str {
  if (name == "map") { return "list_map" }
  if (name == "filter") { return "list_filter" }
  if (name == "reduce") { return "list_reduce" }
  return ""
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------- ADT constructors
//
// item 391 (self-host port): TAGGED ADT construction. `Ok(x)`, `Err(e)` and any
// user variant case (`Circle(r)`) lower to an `adt` node naming the ADT the case
// belongs to — lower.py's `_case_table` + `_tagged_case` — not to a plain `call`
// of a function that does not exist. `Opt`'s `Some`/`None` are deliberately NOT
// tagged: they stay host-null/value and are handled as identity/null downstream.
// Before this, the self-host frontend emitted `{"kind":"call", ...}` for every
// constructor and `selfhost/emit_py.rvl` had no `adt` branch at all, so no
// program that BUILDS a Result or a user variant could be self-compiled — a hole
// the corpus could not see, because its `result.rvl` document only MATCHES.
//
// The table rides in the type environment under a reserved key shape
// (`"case <Name>"` -> the ADT, `"payload <Name>"` -> the case payload) so it
// reaches `lir_expr` through the `env` every lowering function already threads.
// A space is not an identifier character, so a reserved key can never collide
// with a bound name, and `tenv_get`'s last-wins scan gives shadowing for free.
type CaseAcc = { binds: List[Bind], amb: List[Str] }
type CaseStep = { a: CaseAcc, i: Int }

fn amb_has(amb: List[Str], n: Str) -> Bool {
  var i = 0
  while (i < amb.length()) { if (amb[i] == n) { return true }  i += 1 }
  return false
}

// `_case_table`'s per-case rule. `Some`/`None` are reserved for `Opt`, so a user
// ADT reusing them is ambiguous and dropped; a user ADT MAY reuse `Ok`/`Err`,
// shadowing the built-in `Result`; any other collision is ambiguous and dropped.
// A dropped name is recorded as the empty ADT, which `tenv_get` already answers
// for an absent key — so "dropped" and "never declared" read identically.
fn case_add(a: CaseAcc, name: Str, adt: Str, payload: Str) -> CaseAcc {
  if (name == "Some" || name == "None") {
    return { binds: a.binds.push({ name: "case ".concat(name), ty: "" }),
             amb: a.amb.push(name) }
  }
  let cur = tenv_get(a.binds, "case ".concat(name))
  let shadows_result = (name == "Ok" || name == "Err") && cur.startsWith("Result")
  if (!shadows_result && (cur != "" || amb_has(a.amb, name))) {
    return { binds: a.binds.push({ name: "case ".concat(name), ty: "" }),
             amb: a.amb.push(name) }
  }
  var b = a.binds.push({ name: "case ".concat(name), ty: adt })
  b = b.push({ name: "payload ".concat(name), ty: payload })
  return { binds: b, amb: a.amb }
}

// One `type <Name> = <case> | <case>` declaration's cases, read exactly the way
// `ir_type_cases` reads them for the `types` section.
fn decl_cases(ts: List[Token], i: Int, a: CaseAcc) -> CaseStep {
  let lineHi = line_end(ts, i, ts.length())
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  var j = i + 2
  if (atk(ts, j, "[")) { j = skip_brackets(ts, j) }
  if (!atk(ts, j, "=")) { return { a: a, i: lineHi } }
  j += 1
  if (atk(ts, j, "{")) {
    let bend = close_brace(ts, j)
    if (bend == 0 - 1 || atk(ts, i + 2, "[")) {
      return { a: a, i: bend == 0 - 1 ? lineHi : bend }
    }
    var binds = a.binds
    var k = j + 1
    while (k < bend - 1) {
      if (atk(ts, k, "ident") && atk(ts, k + 1, ":")) {
        let tr = type_at(ts, k + 2)
        if (!tr.ok || tr.i <= k + 2) { break }
        binds = tenv_put(binds, "field ".concat(nm).concat(".").concat(tkc(ts, k).text),
                         taint_strip(tr.ty))
        k = tr.i
      } else { k += 1 }
    }
    let fields = binds
    return { a: { binds: fields, amb: a.amb }, i: bend }
  }
  if (!is_variant_rhs(ts, j, lineHi)) { return { a: a, i: lineHi } }
  var k = j
  var acc = a
  while (k < lineHi) {
    if (atk(ts, k, "ident")) {
      let cn = tkc(ts, k).text
      var payload = ""
      var nk = k + 1
      if (atk(ts, k + 1, "(")) {
        let tr = type_at(ts, k + 2)
        payload = tr.ty
        nk = tr.i
        if (atk(ts, nk, ")")) { nk += 1 }
      }
      acc = case_add(acc, cn, nm, payload)
      k = nk
    } else { k += 1 }
  }
  return { a: acc, i: lineHi }
}

fn case_binds_walk(ts: List[Token], i: Int, a: CaseAcc) -> CaseAcc {
  if (i >= ts.length() || atk(ts, i, "eof")) { return a }
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (at_boot(ts, i)) { return case_binds_walk(ts, i + 1, a) }
  if (t.kind != "kw") { return case_binds_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), a) }
  if (t.text == "type") {
    let d = decl_cases(ts, i, a)
    return case_binds_walk(ts, d.i, d.a)
  }
  if (t.text == "test") {
    if (atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { return case_binds_walk(ts, e, a) }
    }
    return case_binds_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), a)
  }
  if (t.text == "extern") { return case_binds_walk(ts, p_extern(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, a) }
  let public_fn = t.text == "pub" && atw(ts, i + 1, "fn")
  if (t.text == "fn" || public_fn) {
    let fi = public_fn ? i + 1 : i
    let ps = params_at(ts, fi + 3)
    let ret = atk(ts, ps.i, "arrow") ? taint_strip(type_at(ts, ps.i + 1).ty) : "Unit"
    var param_types: List[Str] = []
    var pi = 0
    while (pi < ps.ps.length()) {
      param_types = param_types.push(taint_strip(ps.ps[pi].ty))
      pi += 1
    }
    let ty = "(".concat(param_types.join(", ")).concat(") -> ").concat(ret)
    let binds = tenv_put(a.binds, tkc(ts, fi + 1).text, ty)
    return case_binds_walk(ts, p_fn(ts, fi, empty_prog()).i, { binds: binds, amb: a.amb })
  }
  if (t.text == "service") { return case_binds_walk(ts, p_service(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, a) }
  if (t.text == "component") { return case_binds_walk(ts, p_component(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, a) }
  return case_binds_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), a)
}

// The built-in half of `_case_table`, declared first so a user ADT can shadow
// `Ok`/`Err` and a user reuse of `Some`/`None` collides with something.
fn case_binds(ts: List[Token]) -> List[Bind] {
  var b = []
  b = b.push({ name: "case Some", ty: "Opt[Any]" })
  b = b.push({ name: "payload Some", ty: "" })
  b = b.push({ name: "case None", ty: "Opt[Any]" })
  b = b.push({ name: "payload None", ty: "" })
  b = b.push({ name: "case Ok", ty: "Result[Any, Any]" })
  b = b.push({ name: "payload Ok", ty: "" })
  b = b.push({ name: "case Err", ty: "Result[Any, Any]" })
  b = b.push({ name: "payload Err", ty: "" })
  return case_binds_walk(ts, 0, { binds: b, amb: [] }).binds
}

// `_tagged_case`: the ADT a name constructs, or "" when it constructs none.
// `Some`/`None` are excluded here (untagged), exactly as the reference does.
fn tagged_case_adt(env: List[Bind], name: Str) -> Str {
  if (name == "Some" || name == "None") { return "" }
  return tenv_get(env, "case ".concat(name))
}

fn lir_adt(adt: Str, case_: Str, args: List[Expr], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  return "{\"kind\":\"adt\",\"type\":".concat(jstr(adt))
    .concat(",\"case\":").concat(jstr(case_))
    .concat(",\"args\":[").concat(lir_args(args, env)).concat("]}")
}

// item 189 (self-host port): the Value dot-accessors. A `Value` receiver's
// `.field(k)` / `.str()` / `.list()` / `.keys()` is receiver-first SUGAR for
// stdlib/value.rvl's `value_*` free function — lower.py's `_VALUE_ACCESSORS`.
// It redirects to the SAME call IR `value_str(value_field(node, "k"))` lowers
// to, so the emitted code is byte-identical on every tier and NO backend needs
// a new expr-kind. Gated on a proven `Value` receiver: `.keys()` also names a
// Map builtin, which keeps the builtin path when the head is not `Value`.
fn value_accessor_free(name: Str) -> Str {
  if (name == "field") { return "value_field" }
  if (name == "str") { return "value_str" }
  if (name == "list") { return "value_list" }
  if (name == "keys") { return "value_keys" }
  return ""
}

// `recv.<m>(a, ...)` -> `<free>(recv, a, ...)`: thread the receiver in as the
// leading argument and emit the plain free-function call IR. Shared by the two
// receiver-first redirects (item 383's list transforms, item 189's Value
// accessors), which differ only in the free function they name.
fn lir_receiver_first(free: Str, recv: Expr, args: List[Expr],
                      env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  return "{\"kind\":\"call\",\"callee\":{\"kind\":\"var\",\"name\":"
    .concat(jstr(free)).concat("},\"args\":[").concat(lir_expr(recv, env))
    .concat(args.length() > 0 ? "," : "").concat(lir_args(args, env)).concat("]}")
}

fn is_empty_map_callee(callee: Expr) -> Bool {
  return match callee {
    Field(f) => f.name == "empty" && match f.target { Var(n) => n == "Map", _ => false, },
    _ => false,
  }
}

fn lir_call(callee: Expr, args: List[Expr], env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  if (is_empty_map_callee(callee) && args.length() == 0) {
    return "{\"kind\":\"maplit\",\"entries\":[]}"
  }
  // ADT construction (Result / user variants) beats every other call shape: the
  // callee is a bare `Var` naming a case, not a callable.
  let ctor = match callee { Var(n) => tagged_case_adt(env, n), _ => "", }
  if (ctor != "") {
    let cname = match callee { Var(n) => n, _ => "", }
    return lir_adt(ctor, cname, args, env)
  }
  let transform = match callee {
    Field(f) => is_host_root_recv(f.target) ? "" : list_transform_free(f.name),
    _ => "",
  }
  if (transform != "") {
    // `recv.map(...)` -> `list_map(recv, ...)`.
    let recv = match callee { Field(f) => f.target, _ => callee, }
    return lir_receiver_first(transform, recv, args, env)
  }
  let vaccess = match callee {
    Field(f) => (is_host_root_recv(f.target)
                 || parse_head(infer(f.target, env)) != "Value")
      ? "" : value_accessor_free(f.name),
    _ => "",
  }
  if (vaccess != "") {
    // `node.field("k")` -> `value_field(node, "k")`.
    let recv = match callee { Field(f) => f.target, _ => callee, }
    return lir_receiver_first(vaccess, recv, args, env)
  }
  let builtin = match callee {
    Field(f) => (is_host_root_recv(f.target) || !is_builtin_method(f.name))
      ? "" : lir_builtin(f, args, env),
    _ => "",
  }
  if (builtin != "") { return builtin }
  // A written free-function argument that widens Int/Int32 -> Float or
  // Int32 -> Int is marked on the argument node (`_mark_widen`), so every tier
  // emits the conversion. The callee's declared parameter types drive it; only
  // a `Var` callee names a signature (the reference's `ExprVar` guard), so a
  // higher-order callee keeps its arguments un-marked.
  let ptypes = match callee { Var(n) => fn_param_types(tenv_get(env, n)), _ => [], }
  let argsjs = ptypes.length() == 0 ? lir_args(args, env) : lir_call_args(args, ptypes, env)
  return "{\"kind\":\"call\",\"callee\":".concat(lir_expr(callee, env))
    .concat(",\"args\":[").concat(argsjs).concat("]}")
}

fn is_host_root_recv(recv: Expr) -> Bool {
  return match recv { Var(n) => is_host_root(n), _ => false, }
}

fn lir_arrow(a: ArrowN, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  var pn = ""
  var allTyped = a.params.length() > 0
  var inner = env
  var i = 0
  while (i < a.params.length()) {
    let p = a.params[i]
    pn = i == 0 ? jstr(p.name) : pn.concat(",").concat(jstr(p.name))
    if (p.ty == "") { allTyped = false } else { inner = tenv_put(inner, p.name, p.ty) }
    i += 1
  }
  var s = "{\"kind\":\"arrow\",\"params\":[".concat(pn).concat("],\"captures\":[],\"body\":")
    .concat(lir_expr(a.body, inner))
  if (allTyped) {
    var pt = ""
    var j = 0
    while (j < a.params.length()) {
      pt = j == 0 ? jstr(a.params[j].ty) : pt.concat(",").concat(jstr(a.params[j].ty))
      j += 1
    }
    let bt = infer(a.body, inner)
    s = s.concat(",\"param_types\":[").concat(pt).concat("],\"returns\":")
      .concat(jstr(bt == "" ? "Any" : bt))
  }
  return s.concat("}")
}

fn lir_match(m: MatchN, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  let scrutTy = infer(m.scrut, env)
  var arms = ""
  var i = 0
  while (i < m.arms.length()) {
    let a = m.arms[i]
    let pay = arm_payload(scrutTy, a.pat, env)
    var inner = env
    if (a.bind != "" && pay != "") { inner = tenv_put(inner, a.bind, pay) }
    var arm = "{\"pattern\":".concat(jstr(a.pat))
      .concat(",\"bind\":").concat(a.bind == "" ? "null" : jstr(a.bind))
      .concat(",\"body\":").concat(lir_expr(a.body, inner))
    if (pay != "") { arm = arm.concat(",\"payload_type\":").concat(jstr(pay)) }
    arm = arm.concat("}")
    arms = i == 0 ? arm : arms.concat(",").concat(arm)
    i += 1
  }
  return "{\"kind\":\"match\",\"scrutinee\":".concat(lir_expr(m.scrut, env))
    .concat(",\"arms\":[").concat(arms).concat("]}")
}

fn lir_expr(e: Expr, env: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  return match e {
    IntLit(v)   => "{\"kind\":\"lit\",\"value\":".concat(v).concat("}"),
    FloatLit(v) => "{\"kind\":\"lit\",\"value\":".concat(v).concat("}"),
    BoolLit(v)  => "{\"kind\":\"lit\",\"value\":".concat(v).concat("}"),
    StrLit(v)   => "{\"kind\":\"lit\",\"value\":".concat(jstr(v)).concat("}"),
    NullLit     => "{\"kind\":\"lit\",\"value\":null}",
    // A nullary USER case used as a bare value (`Red`) is an `adt` with no
    // args; the built-in `Result`/`Opt` cases always carry a payload, so they
    // keep the `var` shape the emitters special-case.
    Var(n)      => (tagged_case_adt(env, n) != ""
                    && tenv_get(env, "payload ".concat(n)) == ""
                    && !tagged_case_adt(env, n).startsWith("Result")
                    && !tagged_case_adt(env, n).startsWith("Opt"))
                     ? lir_adt(tagged_case_adt(env, n), n, [], env)
                     : "{\"kind\":\"var\",\"name\":".concat(jstr(n)).concat("}"),
    Bin(b)      => lir_bin(b, env),
    Un(u)       => lir_un(u, env),
    Emit(u)     => "{\"kind\":\"bad\"}",
    Call(c)     => lir_call(c.target, c.args, env),
    Field(f)    => lir_field(f, env),
    OptField(f) => "{\"kind\":\"optfield\",\"target\":".concat(lir_expr(f.target, env))
                     .concat(",\"name\":").concat(jstr(f.name)).concat("}"),
    OptCall(c)  => "{\"kind\":\"optcall\",\"target\":".concat(lir_expr(c.target, env))
                     .concat(",\"method\":").concat(jstr(c.name))
                     .concat(",\"args\":[").concat(lir_args(c.args, env)).concat("]}"),
    Index(x)    => "{\"kind\":\"index\",\"target\":".concat(lir_expr(x.target, env))
                     .concat(",\"index\":").concat(lir_expr(x.idx, env)).concat("}"),
    If(f)       => "{\"kind\":\"if\",\"cond\":".concat(lir_expr(f.cond, env))
                     .concat(",\"then\":").concat(lir_expr(f.then_, env))
                     .concat(",\"else\":").concat(lir_expr(f.els, env)).concat("}"),
    Rec(r)      => "{\"kind\":\"record\",\"fields\":[".concat(lir_fields(r.fields, env)).concat("]}"),
    Lst(l)      => "{\"kind\":\"list\",\"items\":[".concat(lir_args(l.items, env)).concat("]}"),
    Arrow(a)    => lir_arrow(a, env),
    Match(m)    => lir_match(m, env),
    Templ(t)    => "{\"kind\":\"interp\",\"parts\":[".concat(lir_parts(t.parts, env)).concat("]}"),
    Hole(h)     => "{\"kind\":\"bad\"}",
    Bad(m)      => "{\"kind\":\"bad\"}",
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- stmt lowering

// The `T` -> `Opt[T]` injection the checker accepts at a declared boundary
// (lower.py's `_inject_opt`): materialised on a `return` whose value type is a
// bare `T` where an `Opt[T]` is declared, plus numeric widening.
fn apply_ret_markers(node: Str, expected: Str, actual: Str) -> Str {
  if ((expected == "Float" && (actual == "Int" || actual == "Int32"))
      || (expected == "Int" && actual == "Int32")) {
    return node.slice(0, node.length() - 1).concat(",\"widen\":").concat(jstr(expected)).concat("}")
  }
  if (actual != "" && parse_head(expected) == "Opt" && type_args(expected).length() > 0
      && parse_head(actual) != "Opt") {
    return "{\"kind\":\"call\",\"callee\":{\"kind\":\"var\",\"name\":\"Some\"},\"args\":["
      .concat(node).concat("]}")
  }
  return node
}

// The numeric-widen half of `apply_ret_markers`, for a call ARGUMENT position:
// `_mark_widen` marks Int/Int32 -> Float and Int32 -> Int on the argument node,
// but (unlike a return) never injects `Some` — the reference's call branch calls
// only `_mark_widen`, not `_inject_opt`.
fn apply_arg_markers(node: Str, expected: Str, actual: Str) -> Str {
  if ((expected == "Float" && (actual == "Int" || actual == "Int32"))
      || (expected == "Int" && actual == "Int32")) {
    return node.slice(0, node.length() - 1).concat(",\"widen\":").concat(jstr(expected)).concat("}")
  }
  return node
}

fn is_compound_op(ts: List[Token], k: Str) -> Bool {
  return k == "+" || k == "-" || k == "*" || k == "/" || k == "%"
}

// Record-update (`{ base | field = expr, ... }`) is not in the shared parser's
// expression grammar (parser.rvl's `p_inits` reads only `field: expr` literals),
// so it is recognised at the token level here — the one covered fn-body form the
// AST cannot carry. The `|` is a bare punctuation token (a template's `|` lives
// inside the flattened `template` token, and `||` is its own token), so a
// depth-0 `|` inside a brace block is unambiguously the update separator.
type RhsR = { js: Str, ty: Str, i: Int, ok: Bool }
type UpsR = { js: Str, ok: Bool }

fn top_pipe(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Int {
  var depth = 0
  var i = lo
  while (i < hi) {
    if (atk(ts, i, "{") || atk(ts, i, "[") || atk(ts, i, "(")) { depth += 1 }
    else { if (atk(ts, i, "}") || atk(ts, i, "]") || atk(ts, i, ")")) { depth -= 1 }
    else { if (depth == 0 && atk(ts, i, "|")) { return i } } }
    i += 1
  }
  return 0 - 1
}

fn lir_updates(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, env: List[Bind]) -> UpsR {
  var i = lo
  var out = ""
  var first = true
  while (i < hi && !atk(ts, i, "}")) {
    if (atk(ts, i, ",")) { i += 1 }
    else {
      if (!atk(ts, i, "ident") || !atk(ts, i + 1, "=")) { return { js: "", ok: false } }
      let name = tkc(ts, i).text
      let r = expr_at(ts, i + 2)
      if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= i + 2) { return { js: "", ok: false } }
      let seg = "[".concat(jstr(name)).concat(",").concat(lir_expr(r.e, env)).concat("]")
      out = first ? seg : out.concat(",").concat(seg)
      first = false
      i = r.i
    }
  }
  return { js: out, ok: true }
}

// An rvalue: an ordinary expression, or a token-level record-update.
fn lir_rhs(ts: List[Token], j: Int, env: List[Bind]) -> RhsR {
  if (atk(ts, j, "{")) {
    let close = close_brace(ts, j)
    if (close != 0 - 1) {
      let pipe = top_pipe(ts, j + 1, close - 1)
      if (pipe != 0 - 1) {
        // Parse the base over the tokens BEFORE the separator `|` only. Since
        // item 366 made `|` the bitwise-OR operator, parsing from `j + 1` over
        // the full token stream would swallow the `|` (and the update fields)
        // as a bitwise-OR expression; bounding the slice to `[j+1, pipe)` keeps
        // the update separator out of the base, mirroring the reference's
        // `_suppress_bor` (which suppresses exactly this leading top-level `|`).
        let sub = ts.slice(j + 1, pipe)
        let base = expr_at(sub, 0)
        if (!is_bad(base.e) && base.i == sub.length()) {
          let ups = lir_updates(ts, pipe + 1, close - 1, env)
          if (ups.ok) {
            let js = "{\"kind\":\"record_update\",\"base\":".concat(lir_expr(base.e, env))
              .concat(",\"updates\":[").concat(ups.js).concat("]}")
            return { js: js, ty: infer(base.e, env), i: close, ok: true }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let r = expr_at(ts, j)
  if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= j) { return { js: "", ty: "", i: j, ok: false } }
  return { js: lir_expr(r.e, env), ty: infer(r.e, env), i: r.i, ok: true }
}

fn assign_env(env: List[Bind], name: Str, valExpr: Expr) -> List[Bind] {
  if (tenv_get(env, name) != "") { return env }
  let inf = infer(valExpr, env)
  return inf == "" ? env : tenv_put(env, name, inf)
}

fn lir_one_stmt(ts: List[Token], i: Int, hi: Int, env: List[Bind],
                muts: List[Str], ret: Str) -> StmtOne {
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (t.kind == "kw" && (t.text == "let" || t.text == "var")) {
    let mutable = t.text == "var"
    let name = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    var j = i + 2
    var declared = ""
    if (atk(ts, j, ":")) { let tr = type_at(ts, j + 1)  declared = tr.ty  j = tr.i }
    if (!atk(ts, j, "=")) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let r = lir_rhs(ts, j + 1, env)
    if (!r.ok) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let bindty = declared != "" ? declared : r.ty
    let env2 = bindty == "" ? env : tenv_put(env, name, bindty)
    let value = declared != "" && r.js == "{\"kind\":\"maplit\",\"entries\":[]}"
      ? r.js.slice(0, r.js.length() - 1).concat(",\"expected\":").concat(jstr(declared)).concat("}")
      : r.js
    let js = "{\"step\":\"let\",\"name\":".concat(jstr(name)).concat(",\"value\":")
      .concat(value).concat(",\"mutable\":").concat(mutable ? "true" : "false").concat("}")
    return mk_one(js, env2, mutable ? muts.push(name) : muts, r.i)
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "return") {
    let r = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= i + 1) {
      return mk_one("{\"step\":\"return\",\"expr\":null}", env, muts, i + 1)
    }
    let js = "{\"step\":\"return\",\"expr\":"
      .concat(apply_ret_markers(lir_expr(r.e, env), ret, infer(r.e, env))).concat("}")
    return mk_one(js, env, muts, r.i)
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "assert") {
    let r = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
    if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= i + 1) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    return mk_one("{\"step\":\"assert\",\"expr\":".concat(lir_expr(r.e, env)).concat("}"),
                  env, muts, r.i)
  }
  // item 379 / 391: bare loop control. Additive step kinds, no payload but a
  // `line` for diagnostics (the reference `_lower_pure_stmt` carries `line` on
  // break/continue, unlike the sibling while/for/if steps). The parser refused a
  // `break` outside a loop, so lowering only records it; teardown is untouched
  // (frame-neutral, docs/design/379-break-continue.md Decision 1).
  if (t.kind == "kw" && (t.text == "break" || t.text == "continue")) {
    let js = "{\"step\":".concat(jstr(t.text)).concat(",\"line\":")
      .concat(t.line.to_str()).concat("}")
    return mk_one(js, env, muts, i + 1)
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "if") {
    let c = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
    if (is_bad(c.e) || c.i <= i + 1 || !atk(ts, c.i, "{")) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let tend = close_brace(ts, c.i)
    if (tend == 0 - 1) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let thenjs = lir_stmts(ts, c.i + 1, tend - 1, env, muts, ret)
    var elsejs = "null"
    var nexti = tend
    if (atw(ts, tend, "else") && atk(ts, tend + 1, "{")) {
      let eend = close_brace(ts, tend + 1)
      if (eend != 0 - 1) {
        elsejs = "[".concat(lir_stmts(ts, tend + 2, eend - 1, env, muts, ret)).concat("]")
        nexti = eend
      }
    }
    let js = "{\"step\":\"if\",\"cond\":".concat(lir_expr(c.e, env))
      .concat(",\"then\":[").concat(thenjs).concat("],\"else\":").concat(elsejs).concat("}")
    return mk_one(js, env, muts, nexti)
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "while") {
    let c = expr_at(ts, i + 1)
    if (is_bad(c.e) || !atk(ts, c.i, "{")) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let bend = close_brace(ts, c.i)
    if (bend == 0 - 1) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let bodyjs = lir_stmts(ts, c.i + 1, bend - 1, env, muts, ret)
    let js = "{\"step\":\"while\",\"cond\":".concat(lir_expr(c.e, env))
      .concat(",\"body\":[").concat(bodyjs).concat("]}")
    return mk_one(js, env, muts, bend)
  }
  if (t.kind == "kw" && t.text == "for") {
    if (!atk(ts, i + 1, "(")) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let bind = tkc(ts, i + 2).text
    let it = expr_at(ts, i + 4)
    if (is_bad(it.e) || !atk(ts, it.i, ")") || !atk(ts, it.i + 1, "{")) {
      return mk_one("", env, muts, hi)
    }
    let bend = close_brace(ts, it.i + 1)
    if (bend == 0 - 1) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let iterTy = infer(it.e, env)
    var inner = env
    if (parse_head(iterTy) == "List") {
      let el = type_arg1(iterTy)
      if (el != "") { inner = tenv_put(env, bind, el) }
    }
    let bodyjs = lir_stmts(ts, it.i + 2, bend - 1, inner, muts, ret)
    let js = "{\"step\":\"for\",\"bind\":".concat(jstr(bind)).concat(",\"iterable\":")
      .concat(lir_expr(it.e, env)).concat(",\"body\":[").concat(bodyjs).concat("]}")
    return mk_one(js, env, muts, bend)
  }
  if (t.kind == "ident" && atk(ts, i + 1, "=")) {
    let name = t.text
    let r = expr_at(ts, i + 2)
    if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= i + 2) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let js = "{\"step\":\"assign\",\"name\":".concat(jstr(name)).concat(",\"value\":")
      .concat(lir_expr(r.e, env)).concat("}")
    return mk_one(js, assign_env(env, name, r.e), muts, r.i)
  }
  if (t.kind == "ident" && is_compound_op(ts, tkc(ts, i + 1).kind) && atk(ts, i + 2, "=")) {
    let name = t.text
    let baseop = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
    let r = expr_at(ts, i + 3)
    if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= i + 3) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
    let binE = Bin({ op: baseop, l: Var(name), r: r.e, paren: false })
    let js = "{\"step\":\"assign\",\"name\":".concat(jstr(name)).concat(",\"value\":")
      .concat(lir_expr(binE, env)).concat("}")
    return mk_one(js, assign_env(env, name, binE), muts, r.i)
  }
  let r = expr_at(ts, i)
  if (is_bad(r.e) || r.i <= i) { return mk_one("", env, muts, hi) }
  return mk_one("{\"step\":\"expr\",\"expr\":".concat(lir_expr(r.e, env)).concat("}"),
                env, muts, r.i)
}

fn lir_stmts(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int, env0: List[Bind],
             muts0: List[Str], ret: Str) -> Str {
  var i = lo
  var env = env0
  var muts = muts0
  var out = ""
  while (i < hi && !atk(ts, i, "}")) {
    if (atk(ts, i, ";")) { i += 1 }
    else {
      let st = lir_one_stmt(ts, i, hi, env, muts, ret)
      if (st.i <= i) { i = hi }
      else {
        if (st.js != "") { out = out == "" ? st.js : out.concat(",").concat(st.js) }
        env = st.env
        muts = st.muts
        i = st.i
      }
    }
  }
  return out
}

fn params_env(ps: List[ParamN], acc: List[Bind]) -> List[Bind] {
  var env = acc
  var i = 0
  while (i < ps.length()) {
    // stripped: past the declaration a `Secret[T]` parameter IS a `T` (the
    // qualifier is orthogonal to the base type), and the marking lives on in
    // the IR flag rather than in the type the body is checked against.
    if (ps[i].ty != "") { env = tenv_put(env, ps[i].name, taint_strip(ps[i].ty)) }
    i += 1
  }
  return env
}

// One module `fn` -> its `functions` entry. Advances past the whole fn.
fn lir_function(ts: List[Token], i: Int, cases: List[Bind], public_fn: Bool) -> LirFn {
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  let ps = params_at(ts, i + 3)
  var reti = ps.i
  var retJson = "null"
  var retTy = ""
  if (atk(ts, ps.i, "arrow")) {
    let tr = type_at(ts, ps.i + 1)
    // the qualifier is stripped for BOTH the IR and the body's type
    // environment: a `Secret[T]` is a `T` everywhere past the declaration, and
    // the marking survives only as the flag `ir_params_json` stamps.
    retJson = jstr(taint_strip(tr.ty))
    retTy = taint_strip(tr.ty)
    reti = tr.i
  }
  let cache_pure = ati(ts, reti, "cache") && atw(ts, reti + 1, "pure")
  if (cache_pure) { reti += 2 }
  if (!atk(ts, reti, "{")) { return { js: "", i: skip_line(ts, i) } }
  let bend = close_brace(ts, reti)
  if (bend == 0 - 1) { return { js: "", i: ts.length() } }
  let bodyjs = lir_stmts(ts, reti + 1, bend - 1,
                         params_env(ps.ps, cases), [], retTy)
  let js = "{\"name\":".concat(jstr(nm)).concat(",\"params\":[").concat(ir_params_json(ps.ps))
    .concat("],\"returns\":").concat(retJson).concat(",\"public\":")
    .concat(public_fn ? "true" : "false").concat(",\"body\":[")
    .concat(bodyjs).concat("]")
    .concat(cache_pure ? ",\"cache\":{\"class\":\"pure_fn\"}" : "").concat("}")
  return { js: js, i: bend }
}

// Collect every top-level `fn` into the `functions` list; skip other decls
// wholesale (a nested `fn` inside a component is not a module function).
fn fns_walk(ts: List[Token], i: Int, acc: Str, cases: List[Bind]) -> Str {
  if (i >= ts.length() || atk(ts, i, "eof")) { return acc }
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (at_boot(ts, i)) { return fns_walk(ts, i + 1, acc, cases) }
  if (t.kind != "kw") { return fns_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, cases) }
  if (t.text == "use") { return fns_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, cases) }
  if (t.text == "test") {
    if (atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { return fns_walk(ts, e, acc, cases) }
    }
    return fns_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, cases)
  }
  if (t.text == "type") { return fns_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, cases) }
  if (t.text == "extern") { return fns_walk(ts, p_extern(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc, cases) }
  let public_fn = t.text == "pub" && atw(ts, i + 1, "fn")
  if (t.text == "fn" || public_fn) {
    let f = lir_function(ts, public_fn ? i + 1 : i, cases, public_fn)
    let acc2 = f.js == "" ? acc : (acc == "" ? f.js : acc.concat(",").concat(f.js))
    return fns_walk(ts, f.i, acc2, cases)
  }
  if (t.text == "service") { return fns_walk(ts, p_service(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc, cases) }
  if (t.text == "component") { return fns_walk(ts, p_component(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc, cases) }
  return fns_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, cases)
}

// ================================================================ externs IR
//
// The `externs` section (item 241): each `extern` lowered to the reference's
// `_lower_externs` shape — `{name, class, params, returns, bodies[, async]}`.
// The verbatim `@backend { ... }` body is the raw source between the braces,
// carried by the lexer's `hostbody` token (item 241's source-slice enabler,
// selfhost/lexer.rvl). `bodies` maps each backend to that raw text, byte-
// identical to the reference (which stores the un-dedented body; the dedent is
// an emitter concern). An extern that declares an `undo`/`compensate` slot (an
// `acquire`/`emission` extern) reaches a pure-expression form outside this
// projection, so it leaves the covered surface and the whole `externs` section
// is omitted rather than emitted wrong.

type ExAcc = { js: Str, ok: Bool }

// One extern declaration -> its IR entry. `ok:false` when the declaration
// reaches a form outside the covered surface (an `undo`/`compensate` slot).
fn ir_extern(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> IrRes {
  let cls = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  var j = i + 2
  var isAsync = false
  if (atw(ts, j, "async")) { isAsync = true  j += 1 }
  if (!atw(ts, j, "fn")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  let nm = tkc(ts, j + 1).text
  var np = j + 2
  if (atk(ts, np, "[")) { np = skip_brackets(ts, np) }
  if (!atk(ts, np, "(")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  let ps = params_at(ts, np + 1)
  var k = ps.i
  var retJson = "null"
  // the return AS DECLARED, qualifier and all - `retJson` is already stripped,
  // so this is what the marking below has to be read off.
  var retDecl = ""
  if (atk(ts, k, "arrow")) {
    let tr = type_at(ts, k + 1)
    retJson = jstr(taint_strip(tr.ty))
    retDecl = tr.ty
    k = tr.i
  }
  // undo/compensate lower a pure expression in the extern scope — outside this
  // projection; leave the covered surface so the section is omitted, not wrong.
  if (atw(ts, k, "undo") || atw(ts, k, "compensate")) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  var bodies = ""
  var bcount = 0
  while (atk(ts, k, "=") && atk(ts, k + 1, "hostbody")) {
    let raw = tkc(ts, k + 1).text
    let bi = raw.indexOf("{")
    if (bi == 0 - 1) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
    let backend = raw.slice(0, bi)
    let body = raw.slice(bi + 1, raw.length())
    let seg = jstr(backend).concat(": ").concat(jstr(body))
    bodies = bcount == 0 ? seg : bodies.concat(", ").concat(seg)
    bcount += 1
    k = k + 2
  }
  if (bcount == 0) { return mk_irres(false, "") }
  var js = "{\"name\": ".concat(jstr(nm)).concat(", \"class\": ").concat(jstr(cls))
    .concat(", \"params\": [").concat(ir_params_json(ps.ps)).concat("], \"returns\": ").concat(retJson)
    .concat(", \"bodies\": {").concat(bodies).concat("}")
  // The RETURN-side half of the marking (the counterpart of a parameter's
  // `secret`). `returns` above is stripped, so these two flags are the only
  // surviving record that this crossing's result is confidential:
  // `secret_return` for the value anywhere in the declared return, and the
  // narrower `secret_witness` for the `Ok` arm a witnessed extern's durable
  // discharge descriptor records as its inverse's referent.
  if (taint_mentions_secret(retDecl)) { js = js.concat(", \"secret_return\": true") }
  if (taint_secret_witness(retDecl)) { js = js.concat(", \"secret_witness\": true") }
  if (isAsync) { js = js.concat(", \"async\": true") }
  return mk_irres(true, js.concat("}"))
}

// Collect every top-level `extern` into the `externs` list. A single extern
// outside the covered surface drops the whole section (ok=false), mirroring the
// component-body omission discipline.
fn externs_walk(ts: List[Token], i: Int, acc: Str, ok: Bool) -> ExAcc {
  if (i >= ts.length() || atk(ts, i, "eof")) { return { js: acc, ok: ok } }
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (at_boot(ts, i)) { return externs_walk(ts, i + 1, acc, ok) }
  if (t.kind != "kw") { return externs_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, ok) }
  if (t.text == "extern") {
    let ex = ir_extern(ts, i)
    let ni = p_extern(ts, i, empty_prog()).i
    let acc2 = ex.ok ? (acc == "" ? ex.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(ex.js)) : acc
    return externs_walk(ts, ni, acc2, ok && ex.ok)
  }
  if (t.text == "test") {
    if (atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { return externs_walk(ts, e, acc, ok) }
    }
    return externs_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, ok)
  }
  if (t.text == "fn") { return externs_walk(ts, p_fn(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc, ok) }
  if (t.text == "service") { return externs_walk(ts, p_service(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc, ok) }
  if (t.text == "component") { return externs_walk(ts, p_component(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc, ok) }
  return externs_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc, ok)
}

// ================================================================ types IR
//
// The `types` section: user record/variant declarations lowered to the
// reference's `_lower_type_decls` shape — `{Name: {params, kind, fields|cases}}`.
// Field/payload type spellings come straight from the shared type reader
// (`type_at`), which renders them exactly as the reference does (`List[Row]`,
// `Map[Int, Row]`, `(Int) -> Str`). Generic parameters and type aliases are not
// in the covered corpus; a bare-alias RHS is skipped rather than mis-lowered.

fn skip_brackets(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> Int {
  var depth = 0
  var j = i
  while (j < ts.length()) {
    if (atk(ts, j, "[")) { depth += 1 }
    else { if (atk(ts, j, "]")) { depth -= 1  if (depth == 0) { return j + 1 } } }
    j += 1
  }
  return i
}

fn ir_type_fields(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Str {
  var k = lo
  var out = ""
  var first = true
  while (k < hi && !atk(ts, k, "}")) {
    if (atk(ts, k, "ident") && atk(ts, k + 1, ":")) {
      let nm = tkc(ts, k).text
      let tr = type_at(ts, k + 2)
      let seg = jstr(nm).concat(":").concat(jstr(tr.ty))
      out = first ? seg : out.concat(",").concat(seg)
      first = false
      k = tr.i
      if (atk(ts, k, ",")) { k += 1 }
    } else { k += 1 }
  }
  return out
}

fn ir_type_cases(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Str {
  var k = lo
  var out = ""
  var first = true
  while (k < hi) {
    if (atk(ts, k, "ident")) {
      let nm = tkc(ts, k).text
      var payload = "null"
      var nk = k + 1
      if (atk(ts, k + 1, "(")) {
        let tr = type_at(ts, k + 2)
        payload = jstr(tr.ty)
        nk = tr.i
        if (atk(ts, nk, ")")) { nk += 1 }
      }
      let seg = "{\"name\":".concat(jstr(nm)).concat(",\"payload\":").concat(payload).concat("}")
      out = first ? seg : out.concat(",").concat(seg)
      first = false
      k = nk
    } else { k += 1 }
  }
  return out
}

// True iff a variant RHS (has a `|` alternation or a `(payload)`), as opposed to
// a bare type alias (`type Name = Other`), within [lo, hi).
fn is_variant_rhs(ts: List[Token], lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Bool {
  var k = lo
  while (k < hi) {
    if (atk(ts, k, "|") || atk(ts, k, "(")) { return true }
    k += 1
  }
  return false
}

fn ir_type_decl(ts: List[Token], i: Int) -> LirFn {
  let lineHi = line_end(ts, i, ts.length())
  let nm = tkc(ts, i + 1).text
  var j = i + 2
  if (atk(ts, j, "[")) { j = skip_brackets(ts, j) }
  if (!atk(ts, j, "=")) { return { js: "", i: lineHi } }
  j += 1
  if (atk(ts, j, "{")) {
    let bend = close_brace(ts, j)
    if (bend == 0 - 1) { return { js: "", i: lineHi } }
    let fields = ir_type_fields(ts, j + 1, bend - 1)
    // Reference declaration lowering treats an empty field table as a variant.
    if (fields == "") {
      return { js: jstr(nm).concat(": {\"params\": [], \"kind\": \"variant\", \"cases\": []}"), i: bend }
    }
    let js = jstr(nm).concat(": {\"params\": [], \"kind\": \"record\", \"fields\": {")
      .concat(fields).concat("}}")
    return { js: js, i: bend }
  }
  if (is_variant_rhs(ts, j, lineHi)) {
    let cases = ir_type_cases(ts, j, lineHi)
    let js = jstr(nm).concat(": {\"params\": [], \"kind\": \"variant\", \"cases\": [")
      .concat(cases).concat("]}")
    return { js: js, i: lineHi }
  }
  return { js: "", i: lineHi }
}

fn types_walk(ts: List[Token], i: Int, acc: Str) -> Str {
  if (i >= ts.length() || atk(ts, i, "eof")) { return acc }
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (at_boot(ts, i)) { return types_walk(ts, i + 1, acc) }
  if (t.kind != "kw") { return types_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc) }
  if (t.text == "type") {
    let d = ir_type_decl(ts, i)
    let acc2 = d.js == "" ? acc : (acc == "" ? d.js : acc.concat(", ").concat(d.js))
    return types_walk(ts, d.i, acc2)
  }
  if (t.text == "test") {
    if (atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { return types_walk(ts, e, acc) }
    }
    return types_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc)
  }
  if (t.text == "extern") { return types_walk(ts, p_extern(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc) }
  if (t.text == "fn") { return types_walk(ts, p_fn(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc) }
  if (t.text == "service") { return types_walk(ts, p_service(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc) }
  if (t.text == "component") { return types_walk(ts, p_component(ts, i, empty_prog()).i, acc) }
  return types_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), acc)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- top level

fn ir_walk(ts: List[Token], i: Int, a: IrAcc) -> IrAcc {
  if (i >= ts.length() || atk(ts, i, "eof")) { return a }
  let t = tkc(ts, i)
  if (at_boot(ts, i)) { return ir_walk(ts, i + 1, a) }
  if (t.kind != "kw") { return ir_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), a) }
  if (t.text == "use") { return ir_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), a) }
  if (t.text == "test") {
    let na = mk_iracc(a.svcs, a.comps, true, a.v2)
    if (atk(ts, i + 1, "{")) {
      let e = close_brace(ts, i + 1)
      if (e != 0 - 1) { return ir_walk(ts, e, na) }
    }
    return ir_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), na)
  }
  if (t.text == "type") {
    return ir_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), mk_iracc(a.svcs, a.comps, true, a.v2))
  }
  if (t.text == "extern") {
    return ir_walk(ts, p_extern(ts, i, empty_prog()).i,
                   mk_iracc(a.svcs, a.comps, true, a.v2))
  }
  if (t.text == "fn") {
    return ir_walk(ts, p_fn(ts, i, empty_prog()).i,
                   mk_iracc(a.svcs, a.comps, true, a.v2))
  }
  if (t.text == "service") {
    let sv = ir_service(ts, i)
    let svcs2 = a.svcs == "" ? sv.js : a.svcs.concat(", ").concat(sv.js)
    return ir_walk(ts, p_service(ts, i, empty_prog()).i,
                   mk_iracc(svcs2, a.comps, a.v3 || sv.v3, a.v2))
  }
  if (t.text == "component") {
    let cp = ir_component(ts, i)
    let comps2 = a.comps == "" ? cp.js : a.comps.concat(", ").concat(cp.js)
    return ir_walk(ts, p_component(ts, i, empty_prog()).i,
                   mk_iracc(a.svcs, comps2, a.v3 || cp.v3, a.v2 || cp.v2))
  }
  return ir_walk(ts, skip_line(ts, i), a)
}

// The IR producer (SEAM 1). Returns the covered surface of the interchange IR
// as canonical JSON: `{ir_version, services, components}`. Additive to, and
// independent of, `admit_src` — a caller admits FIRST, then lowers an admitted
// program to this IR for the emitter.
pub fn lower_to_ir(src: Str) -> Str {
  let ts = lex_src(src)
  let a = ir_walk(ts, 0, mk_iracc("", "", false, false))
  let ver = a.v3 ? "3" : (a.v2 ? "2" : "1")
  let fnsjs = fns_walk(ts, 0, "", case_binds(ts))
  let typesjs = types_walk(ts, 0, "")
  let exres = externs_walk(ts, 0, "", true)
  var out = "{\"ir_version\": ".concat(ver).concat(", \"services\": {")
    .concat(a.svcs).concat("}, \"components\": [").concat(a.comps).concat("]")
  if (typesjs != "") { out = out.concat(", \"types\": {").concat(typesjs).concat("}") }
  if (fnsjs != "") { out = out.concat(", \"functions\": [").concat(fnsjs).concat("]") }
  if (exres.ok && exres.js != "") { out = out.concat(", \"externs\": [").concat(exres.js).concat("]") }
  return out.concat("}")
}

// ================================================================ tests

test "lower_to_ir emits the services table for a simple provider" {
  assert lower_to_ir("service Cache { fn put(key: Str, value: Str) } component C provides cache: Cache { provide cache { fn put(key, value) { let k = key } } }")
    == "{\"ir_version\": 1, \"services\": {\"Cache\": {\"methods\": {\"put\": {\"params\": [{\"name\": \"key\", \"type\": \"Str\"}, {\"name\": \"value\", \"type\": \"Str\"}], \"returns\": null, \"emission\": false}}}}, \"components\": [{\"name\": \"C\", \"config\": [], \"requires\": {}, \"provides\": {\"cache\": \"Cache\"}, \"body\": [{\"step\": \"provide\", \"name\": \"cache\", \"service\": \"Cache\", \"methods\": [{\"name\": \"put\", \"params\": [\"key\", \"value\"], \"body\": [{\"step\": \"let\", \"name\": \"k\", \"value\": {\"kind\": \"name\", \"id\": \"key\"}, \"mutable\": false}]}]}]}]}"
}

test "lower_to_ir marks an emission op and a scoped capability" {
  assert lower_to_ir("service Db { emission[net] fn go(x: Str) -> Int }")
    == "{\"ir_version\": 1, \"services\": {\"Db\": {\"methods\": {\"go\": {\"params\": [{\"name\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Str\"}], \"returns\": \"Int\", \"emission\": true, \"capabilities\": [\"net\"]}}}}, \"components\": []}"
}

test "lower_to_ir lowers a simple effect/provide body" {
  assert lower_to_ir("service Store { fn put(k: Int, v: Int) } service Health { fn status() -> Str } component B requires store: Store provides health: Health { effect store.put(1, 10) undo store.put(1, 0) provide health { fn status() = \"ok\" } }")
    == "{\"ir_version\": 1, \"services\": {\"Store\": {\"methods\": {\"put\": {\"params\": [{\"name\": \"k\", \"type\": \"Int\"}, {\"name\": \"v\", \"type\": \"Int\"}], \"returns\": null, \"emission\": false}}}, \"Health\": {\"methods\": {\"status\": {\"params\": [], \"returns\": \"Str\", \"emission\": false}}}}, \"components\": [{\"name\": \"B\", \"config\": [], \"requires\": {\"store\": \"Store\"}, \"provides\": {\"health\": \"Health\"}, \"body\": [{\"step\": \"effect\", \"acquire\": {\"kind\": \"call\", \"target\": {\"kind\": \"req\", \"name\": \"store\"}, \"method\": \"put\", \"args\": [{\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": 1}, {\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": 10}]}, \"undo\": {\"kind\": \"call\", \"target\": {\"kind\": \"req\", \"name\": \"store\"}, \"method\": \"put\", \"args\": [{\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": 1}, {\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": 0}]}}, {\"step\": \"provide\", \"name\": \"health\", \"service\": \"Health\", \"methods\": [{\"name\": \"status\", \"params\": [], \"body\": [{\"step\": \"return\", \"expr\": {\"kind\": \"lit\", \"value\": \"ok\"}}]}]}]}]}"
}

test "lower_to_ir bumps ir_version to 3 on a module fn" {
  assert starts_with(lower_to_ir("fn id(x: Int) -> Int { return x }"), "{\"ir_version\": 3,")
}

test "lower_to_ir emits a module fn body (functions section)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("fn id(x: Int) -> Int { return x }")
    == "{\"ir_version\": 3, \"services\": {}, \"components\": [], \"functions\": [{\"name\":\"id\",\"params\":[{\"name\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Int\"}],\"returns\":\"Int\",\"public\":false,\"body\":[{\"step\":\"return\",\"expr\":{\"kind\":\"var\",\"name\":\"x\"}}]}]}"
}

test "lower_to_ir annotates typed arithmetic operands" {
  assert lower_to_ir("fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { return a + b }")
    == "{\"ir_version\": 3, \"services\": {}, \"components\": [], \"functions\": [{\"name\":\"add\",\"params\":[{\"name\": \"a\", \"type\": \"Int\"}, {\"name\": \"b\", \"type\": \"Int\"}],\"returns\":\"Int\",\"public\":false,\"body\":[{\"step\":\"return\",\"expr\":{\"kind\":\"bin\",\"op\":\"+\",\"left\":{\"kind\":\"var\",\"name\":\"a\"},\"right\":{\"kind\":\"var\",\"name\":\"b\"},\"operands\":\"Int\"}}]}]}"
}

test "lower_to_ir emits a record type declaration (types section)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("type P = { x: Int, y: Int }")
    == "{\"ir_version\": 3, \"services\": {}, \"components\": [], \"types\": {\"P\": {\"params\": [], \"kind\": \"record\", \"fields\": {\"x\":\"Int\",\"y\":\"Int\"}}}}"
}

test "lower_to_ir lowers a config read in a provide method (component spine)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("""service Conf { fn get() -> Str } component S provides conf: Conf { config { name: Str } provide conf { fn get() = config.name } }""")
    == """{"ir_version": 1, "services": {"Conf": {"methods": {"get": {"params": [], "returns": "Str", "emission": false}}}}, "components": [{"name": "S", "config": [{"name": "name", "type": "Str", "default": null}], "requires": {}, "provides": {"conf": "Conf"}, "body": [{"step": "provide", "name": "conf", "service": "Conf", "methods": [{"name": "get", "params": [], "body": [{"step": "return", "expr": {"kind": "config", "field": "name"}}]}]}]}]}"""
}

test "lower_to_ir lowers a typed provide-method arithmetic body (name/id, no operands)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("""service Calc { fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int } component A provides calc: Calc { provide calc { fn add(a, b) = a + b } }""")
    == """{"ir_version": 1, "services": {"Calc": {"methods": {"add": {"params": [{"name": "a", "type": "Int"}, {"name": "b", "type": "Int"}], "returns": "Int", "emission": false}}}}, "components": [{"name": "A", "config": [], "requires": {}, "provides": {"calc": "Calc"}, "body": [{"step": "provide", "name": "calc", "service": "Calc", "methods": [{"name": "add", "params": ["a", "b"], "body": [{"step": "return", "expr": {"kind": "bin", "op": "+", "left": {"kind": "name", "id": "a"}, "right": {"kind": "name", "id": "b"}}}]}]}]}]}"""
}

test "lower_to_ir lowers a timer step with interval_ms (component spine)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("""service Log { emission fn write(msg: Str) } component P requires log: Log { every 15s { emit log.write("beat") } }""")
    == """{"ir_version": 3, "services": {"Log": {"methods": {"write": {"params": [{"name": "msg", "type": "Str"}], "returns": null, "emission": true}}}}, "components": [{"name": "P", "config": [], "requires": {"log": "Log"}, "provides": {}, "body": [{"step": "timer", "mode": "every", "interval_ms": 15000, "body": [{"step": "emit", "expr": {"kind": "call", "target": {"kind": "req", "name": "log"}, "method": "write", "args": [{"kind": "lit", "value": "beat"}]}}]}]}]}"""
}

test "lower_to_ir emits an extern with a verbatim @py body (externs section)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("""extern pure fn dbl(n: Int) -> Int = @py { return n * 2 }""")
    == """{"ir_version": 3, "services": {}, "components": [], "externs": [{"name": "dbl", "class": "pure", "params": [{"name": "n", "type": "Int"}], "returns": "Int", "bodies": {"py": " return n * 2 "}}]}"""
}

test "lower_to_ir emits literal config-field defaults (config schema)" {
  assert lower_to_ir("""service G { fn greet(w: Str) -> Str } component H provides greeting: G { config { prefix: Str = "hi, ", count: Int = 1 } provide greeting { fn greet(w) = config.prefix } }""")
    == """{"ir_version": 1, "services": {"G": {"methods": {"greet": {"params": [{"name": "w", "type": "Str"}], "returns": "Str", "emission": false}}}}, "components": [{"name": "H", "config": [{"name": "prefix", "type": "Str", "default": "hi, "}, {"name": "count", "type": "Int", "default": 1}], "requires": {}, "provides": {"greeting": "G"}, "body": [{"step": "provide", "name": "greeting", "service": "G", "methods": [{"name": "greet", "params": ["w"], "body": [{"step": "return", "expr": {"kind": "config", "field": "prefix"}}]}]}]}]}"""
}

test "clean provider admits" {
  assert admit_src("service Cache { fn put(key: Str, value: Str) } component C provides cache: Cache { provide cache { fn put(key, value) { let k = key } } }") == ""
}

test "plain provider reaching an emission is refused (G4), exact wording" {
  let v = admit_src("service Database { emission fn execute(sql: Str) -> Int } service Cache { fn put(key: Str) } component C requires db: Database provides cache: Cache { provide cache { fn put(key) { emit db.execute(key) } } }")
  assert v == "G4|`Cache.put` is declared plain, but this implementation reaches `db.execute`"
}

test "unmarked emission is refused (G4)" {
  assert admit_src("""service Database { emission fn execute(sql: Str) -> Int } component P requires db: Database { effect db.execute("x") undo db.execute("y") }""") == "G4|call to emission `db.execute` must be marked `emit` (G4)"
}

test "admit_tag exposes the bare guarantee" {
  assert admit_tag("service D { fn q(s: Str) -> Int } component A provides db: D { provide db { fn q(s) { let x = s   return 0 } } } component B provides db: D { provide db { fn q(s) { let x = s   return 0 } } }") == "G2"
}

test "two providers of one key are refused (G2)" {
  let v = admit_src("service D { fn q(s: Str) -> Int } component A provides db: D { provide db { fn q(s) { let x = s   return 0 } } } component B provides db: D { provide db { fn q(s) { let x = s   return 0 } } }")
  assert v == "G2|provision conflict: key `db` is provided by both A and B (G2)"
}

test "sync provide method reaching an async extern is refused (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn http_post(url: Str, body: Str) -> Str = @py { return url } service Http { emission fn post(url: Str, body: Str) -> Str } component Poster provides http: Http { provide http { fn post(url, body) = http_post(url, body) } }")
  assert v == "A1|`Http.post` is declared sync, but this implementation reaches async extern `http_post` — a sync method has no in-flight window (A1)"
}

test "async-declared op admits the same async body" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn http_post(url: Str, body: Str) -> Str = @py { return url } service Http { emission async fn post(url: Str, body: Str) -> Str } component Poster provides http: Http { provide http { async fn post(url, body) = http_post(url, body) } }")
  assert v == ""
}

test "sync method implementing an async op is refused (signature parity)" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn http_post(url: Str, body: Str) -> Str = @py { return url } service Http { emission async fn post(url: Str, body: Str) -> Str } component Poster provides http: Http { provide http { fn post(url, body) = http_post(url, body) } }")
  assert v == "A1|method `post` of provision `http` is not async but service Http declares it async"
}

test "undeclared access is refused (G1), exact wording" {
  let v = admit_src("service Log { fn write(msg: Str) } component Logger provides log: Log { provide log { fn write(msg) { emit db.execute(msg) } } }")
  assert v == "G1|`db` is not a declared requirement of Logger"
}

test "a declared local receiver is not a G1 access" {
  let v = admit_src("service Cache { fn put(key: Str, value: Str) } component C provides cache: Cache { let store = effect Map.new() undo store.drop() provide cache { fn put(key, value) { effect store.insert(key, value) undo store.remove(key) } } }")
  assert v == ""
}

test "dependency cycle is refused (G3), exact path" {
  let v = admit_src("service A { fn ping(tag: Str) -> Str } service B { fn pong(tag: Str) -> Str } component Alpha requires b: B provides a: A { provide a { fn ping(tag) = b.pong(tag) } } component Beta requires a: A provides b: B { provide b { fn pong(tag) = a.ping(tag) } }")
  assert v == "G3|dependency cycle: Alpha -> Beta -> Alpha (G3)"
}

test "same key in the same realm conflicts (G2), realm named" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str } component StoreOne provides kv: Kv { isolate kv in realm("tenant_a") provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } } } component StoreTwo provides kv: Kv { isolate kv in realm("tenant_a") provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } } }""")
  assert v == "G2|provision conflict: key `kv` in realm `tenant_a` is provided by both StoreOne and StoreTwo (G2)"
}

test "same key in different realms composes (per-realm G2)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str } component StoreOne provides kv: Kv { isolate kv in realm("tenant_a") provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } } } component StoreTwo provides kv: Kv { isolate kv in realm("tenant_b") provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } } }""")
  assert v == ""
}

test "undeclared bare value in a method is refused (G1)" {
  let v = admit_src("service S { fn go() -> Int } component C provides s: S { provide s { fn go() { let x = nope   return 0 } } }")
  assert v == "G1|`nope` is not a declared requirement of C"
}

test "an arrow parameter resolves in its body (no false G1)" {
  assert admit_src("fn apply(f: (Int) -> Int, x: Int) -> Int { return f(x) } service S { fn go(n: Int) -> Int } component C provides s: S { provide s { fn go(n) { let r = apply(x => x, n)   return r } } }") == ""
}

test "async extern used as a value is refused (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 } service S { emission async fn go() -> Int } component C provides s: S { provide s { async fn go() { let f = tick   return 0 } } }")
  assert v == "A1|`S.go` uses async extern `tick` as a function value, but an async callable has no arrow type"
}

test "setup reaching an async extern is refused (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 } service S { fn go() -> Int } component C provides s: S { emit tick() provide s { fn go() { return 0 } } }")
  assert v == "A1|component `C` reaches async extern `tick` in a setup/activation body, which cannot suspend a fiber (A1)"
}

// A statement stream is split by line (checker.rvl's convention), so the
// prelude programs are written multi-line — one statement per line — exactly
// as a source file spells them. `admit_src` re-lexes the string, and the
// embedded newlines carry the line numbers the split reads.
test "isolate after an effect is refused (prelude rule)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service D { fn q(s: Str) -> Int }
service S { fn go() -> Int }
component C requires d: D provides s: S {
  let store = effect Map.new() undo store.drop()
  isolate d in realm("r1")
  provide s { fn go() { return 0 } }
}""")
  assert v == "PRELUDE|`isolate` must precede every effect, emit, await, and provide statement"
}

test "config before isolate composes (prelude ok)" {
  assert admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
component C requires kv: Kv {
  config { tenant: Str }
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  let v = effect kv.get("boot") undo kv.get("boot")
}""") == ""
}

// ---- the arrow-color leak trio + Async[T] coercion (item 171) -------------

test "sync-typed arrow reaching an async op leaks (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Model { emission async fn complete(msgs: Str) -> Str } service S { emission async fn go() -> Str } component C requires model: Model provides s: S { provide s { async fn go() { let f = msgs => emit model.complete(msgs)   return "x" } } }""")
  assert v == "A1|this arrow reaches an async operation, but its type carries no async color — the caller would receive an unawaited suspension (A1)"
}

test "an arrow in an Async[T] slot is coerced, not leaky (admits)" {
  assert admit_src("""extern emission async fn tick(n: Str) -> Str = @py { return n } fn apply(f: (Str) -> Async[Str], x: Str) -> Str { return f(x) } service S { emission async fn go() -> Str } component C provides s: S { provide s { async fn go() { let r = apply(msgs => tick(msgs), "x")   return r } } }""") == ""
}

test "the same arrow in a sync slot leaks (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""extern emission async fn tick(n: Str) -> Str = @py { return n } fn apply(f: (Str) -> Str, x: Str) -> Str { return f(x) } service S { emission async fn go() -> Str } component C provides s: S { provide s { async fn go() { let r = apply(msgs => tick(msgs), "x")   return r } } }""")
  assert v == "A1|this arrow reaches an async operation, but its type carries no async color — the caller would receive an unawaited suspension (A1)"
}

test "a module fn using an async callable as a value is refused (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 } fn holder() -> Int { let f = tick   return 0 } service S { fn go() -> Int } component C provides s: S { provide s { fn go() { return 0 } } }")
  assert v == "A1|function `holder` uses async callable `tick` as a function value, but an async callable has no arrow type"
}

test "a module fn's sync arrow reaching an async callable leaks (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 } fn holder(g: (Int) -> Int) -> Int { let h = x => tick()   return 0 } service S { fn go() -> Int } component C provides s: S { provide s { fn go() { return 0 } } }")
  assert v == "A1|this arrow reaches async callable `tick`, but its type carries no async color — the caller would receive an unawaited suspension (A1)"
}

test "a module fn's arrow into an Async[T] slot is coerced (admits)" {
  assert admit_src("extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 } fn apply_cb(cb: () -> Async[Int]) -> Int { return cb() } fn holder() -> Int { return apply_cb(() => tick()) } service S { fn go() -> Int } component C provides s: S { provide s { fn go() { return 0 } } }") == ""
}

// ---- slice 5b: the spawn/instance dimension -------------------------------

// Capability attenuation (item 66): an activation-body spawn may only grant a
// child capabilities its spawner HOLDS. Supervisor holds only `kv_a` but spawns
// Leaker, which reaches `kv_b` — widening, refused (G4).
test "a spawn that widens a child's capabilities is refused (G4)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service StoreA { emission[kv_a] fn wa(r: Str) -> Int }
service StoreB { emission[kv_b] fn wb(r: Str) -> Int }
service Task { emission fn go() -> Int }
component Leaker requires kv_b: StoreB provides task: Task {
  provide task { fn go() { emit kv_b.wb("x")  return 0 } }
}
component Supervisor requires kv_a: StoreA {
  let l = effect spawn Leaker with { } undo l.dispose()
}""")
  assert v == "G4|`Supervisor` spawns `Leaker`, granting it `kv_b`, but `Supervisor` holds only `kv_a` — a spawn may narrow a child's capabilities, never widen them"
}

// An emission reached through a spawn handle (`w.task.run`) must still be marked
// `emit` — reaching it through a handle instead of a `requires` key does not
// exempt it (G4, lower.py's `_instance_get_call`).
test "an unmarked emission through a spawn handle is refused (G4)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Net { emission[net] fn send(m: Str) -> Int }
service Task { emission[net] fn run(p: Str) -> Int  fn status() -> Int }
component Worker requires net: Net provides task: Task {
  provide task { fn run(p: Str) { emit net.send(p)  return 1 }  fn status() = 0 }
}
service Sup { emission fn go(p: Str) -> Int }
component Supervisor provides sup: Sup {
  provide sup { fn go(p: Str) { let w = effect spawn Worker with { } undo w.dispose()  let r = w.task.run(p)  return r } }
}""")
  assert v == "G4|call to emission `w.task.run` must be marked `emit` (G4)"
}

// G4/G6 spawn-emission bounds (decision 8): a PLAIN provide method may not spawn
// a target that emits — the emission cannot escape the (absent) bound by moving
// into a child.
test "a plain method spawning an emitting target is refused (G4)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Store { emission[kv] fn write(row: Str) -> Int }
service Task { emission[kv] fn go() -> Int }
service Sup { fn run() -> Int }
component Worker requires kv: Store provides task: Task {
  provide task { fn go() { emit kv.write("x")  return 0 } }
}
component Supervisor provides sup: Sup {
  provide sup { fn run() { let w = effect spawn Worker with { } undo w.dispose()  return 0 } }
}""")
  assert v == "G4|`Sup.run` is declared plain, but it spawns `Worker`, which emits through `kv`"
}

// An `emission[kv]`-scoped method spawning a target that emits outside its
// declared caps is refused, the caps named (G4).
test "a scoped method spawning a target that widens its caps is refused (G4)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Store { emission[kv] fn write(row: Str) -> Int }
service Task { emission[kv] fn go() -> Int }
service Sup { emission[other] fn run() -> Int }
component Worker requires kv: Store provides task: Task {
  provide task { fn go() { emit kv.write("x")  return 0 } }
}
component Supervisor requires other: Store provides sup: Sup {
  provide sup { fn run() { let w = effect spawn Worker with { } undo w.dispose()  return 0 } }
}""")
  assert v == "G4|`Sup.run` is declared `emission[other]`, but it spawns `Worker`, which emits through `kv`"
}

// The admitted twin: an `emission[kv]`-scoped method spawning a target that
// emits only `kv` is within bound — admitted. The spawn target `Worker` is a
// template, so its `task` provision never collides at G2 either.
test "a scoped method whose spawn target stays in bound admits" {
  assert admit_src("""service Store { emission[kv] fn write(row: Str) -> Int }
service Task { emission[kv] fn go() -> Int }
service Sup { emission[kv] fn run() -> Int }
component Worker requires kv: Store provides task: Task {
  provide task { fn go() { emit kv.write("x")  return 0 } }
}
component Supervisor requires kv: Store provides sup: Sup {
  provide sup { fn run() { let w = effect spawn Worker with { } undo w.dispose()  return 0 } }
}""") == ""
}

// A multi-tenant router that spawns one worker per tenant NARROWS each instance
// to its own store (`kv_a`/`kv_b` ⊆ {kv_a, kv_b}) — least authority per
// instance, admitted. Both workers provide `worker`, but as templates neither
// enters the static G2 table, so there is no provision conflict.
test "per-tenant spawn narrowing composes (attenuation admits)" {
  assert admit_src("""service Store { emission[kv_a] fn write_a(row: Str) -> Int }
service StoreB { emission[kv_b] fn write_b(row: Str) -> Int }
service Worker { emission fn tenant() -> Str }
component TenantAWorker requires kv_a: Store provides worker: Worker {
  provide worker { fn tenant() { emit kv_a.write_a("a")  return "a" } }
}
component TenantBWorker requires kv_b: StoreB provides worker: Worker {
  provide worker { fn tenant() { emit kv_b.write_b("b")  return "b" } }
}
component Router requires kv_a: Store requires kv_b: StoreB {
  let a = effect spawn TenantAWorker with { } undo a.dispose()
  let b = effect spawn TenantBWorker with { } undo b.dispose()
}""") == ""
}

// ---- slice 6 (final): coloring rule 2, code-less spawn forms, handoff, isolate

// async coloring rule 2 (item 92 §3): `caller` CALLS its async-typed parameter,
// so it is colored; a sync method reaching it is refused, naming both the
// colored fn and the async extern the arrow reaches, sorted.
test "rule-2 param-colored fn in a sync method is refused (A1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 }
fn caller(cb: () -> Async[Int]) -> Int { return cb() }
service S { emission fn go() -> Int }
component C provides s: S {
  provide s { fn go() { let r = caller(() => tick())   return 0 } }
}""")
  assert v == "A1|`S.go` is declared sync, but this implementation reaches async function `caller`, `tick` — a sync method has no in-flight window (A1)"
}

// the same rule-2-colored fn reached from an ASYNC method admits (it has an
// in-flight window) — the coercion/coloring half that stays clean.
test "async method reaching a rule-2-colored fn admits" {
  assert admit_src("""extern emission async fn tick() -> Int = @py { return 1 }
fn caller(cb: () -> Async[Int]) -> Int { return cb() }
service S { emission async fn go() -> Int }
component C provides s: S {
  provide s { async fn go() { let r = caller(() => tick())   return 0 } }
}""") == ""
}

// a spawn naming a component not in this composition (`_lower_spawn`).
test "spawn of an unknown component is refused (SPAWN)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Sup { fn run() -> Int }
component Supervisor provides sup: Sup {
  provide sup { fn run() { let w = effect spawn Nope with { } undo w.dispose()  return 0 } }
}""")
  assert v == "SPAWN|`spawn Nope` names an unknown component"
}

// an UNBOUND `effect spawn` — a spawn's teardown needs a handle to name it.
test "an unbound spawn is refused (bind-to-a-handle)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Task { fn go() -> Int }
component Worker provides task: Task { provide task { fn go() { return 0 } } }
component Supervisor requires t: Task {
  effect spawn Worker with { } undo dispose()
}""")
  assert v == "SPAWN|`spawn` must be bound to a handle: `let s = effect spawn Worker … undo s.dispose()`"
}

// item 53: a `handoff` targets a key the component PROVIDES; a required key has
// no state to hand off.
test "handoff on a non-provided key is refused (HANDOFF)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
component C requires kv: Kv {
  handoff kv: Str
  let v = effect kv.get("x") undo kv.get("x")
}""")
  assert v == "HANDOFF|`kv` is not a declared provision of C"
}

// item 53: at most one `handoff` per component.
test "two handoffs in one component are refused (HANDOFF)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
component C provides kv: Kv {
  handoff kv: Str
  handoff kv: Int
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}""")
  assert v == "HANDOFF|C declares more than one `handoff` — a component has one activation frame, so it hands off one state shape"
}

// a `handoff` on a provided key composes.
test "handoff on a provided key composes" {
  assert admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
component C provides kv: Kv {
  handoff kv: Str
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}""") == ""
}

// isolate target validation: an undeclared key is the declared-wiring refusal.
test "isolate on an undeclared key is refused (G1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
component C requires kv: Kv {
  isolate nope in realm("r1")
  let v = effect kv.get("x") undo kv.get("x")
}""")
  assert v == "G1|`nope` is not a declared requirement or provision of C"
}

// isolate uniqueness: a key is pinned at most once.
test "a key isolated twice is refused (G1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
component C requires kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  isolate kv in realm("r2")
  let v = effect kv.get("x") undo kv.get("x")
}""")
  assert v == "G1|key `kv` is isolated twice in C"
}

// ---- multi-realm routing validation (item 162) ----------------------------
//
// `isolate <key> in realms(...)` was parsed-but-unvalidated before this slice.
// The five component-level rules and the link-time per-realm provider check
// below are lower.py's `RouteStmt` branch and its `_link` route loop, in the
// reference's own order.

test "a multi-realm bind whose realms all have providers composes" {
  assert admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component StoreB provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r2")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate kv in realms("r1", "r2") strategy(round_robin)
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""") == ""
}

// the link-time dual of G2: every routed realm needs its own provider, else the
// route dangles at that leg.
test "a routed realm with no provider is refused (ROUTE)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate kv in realms("r1", "r9")
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""")
  assert v == "ROUTE|multi-realm bind of `kv` in Router names realm `r9`, but no component provides `kv` in realm `r9` (item 162: every routed realm needs a provider)"
}

// the strategy name is validated against the closed set, so a typo is a
// compile-time refusal and not a silent runtime fallback.
test "an unknown routing strategy is refused (ROUTE)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate kv in realms("r1") strategy(round_robbin)
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""")
  assert v == "ROUTE|unknown routing strategy `round_robbin` for `kv` in Router"
}

// routing distributes a CONSUMER's dependency; a provision has one installed
// instance in one realm (G2), so routing one is meaningless.
test "routing a provision is refused (ROUTE)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate api in realms("r1")
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""")
  assert v == "ROUTE|`isolate ... in realms(...)` routes a *required* key — `api` is a provision of Router"
}

// an undeclared route target is the shared declared-wiring (G1) diagnostic.
test "routing an undeclared key is refused (G1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate nope in realms("r1")
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""")
  assert v == "G1|`nope` is not a declared requirement of Router"
}

// issue #285: within one expression the FIRST refusal wins, the way the
// reference's does. `walk_expr` threads the accumulator through both halves of
// a `Bin`/`Index`/`If`, and `ac_refuse` overwrites unconditionally, so the
// right-hand refusal used to replace the left-hand one. On a source holding
// `st|ore` that spelled the subject `ore` — a name the program does not
// contain — and where the two halves carried different tags it also replaced
// the TAG, which is a consumer-visible contract break rather than a cosmetic
// one. Both operand orders are asserted; only one was ever wrong.
test "the left operand's refusal wins over the right one (G1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn put(key: Str, value: Str) }
component C provides kv: Kv {
  provide kv { fn put(key, value) { let n = alpha | beta.drop() } }
}""")
  assert v == "G1|`alpha` is not a declared requirement of C"
}

test "a later refusal does not overwrite an earlier refusal's tag (G4)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { emission fn put(key: Str) -> Int }
service Api { fn go() -> Int }
component C requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  provide api { fn go() { return kv.put("a") + beta } }
}""")
  assert v == "G4|call to emission `kv.put` must be marked `emit` (G4)"
}

test "the earlier refusal still wins when the operands are swapped (G1)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { emission fn put(key: Str) -> Int }
service Api { fn go() -> Int }
component C requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  provide api { fn go() { return beta + kv.put("a") } }
}""")
  assert v == "G1|`beta` is not a declared requirement of C"
}

// the clobber does not need two tags to be wrong: two unmarked emissions in
// one expression are both G4, and the SECOND one's message used to win.
test "two unmarked emissions in one expression name the first (G4)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Db { emission fn run(sql: Str) -> Int }
service Bus { emission fn send(m: Str) -> Int }
service Api { fn go() -> Int }
component C requires db: Db, bus: Bus provides api: Api {
  provide api { fn go() { return db.run("a") + bus.send("b") } }
}""")
  assert v == "G4|call to emission `db.run` must be marked `emit` (G4)"
}

// one binding per key: pinned to a single realm OR routed across a set.
test "a key both pinned and routed is refused (ROUTE)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  isolate kv in realms("r1")
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""")
  assert v == "ROUTE|key `kv` is already isolated to a single realm in Router — it cannot also be routed across `realms(...)`"
}

test "a key routed twice is refused (ROUTE)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  isolate kv in realms("r1")
  isolate kv in realms("r1")
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
}""")
  assert v == "ROUTE|key `kv` is routed twice in Router"
}

// the prelude rule, in the routing form's own wording.
test "a route after a provide block is refused (PRELUDE)" {
  let v = admit_src("""service Kv { fn get(k: Str) -> Str }
service Api { fn go(k: Str) -> Str }
component StoreA provides kv: Kv {
  isolate kv in realm("r1")
  provide kv { fn get(k) { return k } }
}
component Router requires kv: Kv provides api: Api {
  provide api { fn go(k) { return kv.get(k) } }
  isolate kv in realms("r1")
}""")
  assert v == "PRELUDE|`isolate ... in realms(...)` must precede every effect, emit, await, and provide statement"
}

// ---- the closed coloring approximation (item 186) -------------------------
//
// Callee collection and leak-reach now STOP at a nested COERCED arrow, exactly
// as the reference's `stop_async_arrows` does: an arrow in an `Async[T]` slot is
// a value whose suspension belongs to whoever awaits it. Both programs need an
// async-typed-but-UNCALLED parameter, so rule 2 does not color the callee and
// mask the difference.

test "a coerced arrow nested in a sync arrow does not leak (admits)" {
  assert admit_src("""extern emission async fn tick(n: Str) -> Str = @py { return n }
fn wrap(cb: (Str) -> Async[Str], y: Str) -> Str { return y }
fn plain(f: (Str) -> Str) -> Str { return f("a") }
service S { emission async fn go(y: Str) -> Str }
component C provides s: S {
  provide s { async fn go(y) { let r = plain(w => wrap(z => tick(z), w))   return r } }
}""") == ""
}

test "a fn whose only async reach is a coerced arrow stays sync (admits)" {
  assert admit_src("""extern emission async fn tick(n: Str) -> Str = @py { return n }
fn wrap(cb: (Str) -> Async[Str], y: Str) -> Str { return y }
fn h(y: Str) -> Str { return wrap(z => tick(z), y) }
service S { emission fn go(y: Str) -> Str }
component C provides s: S {
  provide s { fn go(y) { let r = emit h(y)   return r } }
}""") == ""
}

// ---------------------------------------------- item 384: foreign redirects
//
// Each `assert` below pins the reference's diagnostic TEXT byte-for-byte
// (src/revl/parser.py `_FOREIGN_STMT_KEYWORDS` / the per-form redirects, and
// src/revl/lower.py `_FOREIGN_NAME_REDIRECTS`). Before this port every one of
// these programs was ADMITTED by the gate — see examples/rejections/foreign_*.rvl,
// the reference fixtures these mirror one-for-one.
//
// The refusal side asserts on `foreign_scan` rather than `admit_src` ON PURPOSE.
// `admit_src` wraps the message in the NEW `FOREIGN` tag, and the differential
// oracle's reference classifier (tests/test_selfhost_lower.py `_classify`)
// does not know that family yet, so it renders these reference refusals
// `OUT:<message>` and an `admit_src` assertion here would be harvested by
// `test_in_file_test_programs_agree` and fail on the TAG while agreeing on
// every byte of the message. Asserting the message directly keeps this port
// pinned without pre-empting the corpus work (roadmap item 429). The
// ADMIT-side block below does go through `admit_src`, so the oracle harvests
// and cross-checks it against the reference — that is the half where a
// regression would be a false REFUSAL, and it is the half worth guarding.

test "foreign word operators redirect to the revl spelling" {
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(a: Bool, b: Bool) -> Bool { return a and b }")
    == "`and` is not a revl operator"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(a: Bool, b: Bool) -> Bool { return a or b }")
    == "`or` is not a revl operator"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(a: Bool) -> Bool { return not a }")
    == "`not` is not a revl operator"
}

test "foreign literals and declaration keywords redirect" {
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Bool { return True }")
    == "revl booleans are lowercase"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Bool { return False }")
    == "revl booleans are lowercase"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { const x = 1 return x }")
    == "revl has no `const`"
  assert foreign_scan("def foo() -> Int { return 1 }")
    == "revl has no `def`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { let g = lambda x: x return g(1) }")
    == "revl has no `lambda`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(n: Int) -> Int { if (n > 0) { return 1 } elif (n < 0) { return 2 } return 0 }")
    == "revl has no `elif`"
}

test "foreign builtins redirect to the revl surface" {
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(xs: List[Int]) -> Int { return len(xs) }")
    == "revl has no `len(...)`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(n: Int) -> Int { print(n) return n }")
    == "revl has no `print`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(n: Int) -> Int { throw n }")
    == "revl has no `throw`"
}

test "foreign statement and expression FORMS redirect" {
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { var i = 0 i++ return i }")
    == "revl has no `++` increment operator — expressions are pure (syntax-2.0 §3.3)"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { var i = 0 i-- return i }")
    == "revl has no `--` decrement operator — expressions are pure (syntax-2.0 §3.3)"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { var s = 0 for (let i = 0; i < 10; i += 1) { s += i } return s }")
    == "revl has no C-style `for (init; cond; step)` loop"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(xs: List[Int]) -> Int { var s = 0 for (x in xs) { s += x } return s }")
    == "revl iterates elements with `for (x of xs)`, not `for (x in xs)`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn g(k: Int) -> Int { return k } fn f() -> Int { return g(k=1) }")
    == "revl has no keyword arguments — `f(k=v)` is not a call"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(c: Bool) -> Int { let x = 1 if c else 2 return x }")
    == "revl has no Python-style `a if c else b` conditional expression"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f(xs: List[Int]) -> Int { let s = xs[0:2] return 0 }")
    == "revl has no slice syntax `xs[a:b]`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { let d = {\"k\": 1} return 0 }")
    == "revl records use identifier keys, not string keys like `{\"k\": v}`"
  assert foreign_scan("fn f() -> Int { let t = (1, 2) return 0 }")
    == "revl has no tuples — `(a, b)` is not a value"
  // the NEW tag `admit_src` wraps a redirect in. Bound to a `let` first so the
  // oracle's in-file program harvester does not pick it up: the reference
  // classifier has no FOREIGN family yet (roadmap item 429), so it would read
  // this reference refusal as `OUT:<message>` and fail on the tag alone.
  let thrower = "fn f(n: Int) -> Int { throw n }"
  assert admit_tag(thrower) == "FOREIGN"
  // ...and the position rules that keep it OFF malformed input. `@@@ not revl
  // @@@` is a parse failure ("expected a top-level declaration, found '@'"),
  // not a Python `not`: a redirect read out of a malformed adjacency would
  // replace an honest BAD with a confident wrong answer, and any consumer
  // matching the gate's BAD code would silently stop matching. (Bound to a
  // `let` for the same harvester reason as above.)
  let garbage = "@@@ not revl @@@"
  assert admit_tag(garbage) == "BAD"
}

test "a redirect never fires on a valid program (no false refusal)" {
  // the shapes each pattern must NOT mistake: a subtraction of a negation, a
  // real `for`-of, a typed arrow parameter list, an `else if` chain, a ternary
  // inside an index, a parameterised capability (item 294 — the one revl form
  // that spells `name=value` inside parens), and a name that SHADOWS a table
  // entry (as it does in the reference, where the resolver reaches the table
  // only after a scope miss).
  assert admit_src("fn f(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { return a - -b }") == ""
  assert admit_src("fn f(xs: List[Int]) -> Int { var s = 0 for (x of xs) { s += x } return s }") == ""
  assert admit_src("fn f() -> Int { let g = (a: Int, b: Int) => a + b return g(1, 2) }") == ""
  assert admit_src("fn f(n: Int) -> Int { if (n > 0) { return 1 } else if (n < 0) { return 2 } return 0 }") == ""
  assert admit_src("fn f(xs: List[Int], c: Bool) -> Int { return xs[c ? 0 : 1] }") == ""
  assert admit_src("fn f(n: Int) -> Int { let len = n return len }") == ""
  // (triple-quoted: the oracle's in-file program harvester reads a plain
  // string literal up to the next `"`, so an escaped quote would truncate it)
  assert admit_src("""service S { emission[fs.write(path="/tmp")] fn go(r: Str) -> Int }""") == ""
}

// ------------------------------------------ the declared `Secret[T]` marking

test "a qualifier-free type is returned verbatim" {
  // the byte-identity fast path: no parse/render round trip, so spacing and
  // spelling survive exactly for every program that uses no annotation.
  assert taint_strip("Str") == "Str"
  assert taint_strip("Map[Str, Int]") == "Map[Str, Int]"
  assert taint_strip("(Int, Str) -> Bool") == "(Int, Str) -> Bool"
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Str") == false
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Result[Str, Str]") == false
}

test "a qualifier is stripped wherever it is written" {
  assert taint_strip("Secret[Str]") == "Str"
  assert taint_strip("Trusted[Int]") == "Int"
  assert taint_strip("Untrusted[Str]") == "Str"
  assert taint_strip("Opt[Secret[Str]]") == "Opt[Str]"
  assert taint_strip("Result[Secret[Str], Str]") == "Result[Str, Str]"
  assert taint_strip("Map[Str, List[Secret[Int]]]") == "Map[Str, List[Int]]"
  assert taint_strip("(Secret[Str]) -> Int") == "(Str) -> Int"
  // idempotent
  assert taint_strip(taint_strip("Secret[Str]")) == "Str"
}

test "a qualifier head is never the tail of a longer identifier" {
  // a user type `MyTrusted[T]` is not a qualifier, so it round-trips verbatim.
  assert taint_strip("MyTrusted[Int]") == "MyTrusted[Int]"
  assert taint_strip("SecretBox[Str]") == "SecretBox[Str]"
  assert taint_mentions_secret("SecretBox[Str]") == false
}

test "the marking follows the VALUE through type constructors" {
  // the half only a recursive read gets right: a fallible lease spells its
  // payload `Result[Secret[Str], Str]`, and those bytes are exactly as
  // confidential as `Secret[Str]`'s.
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Secret[Str]")
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Opt[Secret[Str]]")
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Result[Secret[Str], Str]")
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Result[Str, Secret[Str]]")
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Map[Str, List[Secret[Int]]]")
}

test "a function type's parameter is not a container of the value" {
  // `(Secret[Str]) -> Int` says what a closure will be CALLED WITH, not what
  // the closure value holds; the disclosure happens at the call, and is caught
  // at its own crossing.
  assert taint_mentions_secret("(Secret[Str]) -> Int") == false
  assert taint_mentions_secret("Fn[Secret[Str], Int]") == false
  assert taint_mentions_secret("List[(Secret[Str]) -> Int]") == false
}

test "the witness position is the Ok arm alone" {
  assert taint_secret_witness("Result[Secret[Str], Str]")
  assert taint_secret_witness("Result[Str, Secret[Str]]") == false
  assert taint_secret_witness("Secret[Str]") == false
  assert taint_secret_witness("Opt[Secret[Str]]") == false
}

test "the stamps land in the IR the emitters read" {
  // both ends of the declared marking, on one program: the extern's declared
  // RETURN (the origin) and a service operation's declared PARAMETER (the
  // receiver). `returns`/`type` read the BARE type — the qualifier is gone —
  // and the flag is the only surviving record.
  let ir = lower_to_ir("""extern pure fn mint(u: Str) -> Secret[Str]
= @py { return u }
service V { fn take(t: Secret[Str]) -> Int  fn plain(n: Int) -> Int }""")
  assert ir.indexOf("\"returns\": \"Str\", \"bodies\"") != 0 - 1
  assert ir.indexOf("\"secret_return\": true") != 0 - 1
  assert ir.indexOf("\"name\": \"t\", \"type\": \"Str\", \"secret\": true") != 0 - 1
  assert ir.indexOf("\"name\": \"n\", \"type\": \"Int\"}") != 0 - 1
  assert ir.indexOf("Secret[") == 0 - 1
}
